4100:
** πιστ-εύω**,
_future_ -εύσω: _pluperfect_ πεπιστεύκειν {{NT|NT.Act.14.23}}: (πίστις):—**trust, put faith in, rely on** a person, thing, or statement, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.24; τῷ λόγῳ [prev. author] 2.118, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 886, etc.; π. θεῶν θεσφάτοισι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 800; τῇ τύχῃ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.112; σφίσιν αὐτοῖς [prev. author] 3.5; ταῖς ἀληθείαις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 44.3}}; [σημείοις] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.81; π. τινὶ περί τινος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1157a21; ὑπὲρ τῶν ὅλων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.43.2: with _neuter_}} _adjective_ or _pronoun_, λόγοις ἐμοῖσι πίστευσον τάδε **believe** my words herein, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 710; τοῦτ᾽.. Αἰγυπτίοις πιστεῦσαι δεῖ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 343b10; μὴ πάντα πειρῶ πᾶσι πιστεύειν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 335: later with Preps., π. ἐν τῷ Θεῷ, ἐν τῷ εὐαγγελίῳ, LXX.Psa.77(78).22, NT.Mark.1.15; π. εἰς τὸν Θεόν NT.John.14.1, +others}}; εἰς τὸ ὄνομά τινος{{NT|[prev. work] 1.12; π. ἐπὶ τὸν Κύριον NT.Act.9.42}}: _absolutely_, **believe**, περὶ μὲν τούτου.. οὔτε ἀπιστέω οὔτε ὦν π. τι λίην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.96}}; χαλεπὰ παντὶ ἑξῆς τεκμηρίῳ πιστεῦσαι although it is hard **to believe** every single bit of evidence about them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.20: with _accusative_ _cognate_, π. δόξαν}} **entertain a confident** opinion, {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.105}}:—_passive_, **to be trusted** or **believed**, ἄνδρες ἄξιοι πιστεύεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 181b, compare “Ep.” 309a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.8}}; πιστευθῆναι ὑπό τινος **enjoy** his **confidence**,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 6.1.39, compare “An.” 7.6.33; π. παρά τινι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.4, 58.44; πρός τινας [prev. author] 20.25}}; ὡς πιστευθησόμενος as if **he would be believed**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 27.54, compare 36.43; π. ὡς δημοτικὸς ὤν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1305a28}}; πιστεύονται [οἱ λόγοι] {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1172b6}}; ἐπιστεύοντο ἃ ἔλεγον they **were believed** in what they said, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 32.4}}; πρόγνωσιν ἐπεπίστευντο **were believed to possess** foreknowledge, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.2.4.}}
**2** **comply**, ὡς οὐχ ὑπείξων οὐδὲ πιστεύσων λέγει; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 625, compare 646}}; opposed to ἀπιστέω, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 1228.}}
**3** with _infinitive_, **believe that, feel confident that** a thing is, will be, has been, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 146; ἀληθῆ εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 524a, compare “R.” 450d; ὃς ἂν γνώμῃ πιστεύῃ τῶν ἐναντίων προὔχειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.62; προέσθαι τὴν προῖκ᾽ οὐκ ἐπίστευσεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 30.7}}; π. ὡς.., ὅτι.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 1.37, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 254a3, +others: the}}_infinitive_ is sometimes omitted, τὰ μὲν οὐ πιστεύουσιν οἱ νέοι (i.e. εἶναι or γεγονέναι) {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1142a19, compare “APr.” 68b13, “GA” 716a7}}:—_passive_, παρὰ Διὸς.. οἱ νόμοι πεπιστευμένοι ἦσαν γεγονέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 636d}}; πιστευθεὶς ἀληθεύσειν **believed sure to**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.25; ὁ ἥλιος.. πεπίστευται εἶναι μείζων τῆς οἰκουμένης 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 428b4; πρῶτοι νόμοις ἐγγράπτοις χρήσασθαι πεπιστευμένοι 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 6.1.8}}: without _infinitive_, πιστευθείσης εἱμαρμένης αἴρεται πᾶσα νουθεσία {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 33, compare 23.}}
**4** with _dative_ et _infinitive_, τοῖσι ἐπίστευε σιγᾶν to whom **he trusted** that they would keep silence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.110, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.55, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.54.}}
**5** **have faith,** {{NT|NT.Acts.2.44, 19.18, etc.}}
**II** π. τινί τι **entrust** something to another, τινὶ ἡγεμονίαν, χρήματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.17, “Smp.” 8.36; τὰν ὠνὰν τῷ θεῷ “GDI” 1684, +others (Delph.); γυναικὶ μὴ πίστευε τὸν.. βίον 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 86; also περὶ τῶν ἐμῶν τούτῳ ἀξιῶ πιστεύειν ὑμᾶς 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.7}}:—_middle_, **have entrusted to one**, ἀρχήν **Berichte der russ. Akad. fuür Gesch. der materiellen Kultur** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|4.82 (Olbia, 2nd-3rd c.AD):}} —_passive_, πιστεύεσθαί τι **to be entrusted with** a thing, **have** it **committed** to one, παρά or ὑπό τινος, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.69.1, 3rd c.BC: Phylarchus Historicus 24J., cf. Vett. Val. 65.3}}: with _infinitive_, πιστευθέντας τοῖς ἐχθροῖς διαφθείρειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287a39 (nisi to be read πεισθέντας)}}: with _genitive_, πιστευθεὶς τῆς Κύπρου {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.55.6, compare 6.56.13, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.15, etc.}}

4102:
** πίστις**, ἡ,
_genitive_ εως, _Ionic dialect_ ιος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.12, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 114}}; _dative_ πίστει, _Ionic dialect_ πίστῑ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.74, 9.106: _Ionic dialect_ _nominative_ and _accusative_ _plural_ πίστῑς variant in [prev. author] 3.8; _dative_ πίστισι [prev. author] 4.172}}: (πείθομαι):—**trust** in others, **faith**, first in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus, πίστιες καὶ ἀπιστίαι ὤλεσαν ἄνδρας “Op.” 372; πίστει χρήματ᾽ ὄλεσσα, ἀπιστίῃ δ᾽ ἐσάωσα 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 831; π. ἴσχειν τινί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 950; τῷ θεῷ πίστιν φέροις [prev. author] “OT” 1445, etc.}}: generally, **persuasion** of a thing, **confidence, assurance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.44 (πιστόν Scholia), etc.}}; ἡ βεβαιοτάτη π., ἀταραξία καὶ π. βέβαιος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.19, 2p.36U.}}; σωφροσύνης π. ἔχειν περί τινος to be **persuaded** of his probity, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.215; π. περὶ θεῶν ἔχειν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1101c.}}
**2** in subjective sense, **good faith, trustworthiness, honesty**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1137, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 443, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.105; θνῄσκει δὲ π., βλαστάνει δ᾽ ἀπιστία 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 611.}}
**2.b** of things, **credence, credit**, τὰν π. σμικρὰν παρ᾽ ἔμοιγ᾽ ἔχει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 737 (Lyric poetry); πίστιν τὰ τοιαῦτα ἔχει τινά 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1179a17; π. λαβεῖν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.35.4.}}
**2.c** καλῇ π., = Latin _bona fide,_ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PGnom. 180 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; αἱ κατὰ πίστιν γεινόμεναι κληρονομίαι, = Latin _hereditates fideicommissariae_, {{Refs|[prev. work]56.}}
**3** in a commercial sense, **credit**, π. τοσούτων χρημάτων ἐστί τινι παρά τισι he has **credit** for so much money with them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.57}}, compare 44; εἰς πίστιν διδόναι [τί τινι] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 32.16; εἰ ἕξω ἐλπίδα πίστεως 2nd c.AD: Astrampsychus Onirocriticus “Oracula” 68p.6H.}}
**3.b** **position of trust** or **trusteeship**, ἐν πίστει κληρονόμος ἀπολειφθείς left **in trust**, as guardian, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 41, compare 2c above; ἐν πίστει ὤν τῷ βασιλεῖ “IG” 22.646.11.}}
**4** Theol., **faith**, opposed to sight and knowledge, {{NT|NT.1Cor.13.13, etc.}}
**II** **that which gives confidence**: hence,
**II.1** **assurance, pledge of good faith, guarantee**, οὐκ ἀνδρὸς ὅρκοι π. ἀλλ᾽ ὅρκων ἀνήρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 394, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 887, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1055; ὅρκοις καὶ πίστεσιν ἀναγκάξειν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.25: distinguished from ὅρκοι and δεξιαί, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1375a10, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 22; ἔμβαλλε χειρὸς πίστιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 813}}; δός μοι χερὸς σῆς π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OC” 1632; ὅρκους παρασχών, πίστιν οὐ σμικράν, θεῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1037, compare “Med.” 414 (Lyric poetry)}}; πίστιν καὶ ὅρκια ποιέεσθαι make a treaty by exchange of **assurances** and oaths, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.92, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.107; οἷσιν.. οὔτε π. ὄθ᾽ ὅρκος μένει 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 308}}; ποιέεσθαι τὰς πίστῑς (_Ionic dialect_ for πίστεις) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.8; πίστεις ποιήσασθαι πρός τινας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.51; ἀλλήλοις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.3.12}}; πίστιν δοῦναι to give **assurances**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.91, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.86, 5.45; ὅρκους καὶ πίστιν ἀλλήλοις δότε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1185}}; ἔδοσαν πίστιν καὶ ἔλαβον interchanged **them**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.44; πίστεις ἀλλήλοιν δεδωκέναι τε καὶ δεδέχθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 256d; π. παρά τινος λαβεῖν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.9}}; π. πρός τινας δοῦναι with _infinitive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] 19.32}}; πίστι τε λαβεῖν (or καταλαβεῖν) καὶ ὁρκίοισί τινα bind by **assurances** and oaths, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.74, 9.106; θεῶν πίστεις ὀμόσαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.30}}; πίστιν ἐπιθεῖναι or προσθεῖναι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.26, 49.42, 54.42: with _genitive_ objecti, φόβων π}}. **an assurance against**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 627 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.2** **means of persuasion, argument, proof**, φρὴν παρ᾽ ἡμέων (i.e. τῶν αἰσθήσεων) λαβοῦσα τὰς πίστεις {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 125; τοὺς δεομένους πίστεως αἰσθήσει κεκραμένης 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.7.15}}; especially of proofs used by orators, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.84, 6.28, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 70b, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.8, etc.: in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}}, opposed to a demonstrative proof (ἀπόδειξις), π. ἔντεχνοι, ἄτεχνοι, {{Refs|“Rh.” 1355b35, 1375a22: also, generally, π. ἐκ τῆς ἐπαγωγῆς “APo.” 90b14, +others; π. ἡ διὰ συλλογισμοῦ “Top.” 103b7}}; ἡ τῶν λόγων π. (compare λόγος{{Refs|IV. 1) “Pol.” 1326a29; ὁ ἀναιρῶν ταύτην τὴν π. οὐ πολὺ πιστότερα ἐρεῖ “EN” 1173a1.}}
**III** **that which is entrusted, a trust**, πίστιν ἐγχειρίζειν τινί {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.41.2, compare 16.22.2, “IG” 7.21.12 (Megara, 2nd c.BC), 5 (1).26.6 (Amyclae, 2nd-1st c.BC), “BMus.Inscription” 422.7 (Priene, 2nd c.BC)}}; σὴ π. **given in trust** to thee, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“IG” 14.2012 A 23 (1st c.AD: Sulpicius Maximus Epicus).}}
**IV** political **protection** or **suzerainty**, Latin _fides_, Αἰτωλοὶ.. δόντες αὑτοὺς εἰς τὴν Ῥωμαίων π... τῷ τῆς π. ὀνόματι πλανηθέντες {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 20.9.10, compare 3.30.1; πάντες εἰς τὴν [τῆς συγκλήτου] π. ἐνδεδεμένοι [prev. author] 6.17.8.}}
**IV.2** in Egypt, **safe-conduct, safeguard**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 119.32 (_plural_, 2nd c.BC); δοῦναί μοι ἔγγραπτον π. [prev. work] 124.30 (2nd c.BC).}}
**V** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus}} name for **ten,** {{Refs|Theol.Ar. 59, 60.}}
**VI** personified, = Latin {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Fides”, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Numa” 16, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.16, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 45.17}}; π. δημοσία, = **Fides publica**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.75.}}

4103:
** πιστός**, ή, όν,
(πείθω):
**B** _passive_, **to be trusted** or **believed**:
**B.I** of persons, **faithful, trusty**, ἑταῖρος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.331, etc.; φύλακες 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 735; μάρτυρες 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.88; Ζηνὶ π. ἄγγελος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 969, etc.}}: _comparative_ -ότερος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.108, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.38}}: _superlative_ πιστότατος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 27: with _dative_, - ότατος δέ οἱ ἔσκε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.147; ὁ π. ἡμῖν κἀγαθὸς καλούμενος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 541, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 153 (_anapaest meter_), etc.: with _genitive_, τοῦ Φαλάνθου πιστόν τινα}} a **trusted friend** of P., Ergias cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.36o f; π. πρὸς τὰ συμβόλαια 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1283a33}}; οἱ πιστοί, in Persia, **trusty councillors**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.15, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.108 (_superlative_)}}; τάδε Περσῶν πιστὰ καλεῖται [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 2 (_anapaest meter_); so πιστὰ πιστῶν, ={πιστότατοι}, [prev. work] 681, compare 528, 979.}}]
**B.I.2** **trustworthy, worthy of credit**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.5 (_comparative_), 5.3, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.43}}. _adverb_, - τῶς καὶ ἀδόλως {{Refs|“IG” 12.90.14,17.}}
**B.I.3** **genuine**, π. Ἀταλάντης γόνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1322}}; Θηρικλέους π. τέκνον, of a cup, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Theopompus Comicus 32.1}}; **unmistakable**, νόσοι πονηραὶ καὶ π. {{LXX|LXX.Deu.28.59.}}
**B.II** of things, **trustworthy, sure**, ὅρκια π. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.269, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 11(10).6, etc.}}; τέκμαρ τῶνδε, τεκμήρια, μαντεῖα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 272, 352, “Th.” 66; τοῖσι Ἕλλησι ὡς πιστὰ δὴ τὰ λεγόμενα ἦν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.83; ἔσται πιστὰ καὶ ἄδολα καὶ ἁπλᾶ ἅπαντα τὰ ἀπ᾽ Ἀθηναίων Ῥηγίνοις “IG” 12.51.11}}; οὐκέτι πιστὰ γυναιξίν no longer **can one trust** women, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.456; βροτῶν δὲ π. οὐδέν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 667.3}}; οὐκ ἔχοντες τὴν ἐλπίδα.. πιστὴν ἔτι no longer having such hope **as could be relied on**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.14}}; ὑπόληψις ἡ πιστοτάτη, of knowledge, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 131a23.}}
**B.II.2** **deserving belief, credible**, π. καὶ οἰκότα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.82, compare 8.80; π. ὑπόθεσις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 107b; τοῦτο π. ἐκ τῆς ἐπαγωγῆς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 276a14}}; πιθανὸν καὶ π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1356b28; [λόγος] ἀποδεικτικὸς καὶ π. [prev. work] 1377b23.}}
**B.III** πιστόν, τό, as _substantive_, **pledge, security, warrant**, τὸ π. τῆς ἀληθείας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 398, etc.; τὸ π. τῆς ἐπιστήμης 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.72 (but τὸ π. τῆς καθ᾽ ὑμᾶς πολιτείας}} its **honesty**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.68)}}; τὸ π. ἔχοντες.. κἂν περιγενέσθαι feeling **confidence** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.141: frequently in _plural_, τὰ πιστὰ ποιέεσθαι,πίστιν ποιεῖσθαι, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.8}}; πιστὰ θεῶν, of oaths, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.7}}; ἐδώκαμεν καὶ ἐλάβομεν πιστά we gave and received **pledges**, with _future_ _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “An.” 3.2.5, compare 4.8.7, etc.; πιστὰ ἠξίου γενέσθαι [prev. author] “Cyr.” 7.4.3; τὰ πίστ᾽ ἐδειξάτην 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 651; στέργειν τὰ π. τῶνδε [prev. author] “Eu.” 673; τὰ π. ἐμαυτῷ τοῦ θράσους παρέξομαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 268.}}
**B** _active_, **believing, relying on**, τινι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 283, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 917, “Pers.” 55 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1031}}; **trustful**, τῆς ἐλευθερίας τὸ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.40, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 824; τινὶ τὸ π. νέμειν 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 3.39.}}
**B.2** **obedient, loyal**, τὴν τῶν Ἀθηναίων χώραν οἰκείαν καὶ π. ποιήσασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.30.}}
**B.3** **faithful, believing,** {{NT|NT.Acts.16.1, “IG” 3.3435.}}
**C** _adverb_ πιστῶς **with good faith**, μὴ π. καταμαρτυρηθείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.7}}; **loyally**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.26}}: _comparative_ -οτέρως {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 22.17.}}
**C.2** **persuasively**, in _comparative_, πιστότερον ἢ ἀληθέστερον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.4}}; **credibly, demonstrably**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epinomis” 983e}}; **unmistakably**, κριθῆναι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 9.857.}}
**C.3** **with disposition to believe**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.49}}: _comparative_, πιστότερον πρὸς ἐκείνους ἢ πρὸς ὑμᾶς αὐτοὺς διακείμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 18.15.}}

4104:
** πιστόω**,
**make trustworthy**, πιστοῦν τινα ὅρκοις **bind** him by oaths, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.88.}}
**II** _passive_, **to be made trustworthy, give a pledge** or **warrant**, πιστωθείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 536; ἐπεὶ δ᾽ ἐπιστώθησαν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 66}}; μοι.. ὅρκῳ πιστωθῆναι ἀπήμονά μ᾽ οἴκαδ᾽ ἀπάξειν **bind yourselves** to me by oath.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.436.}}
**II.2** **feel trust** or **confidence, to be persuaded**, ὄφρα.. πιστωθῆτον ἐνὶ θυμῷ {{Refs|21.218}}; πιστωθεὶς ὅτι.. **feeling confidence** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1039.}}
**III** _middle_, **give mutual pledges of fidelity, exchange troth**, χεῖράς τ᾽ ἀλλήλων λαβέτην καὶ πιστώσαντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.233; ἐπιστώσαντ᾽ ἐπέεσσιν 21.286; π. περὶ τῶν ὅλων πρὸς ἀλλήλους 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.39.6.}}
**III.2** πιστοῦσθαί τινα ὑφ᾽ ὅρκου **secure** his **good faith** by oaths, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 650, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.15.2.}}
**III.3** **confirm, prove**, τῷ παρόντι τοὐπιόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1073.6, compare 6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus “Philoponi (Michaelis Ephesii) in libros de Generatione Animalium commentaria - pars” 206.25}}; **guarantee**, τι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.43.5, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philops.” 5, 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 13.407}}; τι διότι.. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.122S.; ἀπὸ τούτων ἕκαστα 3rd c.BC: Polystratus Epicureus “Herc.” 346p.84V.; [τι] ταῖς Αἰσχύλου ἐλεγείαις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.628e}}; μάρτυρι τῷ Νέρωνι, ὅτι.. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Galb.” 5; ἔργοις τὰς ὑποσχέσεις 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hipp.” 1; πείρῃ τὸ πρῆγμα 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.7; φιλοσοφίαν σώφρονι βίῳ 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.2.4; ἐκ τῆς ἀποβάσεως τὴν φήμην [prev. author] 1.14.6, compare 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 11.77}}; τίς ἂν τάδε πιστώσαιτο,.. ὅτι..; {{LXX+4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 3.355: _absolutely_, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 133, LXX.3Ki.1.36.}}

4105:
** πλᾰν-άω**,
_future_ -ήσω {{LXX|LXX.4Ki.4.28, etc.}}:—_passive_ and _middle_, _future_ -ήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Mi.” 376c, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Peregr.” 16, -ηθήσομαι 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 9, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 2.27}}: _aorist_ ἐπλανήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 598, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.4, etc.}}: _perfect_ πεπλάνημαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 565 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.16.β᾽, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 264c, etc.}}: (πλάνη):—Prose verb, ={πλάζω} (used once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, also by Trag., 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus (see. below), and 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 10.15)}}, **cause to wander**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 572 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.128.}}
**2** **lead from the subject**, in talking, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.335.}}
**3** **lead astray, mislead, deceive**, ἢ γνώμη πλαν; {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 316, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356d, “Lg.” 655c, Theognetus Comicus 2.2, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 79; τὸν ὄχλον NT.John.7.12; τὸ ἀόριστον πλανᾷ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1415a14; τὰ μὴ πλανῶντα [prev. author] “Mete.” 347b35}}; πλανῶν τὴν ἔξοδον, of the Labyrinth, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 3.1.4.}}
**II** _passive_, **wander, stray**, ἵπποι πλανόωνται ἀνὰ δρόμον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.321; ὅποι γῆς.. πεπλάνημαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 565 (_anapaest meter_); π. εἰς πόλεις 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.97; κατὰ τὴν χώραν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.76; περὶ τὰ πεδία 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 264c: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 347, etc.}}; of the **planets**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 822a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 346a2, etc.: _metaphorically_, νοῦς ἐν αὑτῷ ὁ ἀληθινὸς πέφυκε πλανᾶσθαι 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.7.13}}; of reports, **travel abroad**, πολλὰ.. ἐμπόρων ἔπη φιλεῖ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 304.}}
**II.b** c._accusative_ loci, πλανηθεὶς τήνδε βάρβαρον χθόνα **having wandered over** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 598; πᾶσαν γῆν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 34: c._accusative_ _cognate_, πολλοὺς ἑλιγμοὺς πλανώμενοι}} **wandering about** as in a labyrinth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.4.}}
**II.2** **wander** in speaking, π. ἐν τῷ λόγῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.115}}; **digress**, π. ἀπὸ τοῦ λόγου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 263a.}}
**II.3** with _genitive_, πλαναθεὶς καιροῦ **having missed** the right moment, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.4.}}
**II.4** **do** a thing **irregularly** or **with variation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.52}}; ἐνύπνια τὰ ἐς ἀνθρώπους πεπλανημένα the **varying** dreams **that visit** them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.16. β; πλανωμένη πρὸς ἄλλοτ᾽ ἄλλον πημονὴ προσιζάνει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 277}}; πεπλανημένον τρόπον **irregularly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 24}}; **to be** **unsettled**, τὰ τῆς ἐλευθερίας ἔτι πλανώμενα καταστήματα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“IG” 42(1).81.13 (Epid., 1st c.AD).}}
**II.5** **to be in doubt** or **at a loss**, π. τὸ θέλει τὸ ἔπος εἶπαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.37: more frequently _absolutely_, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 473, etc.; π. καὶ ἀπορῶ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp. Ma.” 304c; ἡ ψυχὴ π. καὶ ταράττεται [prev. author] “Phd.” 79c}}; π. τῇ διανοίᾳ, ταῖς διανοίαις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.52, “Ep.” 6.10; πεπλανημένην ἔχειν τὴν διάνοιαν [prev. author] 15.265; πλανωμένων θεραπεία παθῶν 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 27.}}
**II.6** in forensic Rhet., χρώματα πεπλανημένα, μετάθεσις πεπ., of **alternative** pleas, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ τῶν στάσεων” 3.}}
**II.7** **to be misled**, ὑπὸ φωνῆς κοινότητος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 7; ταῖς ὁμωνυμίαις [prev. work] 36.}}

4106:
** πλᾰν-η**, ἡ,
**wandering, roaming**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30, 2.103, 116: frequently in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Pr.”, in _sing._, 622, 784, +others: in _plural_, τηλέπλανοι, πολύπλανοι πλάναι, 576 (Lyric poetry), 585 (Lyric poetry),compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 873 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** **discursive treatment**, ἡ διὰ παντὸς διέξοδος καὶ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 136e; ἡ π. τοῦ λόγου [prev. author] “Lg.” 683a.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **going astray**, βίοτος ἀνθρώπων π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 659.8; π. καὶ ἄνοια 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 81a; πλάνης ἔμπλεῳ [prev. author] “R.” 505c}}; ἡ περὶ τὰ χρώματα π. τῆς ὄψεως the **illusion**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 602c}}; πολλὴν ἔχει.. πλάνην **irregularity**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1094b16}}; πολλὰς ἀπορίας ἔχει καὶ π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “de An.” 402a21}}; ἡ κατὰ τὰς αἰσθήσεις π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 28 “fragment” 7; π. καὶ παραλογισμός 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.30S., compare 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 33.}}
**II.2** **deceit, imposture**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Pro.14.8, NT.Matt.27.64.}}

4107:
** πλᾰν-ήτης**, ου,
_Doric dialect_ **πλανάτας**, ὁ, ={πλάνης} 1.1, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 3, 124 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 148 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; τοὺς π. ἐπὶ τὰς πόλεις ἐμπόρους [καλοῦμεν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 371d}}; πλανῆται ἐπὶ πάντας τόπους, of hares, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.17.}}
**II** as _adjective_, π. ἄθλιος βίος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 878, compare 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Marcellam - Porphyrii Opuscula” 22.}}
**II.2** in Medicine texts, ={πλάνης}{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|1.3, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.18.}}

4108:
** πλάνος** [ᾰ], ον,
**1** _active_, **leading astray, deceiving**, π. κατέσειον ἐδωδάν the bait, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.43, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 7.702 (1st c.AD: Apollonides Epigrammaticus)}}; π. δῶρα, ἄγρα, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 1.29, “fragment” 1.10; πνεύματα NT.1Tim.4.1.}}
**2** _passive_, **wandering, roaming, fickle**, ποικίλον πρᾶγμ᾽ ἐστὶ καὶ πλάνον τύχη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Kith.fragment” 8}}; π. φέγγη **planets**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 4.3.}}
**II** _substantive_ πλάνος, ὁ, ={πλάνη}, **wandering, roaming**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1114, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 482, etc.: in _plural_, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 873 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}
**II.b** κερκίδος πλάνοι, of the act of weaving, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1491 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, φροντίδος πλάνοι **wanderings** of thought, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 67}}; π. φρενῶν **wandering** of mind, madness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 283; π. τε καρδίᾳ προσίσταται [prev. author] “fragment” 1038}}; πλάνοις **in uncertain fits**, of a disease, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 758}}; ={πλάνη}{{Refs 5th c.BC+|11.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 79d.}}
**II.3** **digression**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ep.” 344d.}}
**II.4** **error**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Cebes Philosophus 25, 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 26}}; **grammatical mistake**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 84.11.}}
**III** of persons, πλάνος, ὁ, **vagabond,impostor**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Nicostratus Comicus 24, 4th c.BC: Dionysius Comicus 4, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 34 / 5.2.14, NT.Matt.27.63.}}

4109:
** πλάξ**, πλᾰκός, ἡ,
**anything flat and broad**, especially **flat land, plain**, πᾶσαν ἠπείρου πλάκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 718}}; Φλεγραίαν π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eu.” 295}}; νυχίαν π., of Psyttaleia (perhaps μυχίαν), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 953 (Lyric poetry); πλακὸς ὑλίας “Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.7 (_Locrian dialect_, 5th c.BC)}}; νεκύων πλάκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1564 (Lyric poetry); νεκρῶν πλάκες [prev. work] 1577 (Lyric poetry)}}; also of sea and sky, πόντου πλάξ the ocean-**plain**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.24; ἄλοκα Νηρεΐας πλακὸς τέμνοντες 6th c.BC: Arion Lyricus 1.15}}; ποντία, πελαγία π., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 578.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1438; κατ᾽ Αἰγαίην πόντου πλάκα “BMus.Inscription” 1012 (Chalcedon, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD)}}; αἰθερία πλάξ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1349 (_anapaest meter_)}}; **flat top of a hill, table-land**, Σουνίου, Οἴτης π., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1220, “Ph.” 1430; Παρνασοῦ πλάκες 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1267}}; ἀπ᾽ ἄκρας πυργώδους πλακός from the **flat top** of the towering hill, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 273; τὰς π. τοῦ ὄρους 2nd c.AD(?): Antoninus Liberalis Mythographus 4.1.}}
**2** **flat stone, tablet**, ἐργώνας τᾶν πλακῶν τᾶς τομᾶς εἰς τὸν ὀχετόν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).109 iii 154 (Epid., 3rd c.BC)}}; π. ἐπιγεγραμμέναι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 672.12 (Egypt, 1st c.AD),compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Somn.” 3, etc.}}; of the {{LXX+NT+2nd c.AD+|“Tables” of the Jewish Law, αἱ π. τοῦ μαρτυρίου, τῆς διαθήκης, LXX.Exo.31.18, NT.Heb.9.4; λίθων πλαξὶ λείαις 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 12; οὐκ ἐν πλαξὶν λιθίναις ἀλλ᾽ ἐν π. καρδίας NT.2Cor.3.3}}; **tombstone,** {{Refs|Anthologia Graeca 7.324, compare “IG” 12(5).329 (_plural_, Paros)}}: _plural_, **slabs** of marble, Chor.p.89 {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus, compare [same work] in “Rev.Phil.” 1877.79}}; ὥσπερ μαρμάρου π., of ice, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Misopogon” 341b.}}
**2.b** πλάκες χρυσίου gold **plates**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 4.2.1; σαπφείροιο 2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 1105}}; ἡ ἐντὸς π. τῶν κογχυλίων the inner **surface**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 73.}}
**2.c** ἡ π. τοῦ βαλανίου τούτου probably part of the furnace, {{Refs|“PMag.Osl.” 1.340}}. d. _plural_, **flakes** of ἀρσενικὸν τὸ πλακῶδες, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.104.}}
**3** in _plural_, **flaps, tail-fins** of crustacea, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 526b9, “GA” 758a14.}}
**3.b** **folds** within certain parts of the ruminant stomach, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 507b11, “PA” 675a28.}}
**4** κοπτῆς πλάκες,πλακοῦντες, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 12.212 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus)}}. (Cf. Lett.**plakt** 'become flat'.)

4110:
** πλάσμα**, ατος, τό,
**anything formed** or **moulded, image, figure**, πλάσματα πηλοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 686}}; κήρινον.. οὐκ οἶδ᾽ ὅ τι π. as it were a **piece of** wax-**work**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 197d, compare 200c, “Sph.” 239e}}; of figures made by bakers, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 113}}: _plural_, **cakes** of incense, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 2144.29 (3rd c.AD).}}
**b** the **body**, as fashioned by the Creator, {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.212.}}
**II** **counterfeit, forgery**, π. ὅλον ἐστὶν ἡ διαθήκη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.29.}}
**II.b** **figment, fiction**, πλάσματα τῶν προτέρων {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 1.22, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 289a6, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.2.36, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 1.1.2, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 28, etc.}}; of a **story** which is **fictitious but possible**, opposed to ἱστορία {{Refs 1st c.AD+|11 and μῦθος 11.3, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.263, 4th c.AD: Ausonius Poeta Latinus “Prof.” 21.26, compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.528.}}
**II.c** **pretence**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ κακιῶν ί” p.38 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 43.}}
**II.2** **delusion**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.56O.}}
**III** **formed style** in writing or speaking, π. καὶ τὴν ἄλλην κατασκευὴν δημηγόρου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1.199 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; opposed to τρόπος, σχῆμα, [prev. work]164S.; π. ἱστορικόν, opposed to ὑπαγωγικόν, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Epistula ad Pompeium” 4}}; ἡνίκ᾽ ἂν ᾖ ποιητικὸν τοῦ λόγου τὸ π. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 15.8.}}
**III.2** in music, **affected execution**, with trills, falsetto, etc., μετὰ πλάσματος αὐλεῖν, opposed to ἀπλάστως, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.11.5}}; of studied **intonation** in orators or actors, π. φωνῆς ἀθόρυβον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 5, compare 2.405d}}; **lectio plasmate effeminata**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Quintilianus Rhetor Latinus “Inst.” 1.8.2; ἀναγνώσεις μετὰ πλάσματος 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.49.}}

4111:
** πλάσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ **πλάττω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 148 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 420c, etc.:}}_future_ πλάσω (ἀνα-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Mochl.” 2}}:_aorist_ ἔπλᾰσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.70 (κατ-), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 926, etc.; poetry ἔπλασσα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.109}}; _Epic dialect_ πλάσσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 70}}: _perfect_ πέπλᾰκα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.85K., 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.11, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Thucydide” 41}}: _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _pluperfect_ ἐπεπλάκει {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Erotianus Grammaticus “Praef.”}}: —_middle_, _future_ πλάσομαι {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.37}}: _aorist_ ἐπλασάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.58, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 800b, etc.}}:—_passive_, _future_ πλασθήσομαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.82 K., (δια-) 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 4.619}}: _aorist_ ἐπλάσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1130, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.48, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 26e}}: _perfect_ πέπλασμαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1030, etc.}}:—**form, mould**, properly of the artist who works in soft substances, such as earth, clay, wax, ἐκ γαίης π. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 70, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.47, 73}}; of Prometheus, ὃν λέγουσ᾽ ἡμᾶς πλάσαι καὶ τἄλλα.. ζῷα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 89.1, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 535.5; π. καθάπερ ἐκ κηροῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 746a; σχήματα ἐκ χρυσοῦ [prev. author] “Ti.” 50a; ἐκ πηλοῦ ζῷον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 654b29; ἀγγεῖον π. κήρινον [prev. author] “Mete.” 359a1; οὐκ ἔστιν ἀνδριαντοποιὸς ὅστις ἂν πλάσαι κάλλος τοιοῦτον 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 72.2; τοὺς πηλίνους 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.26}}; opposed to γράφειν, as sculpture to painting, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 510e}} (so in _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Lg.” 668e, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.75)}}; τὴν ὑδριαν πλάσαι **mould** the water-jar, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 926; σώματα π. θνητά 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 42d}}; π. κηρία, of bees, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 623b32}}; ἔπλαττεν ἔνδον οἰκίας **made clay** houses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 879}}; **knead** bread, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.313:}} —_middle_, σχῆμα πλασάμενος **having formed oneself** a figure, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 297e}}:— _passive_, **to be moulded, made**, τὸ δὲ ἐν τῇσι μήτρῃσι πλάσσεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.108; οἶκος τεκτόνων πλασθεὶς ὕπο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1130; ἂν ἴδωσι.. κήρινα μιμήματα πεπλασμένα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 933b.}}
**2** **plaster**, τὸν.. ναὸν χρίσαντες καὶ πλάσαντες {{Refs|“BCH” 15.209 (from Panamara).}}
**II** generally, **mould, form** by education, training, etc., π. τὰς ψυχὰς τοῖς μύθοις, τὰ σώματα ταῖς χερσίν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 377c; σῶμα ἐπιμελῶς [prev. author] “Ti.” 88c; ἑαυτόν [prev. author] “R.” 500d}}; παιδεύειν τε καὶ π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 671c}}:—_passive_, τοὔνομ᾽ ἀνὰ χρόνον πεπλασμένον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 830}}; of the voice, **to be trained**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 536b19.}}
**III** **form an image** of a thing **in the mind, imagine**, πλάττομεν οὔτε ἰδόντες οὔτε.. νοήσαντες ἀθάνατόν τι ζῷον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 246c, compare “R.” 420c, 466a; τῷ λόγῳ τοὺς νόμους [prev. author] “Lg.” 712b; τἀρχαῖα 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.85K.:}} —_passive_ {{Refs|[prev. work]p.82 K.}}
**IV** **put in a certain form**, τὸ στόμα π. (so as to pronounce more elegantly) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 414d}}; [κόμιον] {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.24.24; τὴν ὑπόκρισιν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 7:}} —_middle_, ἀδήλως τῇ ὄψει πλυσάμενος πρὸς τὴν ξυμφοράν **having formed himself** in face, i.e. composed his countenance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.58, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.68.}}
**V** _metaphorically_, **fabricate, forge**, λόγους ψιθύρους πλάσσων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 148 (_anapaest meter_); ψευδεῖς π. αἰτίας 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.25; προφάσεις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.28}}; τί λόγους πλάττει; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 18.121, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 17c; μὴ πλάσῃς κακόν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 145; π. ἐπιστολήν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.42.7: _absolutely_, δόξω πλάσας λέγειν}} I shall be thought to speak **from invention**, i.e. not the truth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.80, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.6.37}}:—_middle_, πλάσασθαι τὸν τρόπον τὸν αὑτοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.60; ψεύδη 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.26; τῆς φιλανθρωπίας ἣν.. ἐπλάττετο 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.231}}; προφάσεις π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 19.215; τοιαῦτα πλάττεσθαι τολμᾶτε [prev. author] 28.9; καιρὸν πλάττεσθαι [prev. author] 21.187: _absolutely_, πλαττομένους πρὸς ἑαυτούς (αὐτούς Bonitz) 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1381b28: with}}_infinitive_, Νέρων εἶναι πλασάμενος **pretending** to be N., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 64.9; π. νοσεῖν 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.1:}} —_passive_, οὐ πεπλασμένος ὁ κόμπος not **fictitious**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1030}}; πεπλάσθαι φάσκοντες saying **it was a forgery**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.2; μὴ πλασθέντα μῦθον ἀλλ᾽ ἀληθινὸν λόγον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 26e; π. ὑπὸ ποιητῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.23; ἐξ ὧν ἡ δίκη αὕτη πέπλασται 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 52.12. (πλαθ-yω, compare κορο-πλάθος, πηλο-πλάθος.)}}

4112:
** πλαστός**, ή, όν,
**formed, moulded**, especially in clay or wax, γυνή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 513; τὸ π. ὃ δὴ σκεῦος ὠνομάκαμεν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 219a; π. ἐκ γαίης 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 52.3}}; π. εἰκών **statue**, opposed to painting, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agesilaus” 2.}}
**2** **that can easily be moulded, plastic**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 386a27}}; **in a thick paste**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.4.10.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **fabricated, forged, counterfeit**, ἐκ λόγου πλαστοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.68}}; π. βακχεῖαι **sham** inspirations, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 218; π. τὴν φιλίαν παρέχεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 1.38}}; πλαστὸς πατρί a **supposititious** son, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 780, cf. Sositheus Tragicus 2.4}}; π. ἐπιχείρημα **hypothetical** case, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ εὑρέσεως” 3.11, compare 15.}} _adverb_ -τῶς, opposed to ὄντως{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 216c}}; opposed to ἀληθῶς, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 642d; opposed to φύσει, [prev. work] 777d}}; π. ὀδυρόμενοι **feignedly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.381 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**III** see at {πλατός}.

4113:
Included with: ** πλᾰτύς**, εῖα, ύ,
_Ionic dialect_ _feminine_ πλατέα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.156: _accusative_ _plural_ _feminine_ πλατέας “PMag.Par.” 1.1086}}:—**wide, broad,** τελαμών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.796; πτύον 13.588}}; αἰπόλια πλατέ᾽ αἰγῶν **broad** herds, i.e. **large** or **spread over a wide space,**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.474, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.101, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 445; π. πρόσοδοι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.45; ὁδοί 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.43, “IG” 22.380.20}}; τὴν ὁδὸν τὴν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Broad” Street, “SIG” 57.27 (Milet., 5th c.BC)}}; similarly, π. ὁδὸς τῶν θεῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“PStrassb.” 85.22 (2nd c.BC) compare(below 11); κιβώτιον π. “IG” 12.330.20; τάφρος ὡς πλατυτάτη καὶ βαθυτάτη 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.9.}}
**2** **flat, level,** χῶρος π. καὶ πολλός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.39; πλατυτάτης.. γῆς οὔσης Θετταλίας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.1.9; πότερον ἡ γῆ π. ἐστιν ἢ στρογγύλη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 97d}}; κάρυα τὰ π., i.e. chestnuts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.55, Diocl.fragment 126, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.4.29}}; σελάχη, ἰχθύες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 489b31, “PA” 695b7; ποτήρια πλατέα, τοίχους οὐκ ἔχοντ᾽ 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 143.2.}}
**3** of a man, **broad-shouldered,** οὐ γὰρ οἱ πλατεῖς, οὐδ᾽ εὐρύνωτοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1250, compare “UPZ” 121.19 (2nd c.BC).}}
**4** of seasons, **far advanced,** ἔαρ Procl.ad {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 483.}}
**5** _metaphorically_, π. ὅρκος a **broad strong** oath, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 30.3, compare 115.2}}; κατάγελως π. **flat** (i.e. downright) mockery, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1126}}; π. φλήναφος Amelius cited in {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Vita Plotini” 17, 18}}; but πλατὺ γελάσαι, καταγελᾶν, laugh **loud and** **rudely,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 7.39, “VS” 1.20.2; καταχρεμψαμένη πλατύ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 815, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cat.” 12.}}
**6** **broad,** of pronunciation, π. λέξις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 1.6; φωνή 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.116; πλατέα λαλοῦσι πάντα οἱ Δωριεῖς 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 177.}}
**7** **diffuse,** λέξις {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 19.}} _adverb_ -έως{{Refs|[prev. passage]: _comparative_ -ύτερον}} **in fuller detail,** διαλεξόμεθα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 2.219, compare 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.5, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.15.6}}; -υτέρως Tz.ad {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 177}}: _superlative_ **-υτάτως**{{Refs|[prev. author] “H.” 12.890.}}
**7.b** _adverb_ -έως **loosely,** opposed to ἀκριβῶς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.248 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**8** **widespread, frequent,** χρῆσις {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 1.332: _comparative_, [prev. work] 267.}}
**9** π. δρόμος, = Latin _cursus clabularis,_ {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “Mag.” 3.61.}}
**II** _substantive_ πλατεῖα (i.e. ὁδός,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|compare 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.188, and see.above 1.1)}}, ἡ, **street,** {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 58, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.53, “OGI” 491.9 (Pergam.), LXX.Gen.19.2, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.52, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.1.10, NT.Matt.12.19; οἱ ἐν τῇ Σκυτικῇ π. τεχνεῖται “IGRom.” 4.790, compare 791, +others (from Apamea); hence Σεβαστὴ π. name of a guild, [prev. work]3.711 (from Sura)}}; ἡ ἱερωτάτη π. {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3960b6 (from Apamea).}}
**II.b** (i.e. χείρ) **flat of the hand,** ταῖσι πλατείαις τυπτόμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1096; πλατείᾳ τῇ χειρί 3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus “de Venenatis Animalibus” 5.3.}}
**II.c** a kind of **dish** or **cup,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 11(2).110.22, +others (Delos, 3rd c.BC).}}
**II.d** **broad stripe** or **border,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 108.}}
**III** **salt, brackish,** πλατυτέροισι ἐχρέωντο τοῖσι πόμασι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.108}}; πλατέα or πλατύτερα ὕδατα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 358b4, 358a28 (but πλατὺς Ἑλλήσποντος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.86, 17.432}}, is not **the salt,** but **the broad,** Hellespont,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 875 (Lyric poetry), wrongly explained by 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 2.41b)}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _pṛthú-_ 'broad', **práthati** 'spread out', etc. But in _signification_ {{Refs|111 _cognate_ with Sanskrit}}**pa[tnull]u-** 'sharp', 'pungent', **tripa[tnull]u** 'the three saline substances'.)

4114:
** πλάτος** [ᾰ], εος, τό,
(πλατύς)
**A** **breadth, width,** σώματος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 188, etc.: _absolutely_, τὸ π. or π.}} **in breadth.** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.193, 4.195, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 19.3; ἴση μῆκός τε π. τε 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 17.20.}}
**A.b** in Mathematics texts, **breadth,** i.e. **the second dimension,** ἐν μήκει καὶ π. καὶ βάθει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 235d, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 209a5}}; κατὰ π., opposed to κατὰ μῆκος, κατὰ βάθος, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cael.” 299b26, “Mete.” 341b34.}}
**A.2** **plane surface,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 284e, “Lg.” 819e; μεγέθους τὸ ἐπὶ δύο [συνεχὲς] π. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1020a12.}}
**A.3** **latitude,** whether terrestrial or celestial, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.4.2, 2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 1.4, 2.4, 1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 2.12, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 30.12.}}
**A.4** _metaphorically_, **plane,** ἐν τῷ ψυχικῷ π. {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 201.}}
**A.5** **plane** of flat fish, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 489b33}}; **flat** of the tail, {{Refs|[prev. work] 549b1}}; **flat part** of the body of the fishing-frog, {{Refs|[prev. author] “PA” 695b15.}}
**A.6** **extension, breadth** of a subject, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.316; οὐκ ὀλίγον τὸ π. [prev. author] 11.738.}}
**A.7** ={πλάτας}, Judeich {{Refs|_Altertümervon Hierapolis_ No.322, +others}}
**A.II** _metaphorically_, **range** of variation, **latitude,** π. ἔχειν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.3.20; ἡ ὑγίεια π. ἔχει 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.12, compare11.737.}}
**A.III** with Preps., ἐν πλάτει in a **loose** sense, **broadly,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1.8.42, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.1.39, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 21, “Etymologicum Magnum” 673.24}}; opposed to κατ᾽ ἀκρίβειαν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 10.108; ὡς ἐν π. 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.24 (but περὶ ὧν ἐν τῷ π. λέγομεν}} which we will discuss **in detail,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.76)}}; also ἐπὶ πλάτει Ἑλληνίζειν talk **loose** Greek, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 2.9}}; κατὰ πλάτος λέγεσθαι to be said **loosely,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.164, compare 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.6, 21.}}
**A.IV** ={πλατύτης}{{Refs 4th c.BC+|3, 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 177.}}
**A.V** π. καρδίας, of Solomon, **width** of knowledge, {{LXX|LXX.3Ki.2.35a.}}
**A.VI** ἀργυρίου πλάτη, ={δραχμαί}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 9(1).189.15 (Tithora, 2nd c.AD).}}

4115:
** πλᾰτύν-ω**,
(πλατύς) **widen,** τὰ φυλακτήρια {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Matt.23.5; τὴν εἰσβολήν 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 5.8.1}}; π. τὸ στῖφος **widen** it **out,**{{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work] 5.2.1; τοὺς ἐφεξῆς στίχους 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 17.2: _metaphorically_, τὴν ψυχὴν εὐγενεστέραν τῆς φύσεως πλατύνας “IG” 5(2).268.12 (Mantinea, 1st c.BC):}} —_middle_, τὴν γῆν πλατύνεσθαι **widen one's** territory, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.34}}:—_passive_, **grow broad, widen out,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 841a2, “Mu.” 393a23, etc.}}; of the pupils, **to be dilated,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.376f: _metaphorically_, ἡ καρδία πεπλάτυνται}}**is opened, relieved from care,** {{LXX+NT|NT.2Cor.6.11; ἐπλάτυνας τὴν καρδίαν μου LXX.Psa.118(119).32.}}
**2** ἐπλατύνθη.. τὸ στόμα μου **was opened wide,**{{LXX|LXX.1Ki.2.1: hence, _metaphorically_, μὴ πλατυνθῇ ἡ καρδία}} **be puffed up,**{{LXX|LXX.Deu.11.16}}:—_middle_, **talk big of oneself,** τί πλατύνεαι, ἠλίθιος ὥ; {{Refs|Timo 34.4.}}
**3** **pronounce broadly,** τὴν φωνήν [{{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 1.6, 1st-2nd c.AD: Harpocratio Grammaticus see at {λαρυγγίζειν}.}}]
**4** **amplify,** τὸν λόγον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ind.Sto.” 24; τὴν ἑρμηνείαν 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “προγυμνάσματα” 3}}:—_passive_, διήγησις πλατύνεται τρόποις {{Refs|[prev. author] “Inv.” 2.7}}: _absolutely_, **use amplification,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Dinarcho” 6.}}

4116:
** πλᾰτύς**, εῖα, ύ,
_Ionic dialect_ _feminine_ πλατέα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.156: _accusative_ _plural_ _feminine_ πλατέας “PMag.Par.” 1.1086}}:—**wide, broad,** τελαμών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.796; πτύον 13.588}}; αἰπόλια πλατέ᾽ αἰγῶν **broad** herds, i.e. **large** or **spread over a wide space,**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.474, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.101, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 445; π. πρόσοδοι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.45; ὁδοί 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.43, “IG” 22.380.20}}; τὴν ὁδὸν τὴν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Broad” Street, “SIG” 57.27 (Milet., 5th c.BC)}}; similarly, π. ὁδὸς τῶν θεῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“PStrassb.” 85.22 (2nd c.BC) compare(below 11); κιβώτιον π. “IG” 12.330.20; τάφρος ὡς πλατυτάτη καὶ βαθυτάτη 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.9.}}
**2** **flat, level,** χῶρος π. καὶ πολλός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.39; πλατυτάτης.. γῆς οὔσης Θετταλίας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.1.9; πότερον ἡ γῆ π. ἐστιν ἢ στρογγύλη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 97d}}; κάρυα τὰ π., i.e. chestnuts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.55, Diocl.fragment 126, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.4.29}}; σελάχη, ἰχθύες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 489b31, “PA” 695b7; ποτήρια πλατέα, τοίχους οὐκ ἔχοντ᾽ 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 143.2.}}
**3** of a man, **broad-shouldered,** οὐ γὰρ οἱ πλατεῖς, οὐδ᾽ εὐρύνωτοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1250, compare “UPZ” 121.19 (2nd c.BC).}}
**4** of seasons, **far advanced,** ἔαρ Procl.ad {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 483.}}
**5** _metaphorically_, π. ὅρκος a **broad strong** oath, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 30.3, compare 115.2}}; κατάγελως π. **flat** (i.e. downright) mockery, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1126}}; π. φλήναφος Amelius cited in {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Vita Plotini” 17, 18}}; but πλατὺ γελάσαι, καταγελᾶν, laugh **loud and** **rudely,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 7.39, “VS” 1.20.2; καταχρεμψαμένη πλατύ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 815, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cat.” 12.}}
**6** **broad,** of pronunciation, π. λέξις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 1.6; φωνή 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.116; πλατέα λαλοῦσι πάντα οἱ Δωριεῖς 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 177.}}
**7** **diffuse,** λέξις {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 19.}} _adverb_ -έως{{Refs|[prev. passage]: _comparative_ -ύτερον}} **in fuller detail,** διαλεξόμεθα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 2.219, compare 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.5, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.15.6}}; -υτέρως Tz.ad {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 177}}: _superlative_ **-υτάτως**{{Refs|[prev. author] “H.” 12.890.}}
**7.b** _adverb_ -έως **loosely,** opposed to ἀκριβῶς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.248 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**8** **widespread, frequent,** χρῆσις {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 1.332: _comparative_, [prev. work] 267.}}
**9** π. δρόμος, = Latin _cursus clabularis,_ {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “Mag.” 3.61.}}
**II** _substantive_ πλατεῖα (i.e. ὁδός,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|compare 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.188, and see.above 1.1)}}, ἡ, **street,** {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 58, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.53, “OGI” 491.9 (Pergam.), LXX.Gen.19.2, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.52, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.1.10, NT.Matt.12.19; οἱ ἐν τῇ Σκυτικῇ π. τεχνεῖται “IGRom.” 4.790, compare 791, +others (from Apamea); hence Σεβαστὴ π. name of a guild, [prev. work]3.711 (from Sura)}}; ἡ ἱερωτάτη π. {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3960b6 (from Apamea).}}
**II.b** (i.e. χείρ) **flat of the hand,** ταῖσι πλατείαις τυπτόμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1096; πλατείᾳ τῇ χειρί 3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus “de Venenatis Animalibus” 5.3.}}
**II.c** a kind of **dish** or **cup,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 11(2).110.22, +others (Delos, 3rd c.BC).}}
**II.d** **broad stripe** or **border,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 108.}}
**III** **salt, brackish,** πλατυτέροισι ἐχρέωντο τοῖσι πόμασι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.108}}; πλατέα or πλατύτερα ὕδατα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 358b4, 358a28 (but πλατὺς Ἑλλήσποντος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.86, 17.432}}, is not **the salt,** but **the broad,** Hellespont,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 875 (Lyric poetry), wrongly explained by 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 2.41b)}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _pṛthú-_ 'broad', **práthati** 'spread out', etc. But in _signification_ {{Refs|111 _cognate_ with Sanskrit}}**pa[tnull]u-** 'sharp', 'pungent', **tripa[tnull]u** 'the three saline substances'.)

4117:
** πλέγ-μα**, ατος, τό,
**anything twined** or **twisted,** π. ἕλικος the **twisting** tendril of the vine, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 183.2; γυίων π. “Anthologia Graeca” 5.245 (Paul. Sil.), compare 285 ([prev. author]).}}
**2** **plaited work, wicker-work,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 734e, etc.; τὸ τοῦ κύρτου π. [prev. author] “Ti.” 79d: hence, ={κύρτος}, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.28}}]: in _plural_, **wreaths, chaplets,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1393}}; also, **plaited hair,** {{NT|NT.1Tim.2.9.}}
**3** π. δικτυοειδές **the rete mirabile Galeni,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herophilus Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 5.155, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 9.4, +others}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **complex, combination of words,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 262d}}: generally, **complex,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.372, 651, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.3.4.}}

4118:
** πλεῖστος**, η, ον,
_superlative_ of πολύς, **most, greatest, largest,** in number, size, extent, etc., π. ὅμιλος, λαός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.616, 16.377, etc.; π. κακόν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.697; πλεῖστοι ἐπιχθονίων ἀνθρώπων 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 33.1; π. εὐκλείας γέρας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 478; φιλοσοφία παλαιοτάτη τε καὶ πλείστη}}**most in vogue,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 342a; π. τῶν Ἑλληνικῶν φῦλον τὸ Ἀρκαδικόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.23, etc.}}; τῇ γνώμῃ πλεῖστός εἰμι, ἡ π. γνώμη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.220 (assuming variant), 5.126}}; πλεῖστον σχήσειν, as _superlative_ of πλέον ἔχειν (see. πλείων), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.36.}}
**2** with _Article_, οἱ π. **the greatest number,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.90, etc.}}; τὸ π. τοῦ βίου **the greatest part** of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 718a, etc. (also same gender as the foll. Noun, ὁ π. τοῦ βίου, ἡ π. τῆς στρατιᾶς, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.5, 7.3); τῇ ὄψει τοῦ θαρσεῖν τὸ π. εἰληφότες [prev. author] 4.34; τῷ πλούτῳ διδοὺς τὸ π. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 408.}}
**II** Special usages: with _relative_, ὅσας ἂν πλείστας δύνωνται καταστρέφεσθαι subdue **the greatest** number that they **possibly** could, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.44; ὡς ἂν δύνωνται πλεῖστα “IG” 12.98.4, compare 109.10, 113.37; ὁπόσσω κα πλείστω ἄξιος ᾖ “Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.160 (from Cyrene)}}; ὅς κα πλεῖστον διδῷ ἀποδόμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Leg.Gort.” 5.48; ὡς π. χρόνον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 481b; ὅτι π. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.64, etc.: coupled with εἷς (which see), εἷς ἀνὴρ π. πόνον ἐχθροῖς παρασχών 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 327: in comparative sense, πλείστον ἄξια ἢ ὥς τις οἴεται 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 57 (but πλεῖστα ἤ is corrupt in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.35).}}
**III** _adverb_ usages: πλεῖστον **most,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.287, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 231, etc.; ὡς π. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.12}}: sometimes added to a _superlative_, π. ἐχθίστη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 631; π. ἀνθρώπων.. κάκιστος [prev. author] “OC” 743; τὴν π. ἡδίστην θεῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 790: πλεῖστα}} as _adverb_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.97, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 720, etc.; πολλάκις μὲν.., π. δὲ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 281b}}; π. χαίρειν, frequently in letters, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“POxy.” 742 (1st c.BC), etc.}}
**III.b** **furthest,** π. ἀφεστηκέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 587a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 391a13.}}
**III.2** with _Article_, τὸ π. **at most,** ἡμερῶν τεσσάρων τὸ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 260, etc.; τὰ π.}}**for the most part,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 118c, etc.}}; opposed to ἐνίοτε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 563a31.—The form πλείστως is cited by 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(1).855 from 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.1.10 (πλεῖστα codices).}}
**IV** with Preps.:
**IV.1** διὰ πλείστου **furthest off,** in point of space or time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.115, 6.11.}}
**IV.2** ἐς πλεῖστον **most,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 739.}}
**IV.3** ἐπὶ πλεῖστον **over the greatest distance, to the greatest extent,** in point of space, time, or extent, ἐπὶ π. χλιδῆς ἀπίκετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.127; ἐπὶ π. τοῦ γενησομένου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.138; ἐκ τοῦ ἐπὶ π. [prev. author] 1.2; ἐπὶ π. ἀνθρώπων [prev. work] 1}}; ὡς ἐπὶ π. or ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ π., **for the most part,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.14, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 720d.}}
**IV.4** κατὰ τὸ π. **for the most part,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.4.7, etc.}}
**IV.5** περὶ πλείστου ποιεῖσθαι, see at {περί} {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus IV.}}
**IV.6** ἐν τοῖς πλεῖσται, see ὁ, ἡ, τό, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus v111. 6.}}

4119:
** πλείων**,
**πλέων**, ὁ, ἡ, _neuter_ πλεῖον, πλέον, πλεῖν, _comparative_ of πολύς (on the forms see entry near the end), **more,** of number, size, extent, etc., οἱ δὲ μάχονται παυρότεροι πλεόνεσσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.739; πλείων μὲν πλεόνων μελέτη 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 380}}; ἐς πλείονας οἰκεῖν govern for the interest of **the majority,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.37}}; πλέον᾽ ἔλπομαι λόγον Ὀδυσσέος ἢ πάθαν **greater** than.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.21}}; τὸν πλείω λόγον all **further** speech, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 731; ὁ ὄχλος πλείων καὶ πλείων ἐπέρρει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.39}}; πλείω τὸν πλοῦν.. ποιησάμενοι having made the voyage **longer,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.39}}; ὁ π. βίος a **longer** life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 75c; μακροτέρα καὶ π. ὁδός [prev. author] “R.” 435d, etc.}}; of Time, **longer,** π. χρόνος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.111, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 74; πλέων νὺξ τῶν δύο μοιράων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.252.}}
**2** with _Article_, οἱ πλέονες **the greater number, the mass** or **crowd,**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5.673, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.277; οἱ πλεῦνες 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.106, etc.: with _genitive_, τὰς πλεῦνας τῶν γυναικῶν [prev. work] 1}}; **the people,** opposed to the chief men, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.149, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.73, 89, etc.}}; euphemistic of the dead, ἀνεστηκυῖα παρὰ τῶν πλειόνων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1073; εὖτ᾽ ἂν ἵκηαι ἐς πλεόνων “Anthologia Graeca” 11.42 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus)}}; ἐς πλεόνων μετοικεσίην{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 7.731 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus)}}; τὸ πλεῖον πολέμοιο **the greater part** of.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.165}}; ὅστις τοῦ πλέονος μέρους χρῄζει, opposed to τοῦ μετρίου, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1211 (Lyric poetry); τοῦ πλέονος ἐλπίδι ὀρέγονται 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.17, compare 92.}}
**II** peculiar usages of _neuter_:
**II.1** as a Noun, **more,** πλεῦν ἔτι τούτου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.19, etc.; εἴ τι ἐνορῶ πλέον [prev. author] 1.89}}; τὸ δὲ π. nay, what is **more,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 158 (Musgr. for τί δὲ..)}}; to a **greater** extent, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.90, 7.57, etc.}}; πλέον or τὸ πλέον τινός **a higher degree** of a thing, τίς πλέον τᾶς εὐδαιμονίας φέρε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1189 (Lyric poetry); τὸ π. τοῦ χρόνου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.118, etc.}}; also τὸ π. ὃ ἀναφέρει the **excess** which he reports, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 661 (3rd c.BC)}}; ᾧ πλεῖον the **excess,** probably in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.42 (3rd c.BC),compare “PCair.Zen.” 742.26 (3rd c.BC)}}; πλέον ἔχειν to have **the advantage,** have **the best** of it, like{πλεονεκτέω}, with _genitive_, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.70, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 343d, 349b, etc.; τὸ π. πάντων ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.18: more fully, μοίρης πλεῖον ἔχειν 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 606; π. τινὸς φέρεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.29; π. φέρεσθαι τῶν ἄλλων 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.4, etc.}}; π. ποιεῖν **do some good, be successful,** βουλοίμην ἂν πλέον τί με ποιῆσαι ἀπολογούμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 19a}}; οὐδὲν π. ποιήσειν, -ῆσαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.149, 4.7, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 115c, etc.; παραινοῦσ᾽ οὐδὲν ἐς π. ποιῶ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 918; οὐδὲν π. ὀψοφαγῶν ποιήσεις 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.344b; οὐδὲν εἴργασμαι π. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 284; οὐδὲν π. πρᾶξαι [prev. author] “IA” 1373, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.20, etc.}}; οὐδὲν ἐπίσταμαι π. have no **superior** knowledge, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 161b}}; τί πλέο; what **more,** i.e. what **good** or **use** is it? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.95, etc.; τί π. πλουτεῖν.. πάντων ἀποροῦντα; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 531; τί σοι π. λυπουμένῃ γένοιτ᾽ ἄ; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 322; τί π. ἔστ᾽ εἰς τέκνα πονεῖ; “Supp.epigram” 1.567.1 (Karanis, 3rd c.BC),compare “Anthologia Graeca” 7.261.1 (3rd c.BC(?): Diotimus Epigrammaticus); also οὐδὲν ἦν π. τοῖς πεπονθόσιν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.4 (= 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.7), compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.31; ὧν οὐδέν μοι π. γέγονε 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.28; οὐδέν γέ σοι π. ἔσται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 341a; τί τὸ π.; “epigram Gr.” 306 a. 3}}; ἐπὶ πλέον as _adverb_, **more, further,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.171, 5.51, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.54, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 261b, etc.}}: with _genitive_, **beyond,** ἐπὶ π. τῶν ἄλλων ἰσχύσας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.9 (but, ἐπὶ τὸ π. ἵκεο μοίσας}}**to surpassing height in..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.20); also ὅταν τις ἐς π. πέσῃ τοῦ θέλοντος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1219 codices (Lyric poetry)}}; περὶ πλείονος ποιεῖσθαι, see at {περί} {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus IV.}}
**II.2** as _adverb_, **more, rather,** π. ἔφερέ οἱ ἡ γνώμη κατεργάσεσθαι τὴν Ἑλλάδα he inclined **rather** to the belief.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.100; οὐ τοῦτο δειμαίνεις π.; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 41; σέ.. τῶνδ᾽ ἐς πλέον σέβω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 700; ἢ π. ἢ ἔλαττον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.125}}; π. ἔλαττον **more** or less, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“BGU” 402.9 (6th c.AD), “IG” 14.177 (from Syracuse)}}; also τὸ π., _Ionic dialect_ τὸ πλεῦν, **for the most part,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.81, etc.}}; αὐτῆς τὸ π. μέτοχός εἰμι have **the larger** share, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.52}}; τὸ π., ={μᾶλλον, οὐ χάριτι τὸ π. ἢ φόβῳ} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.9, compare 2.37}}; ἐστὶν ὁ πόλεμος οὐχ ὅπλων τὸ π., ἀλλὰ δαπάνης not **so much.., as..,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.83.}}
**II.2.b** with Numerals, τοξότας π. ἢ εἴκοσι μυριάδας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.6; οἶκος πλέον ἢ τεττάρων ταλάντων 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 10.23}}; ἐν πλέον ἢ διακοσίοις ἔτεσι variant in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.141 (perhaps πλεῖν, see below)}}; π. ἢ ἐν διπλασίῳ χρόνῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 21.3:—in this sense a short form πλεῖν is used by _Attic dialect_ writers (cf. Moer. p.294}} P., but the rule is not universal,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare “IG” 22.657.25 (3rd c.BC), etc.), πλεῖν ἢ τριάκονθ᾽ ἡμέρας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 858}}; πλεῖν ἢ χιλίας (i.e. δραχμάς) {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 444; στάδια πλεῖν ἢ χίλια [prev. author] “Av.” 6, compare “Nu.” 1041, 1065, +others; πλεῖν ἤ γε διπλοῦν [prev. author] “Lys.” 589; πλεῖν ἢ 'νιαυτῷ πρεσβύτερος [prev. author] “Ra.” 18, compare 91}}; πλεῖν (πλεῖον codices) ἢ πέντε τάλαντα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.173; πλεῖν ἢ δυοῖν ποδοῖν 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 119.10}}; ἤ is frequently omitted, πλεῖν ἑξακοσίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1251}}; ἔτη γεγονὼς πλείω ἑβδομήκοντα variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 17d}}; but δέκα πλείοσιν ἔτεσι for ten years **more,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 932c; τρεῖς μῆνας καὶ πλείω 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.2.16; λίθους.. ὅσον μνααίους καὶ πλεῖον καὶ μεῖον [prev. author] “Eq.Mag.” 1.16}}: with number in _genitive_, κώμας.. οὐ πλεῖον εἴκοσι σταδίων ἀπεχούσας {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 3.2.34, compare 7.3.12.}}
**II.2.c** Comedy texts, πλεῖν ἢ μαίνομαι **more than to** madness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 103, 751.}}
**II.2.d** _plural_ πλείω used like{πλέον}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 417b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.213, etc.; τὰ π. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.81; πλέω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 868 codices}}
**II.2.e** regular _adverb_ πλειόνως {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 7.4, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.1.1.}}
**B** FORMS: _Epic dialect_ use πλείων or πλέων as metre requires, also _nominative_ and _accusative_ _plural_ πλέες, πλέᾰς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.129, 11.395, Callias Epigrammaticus “Aet.Oxy.” 2080.85}} (so, with ι from ε, Cretan dialect πλίες, πλίας, {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 7.18,24, “GDI” 5125 B 8, also πλίαδ (δὲ) “Leg.Gort.” 7.29, πλίανς [prev. work]5.54}}; πλέας also _Aeolic dialect_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12(2).1.9 (Mytil., 4th c.BC))}}; _dative_ _plural_ πλεόνεσσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.739 (πλεόνεσιν}} is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.224); Cretan dialect also _accusative_ _sing._ _neuter_ πλίον “Leg.Gort.” 1.37, +others, _genitive_ πλίονος [prev. work]2.39, +others, _neuter_ _plural_ πλίονα [prev. work]4.51, πλία [prev. work]10.17}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **πλήων** Hdn.Gr.2.431, also late _Doric dialect_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“IPE” 12.79.18 (Byzant., 1st c.AD)}}; _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. have -ει- always before -ου- and -ω-, {{Refs|“IG” 12.76.7, 22.657.25, 2498.22, etc., but -ε- and -ει- before -ο-, [prev. work]12.94.33,40.3,4, 22.2670.4 (but always πλέον).}}

4120:
** πλέκ-ω**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.86, etc.}}: _future_ πλέξω {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.146 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus)}}: _aorist_ ἔπλεξα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.176, etc.:}}_perfect_ πέπλοχα (_Attic dialect_ _accusative_ to Hdn.Gr.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|2.356), (δια-) 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ὀστέων φύσιος” 16, (ἐμ-) [prev. passage], but ἐμ-πέπλεχε [prev. work] 17}}:—_middle_, _future_ πλέξομαι {{Refs 5th c.AD+|Perictione Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.28.19}}: _aorist_ ἐπλεξάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.168, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 790:}} —_passive_, _future_ πλεχθήσομαι (ἐμ-) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1079 (_anapaest meter_)}}; πλᾰκήσομαι (ἐπι-) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.873}}: _aorist_ ἐπλέχθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 259 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 283a, (περι-) 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.33}}; but also _aorist 2_ ἐπλάκην [ᾰ], _participle_ πλᾰκείς (ἐμ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1236, (συμ-) 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 618}}; also _participle_ κατα-πλεκείς [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, variant in 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.73.1, περι-πλεκείς 5th-6th c.BC: Timotheus Lyricus “Persae” 157, συμπλεκείς 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.84 (variant{-πλακ-})}}]: _perfect_ πέπλεγμαι {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.72, etc.}}:—**plait, twine,** πλοκάμους ἔπλεξε φαεινούς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.176; στέφανον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).73, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 458; ἐκ τῆς βίβλου ἱστία 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.8.4; ἀνθερίκεσσι ἀκριδοθήραν 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.52:}} —_middle_, πεῖσμα.. πλεξάμενος **having twisted me** a rope, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.168, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.28; π. ἄρκυς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 790 (Lyric poetry)}}:—_passive_, κράνεα πεπλεγμένα **of basket-work,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.72}}; χρέωνται σειρῇσι πεπλεγμένῃσι ἐξ ἱμάντων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 85; βρόχος πεπλ. σπάρτου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 9.13.}}
**2** **make by art,** βωμόν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Apollinem” 61.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **devise, contrive,** mostly of tortuous means, π. δόλον ἀμφί τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 220: proverbial, δεινοὶ πλέκειν τοι μηχανὰς Αἰγύπτιοι [prev. author] “fragment” 373; so π. πλοκάς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 826}}; ἐκ τέχνης τέχνην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1280; παντοίας παλάμας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 644 (Lyric poetry)}}:—_passive_, μηχανὴ πεπλεγμένη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 995.}}
**II.2** of Poets, π. ὕμνον, ῥήματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.86, “N.” 4.94; ᾠδάς 5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus “fragment” 1 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; π. λόγους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 834, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Mi.” 369b}}; **form the plot** of a tragedy, opposed to λύειν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1456a9:}} —hence in _passive_, μῦθοι πεπλεγμένοι **complex,** opposed to ἁπλοῖ, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 1452a12, compare b32, 1459b9; συλλογισμὸς πεπλ. 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.29.34; πέπλεκται [ἡ τῶν συμπάντων τῶν ὄντων νομοθεσία] ἐκ.. λόγων τε καὶ αἰτίων κτλ. 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.3.15.}}
**II.3** χρόνον τοῦ ζῆν π., ={διαπλέκω}{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|11, 3rd c.BC: Euphro Comicus 5.}}
**II.4** **compound,** ἐκ λευκοῦ καὶ μέλανος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 12.165 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus)}}:—_passive_, of words or syllables, **to be compounded,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 202b, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 5.30.}}
**II.5** _passive_, **twine oneself round,** περὶ βρέτει πλεχθεὶς θεᾶς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 259 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.6** _passive_, **to be involved, entangled,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 169.32}}. (Cf. Latin _plecto, im-plico,_ OHG. **flehtan.**)

4121:
** πλεον-άζω**
(rarely πλειονάζω, which see), _future_ -άσω: _perfect_ πεπλεόνακα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.90}}:—_passive_, _perfect_ **-ασμαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 7, etc.}}: _aorist_ **-άσθην**{{Refs|[prev. author] “Art.” 47}}: (πλέον):—**to be more,** especially **to be more than enough, superfluous,** opposed to ἐλλείπειν, ὑπολείπειν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1106a31, “Col.” 799a18}}; τὸ πλεονάζον the **excess,** {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|PRev.Laws 57.13 (3rd c.BC), LXX.Exo.26.12}}; π. παρά with _accusative_, **to be in excess of..,**{{LXX+NT|LXX.Num.3.46; ἐπλεόνασεν ἡ ἁμαρτία NT.Rom.5.20}}; of animals, **have more than the due number** of limbs, opposed to κολοβὰ γίνεσθαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 770b32}}; of visits, **to be frequent,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.3.12}}; of the sea, **encroach,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 351b6, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.366b}}; πάθος defined as ὁρμὴ πλεονάζουσα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.50}}; εἰκασία ἐστὶ μεταφορὰ πλεονάζουσα simile is **expanded** metaphor, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 80; τὸ ς ¯ πλεονάσαν}}**used to excess,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 14}}: Grammars, **to be redundant,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus “Herc.” 1012.21, etc.; Ἀρίσταρχος οὐκ ἔλεγε πλεονάζειν τὸ ἄρθρον 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 6.2}}; also of letters, τὸ ε ¯ πλεονάζει (in ἑ-ώρων) {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pron.” 58.25}}; but π. τῷ ῑ to have an **added** ῑ (as in ἐμεῖο), {{Refs|[prev. work] 38.20; compare 111.6.}}
**2** with _genitive_, **exceed,** opposed to λείπω, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Geographia” 1.20.7: _absolutely_, τὸ -άζον ἔργον}} the **extra** work, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PLille” 1v. 16 (3rd c.BC)}}; τοὺς -άζοντας τῶν ρκέ (i.e. ἐρίφους) the **odd** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|25 out of 125, “PCair.Zen.” 422.7 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II** of persons, **go beyond bounds, take** or **claim too much,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.33, 12.85, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.24, 39.14}}: with _dative_, **presume upon..,** εὐτυχίᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.120; but π. κυνηγεσίαις}}**go beyond bounds in..,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 11.5.1}}; of a writer, τοῖς ὀνόμασι π. {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.3.7}}: _absolutely_, **to be lengthy, tedious,** {{LXX+1st c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.1.16, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.90, LXX.2Mac.2.32}}; περί τινος Parmenisc. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.156d.}}
**II.2** π. τινός **have an excess of, abound in** a thing, opposed to ἐνδεὴς εἶναι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1257a33, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 4}}; but π. τοῦ καιροῦ **exceed all bounds..,** of a writer, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 22.}}
**III** with _accusative_, **state at a higher figure,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 6.3.10}}:—_passive_, **to be magnified, exaggerated,** [νομίσειεν ἂν] ἔστιν ἃ πλεονάζεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.35, compare 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.4.3}}; πεπλεόνασται **has been overdone,** opposed to ἐνδεὲς πεποίηται, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 7, compare “Art.” 47.}}
**III.2** **make to increase,** τινὰς τῇ ἀγάπῃ {{NT|NT.1Thes.3.12.}}
**III.3** **eat in too great quantity,** τι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Diphilus Siphnius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.356d, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.75, 82 (all _passive_).}}
**III.4** **raise the price of,** τι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.170J.}}
**III.5** _passive_, **to be deceived,** probably _falsa lectio_ for{πλεονεκτεῖσθαι}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 2.7.11m.}}
**III.6** Grammars, **use in addition** or **redundantly,** εἰώθασιν οἱ Ἀττικοὶ τὰ ἄρθρα πλεονάζειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 5; Αἰολεῖς πλεονάζουσιν ἕτερον σύμφωνον “Etymologicum Magnum” 84.18:}} —_passive_, τὸ ῡ πλεοναζόμενον ψιλοῦται{{Refs|[prev. work] 440.12.}}
**III.7** **to be in excess of unity, partake of plurality,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 2.}}

4122:
** πλεονεκτ-έω**,
_future_ **-ήσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.62, etc. (πλέον ἐκτήσεται should be read in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 192e)}}:—Prose Verb, **have** or **claim more than one's due,** mostly in bad sense, **to be greedy, grasping,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.112, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.6.21, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 483c, etc.}}
**2** also, **gain** or **have some advantage,** without any bad sense, δυνάμει τινὶ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.62, compare 86}}; opposed to ἐλαττοῦσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1360a3; πολὺ ἐπλεονέκτει ὁ Πελοπίδας παρὰ τῷ Πέρσῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.34: _absolutely_, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1402b25, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.46; π. ἀπὸ τῶν μὴ καθηκόντων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.56.2: frequently with _neuter_ _pronoun_, π. ταῦτα, etc., 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.61, etc.}}
**3** with _genitive_ _of things_, **have** or **claim a larger share of** than others, τῶν ὠφελίμων {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.39}}; τοῦ ἡλίου, τοῦ ψύχους, τῶν πόνων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.25, compare “Oec.” 7.26}}; δόξης, χάριτος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1136b22, 1137a1.}}
**II** with _genitive_ _person_, **have** or **gain the advantage over,** τῶν ἐχθρῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 362b, compare 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Lycophrone” 8, etc.; παρά τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.32 (variant); παρ᾽ ἀλλήλων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1292b19}}; τινι **in** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.3.21, etc.; κατά τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 15a; περί τι [prev. author] “La.” 183a}}; also π. τῶν νόμων **gain at the expense of** the laws, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 691a}}; τῆς ὑμετέρας π. εὐηθείας **take advantage of** your simpleness, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Prooemia” 24.}}
**II.2** later with _accusative_ _person_, **get** or **have the advantage over,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 9.7, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marcellus” 29, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 27}}: usually in bad sense, **overreach, defraud,** πλεονεκτεῖν μηδένα {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 259, cf. NT.1Thes.4.6, NT.2Cor.7.2, 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 17.8, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 52.37}}: in early writers only _passive_ in this sense, ὑπό τινων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.2}}; πλεονεκτεῖσθαι χιλίαις δραχμαῖς **to be defrauded in** or **of** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1, 000 drachmae, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 41.25. In 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.77 πλεονεκτεῖσθαι}} is _impersonal_, **to be an act of** πλεονεξία.
**II.3** _passive_, **to be surpassed, excelled,** τινι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus “Abhandlungen der Göttinger Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften (Phil.-hist. Klasse)” 173.5.}}

4123:
** πλεονέκτ-ης**, ου, ὁ,
={ὁ πλέον ἔχων}, **one who has** or **claims more than his due, greedy, grasping,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.40, etc.: as}} _adjective_ λόγος π. **a greedy, arrogant** speech, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.158}}: _superlative_ πλεονεκτίστατος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.12.}}
**2** ἐν παντὶ πλεονέκτην τῶν πολεμίων **making gain** from their losses, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.6.27.}}
**3** _metaphorically_ in in Mathematics texts, of τὸ ὑπερτελές, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “in Nicomachi arithmeticam introductionem” p.32 P.}}

4124:
** πλεονεξία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, **greediness, assumption, arrogance,** τῶν Σπαρτιητέων ἡ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.149, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.13, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.240, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 359c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.15}}; π. συγγενική **wrong** done to one's kin, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 24.108.}}
**2** **assumption,** αἱ ἐν τῷ πυνθάνεσθαι π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 175a19.}}
**II** **advantage,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.183, 15.275, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.128: _plural_, αἱ ἐν τῷ πολέμῳ π. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.22, etc.}}; αἱ π. αἱ ἴδιαι, αἱ δημόσιαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 13.10}}; ἐπὶ πλεονεξίᾳ with a view to **one's own advantage,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.84, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.12}}; μετὰ πλεονεξίας τινὸς ἀγωνίζεσθαι πρὸς [τὰ θηρία] {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.6.28; πλεονεξίαι ψυχῆς}}**excellences,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.6.3.}}
**II.2** **a larger share of** a thing, τῶν πολιτικῶν δικαίων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1282b29.}}
**II.3** **gain derived** from a thing, τὴν ἐπὶ τῶν ἰδίων δικῶν πλεονεξίαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.28; αἱ π. τῶν πλουσίων}}**undue gains,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1297a11, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 586b; π. ἔκ τινος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.56.3.}}
**III** **excess,** opposed to ἔνδεια, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 82a.}}

4125:
** πλευρ-ά**, ᾶς, ἡ,
={πλευρόν}, **rib,** rare in _singular_, βοὸς π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.64; παρὰ τὴν π. ἑκάστην 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 513b29}}: _plural_, **ribs,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “PA” 654b35.}}
**2** _plural_, generally, **side** of a man or animal, ἄλλοτ᾽ ἐπὶ πλευρὰς κατακείμενος, ἄλλοτε δ᾽ αὖτε ὕπτιος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.10}}; of **both sides,** ἀνὰ πλευράς τε καὶ ὤμους {{Refs 8th c.BC+|23.716; οὐρῇ δὲ πλευράς τε καὶ ἰσχία ἀμφοτέρωθεν μαστίεται 20.170, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 430, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 71, “Eu.” 843 (Lyric poetry)}}: _singular_, also, of **one side,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1260, “Aj.” 834, etc.}}; a **side** of beef, etc., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 381.5 (3rd c.BC, written πλερά)}}: the _plural_ form is variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 826.}}
**II** **side,** of things and places, νηὸς πλευραί {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 513; χωρίου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sisyphus” 388e}}; [Πακτωλοῦ] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 833}}; of an army, αἱ π. τοῦ πλαισίου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.22, compare 28, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 25, etc.; παρὰ π. τινὶ εἶναι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.26.6}}; παρὰ π., opposed to κατὰ κεφαλήν, {{Refs|“IG” 22.463.72}}
**III** in Mathematics texts, **side** of a triangle or other figure, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Sophista 13, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 53d, 54c, etc.: especially}}
**III.b** **side** of a rectangle, {{Refs|[prev. work] 36c: hence}}**one factor** of any product, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tht.” 148a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1051a26, 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 7 “Def.” 17, etc.}}
**III.c** **side** of a square of cube, and **root** of a square of cubic number, {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.11, 12, “Theologumena Arithmeticae” 11}}; κυβικὴ π. cube **root,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 52.4.}}
**III.d** **generator** of a cone or cylinder, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 1.8, 12.}}

4126:
** πλέω**,
_Epic dialect_ **πλείω** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.34, 16.368}}: _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ ἀπ-έπλειον {{Refs|8.501}}: also _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ **πλώω** (see. below): _Attic dialect_ _contraction_ _imperative_ πλεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 102 (_anapaest meter_)}}: _future_ πλεύσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.25, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.22 (ἀνα-), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.29, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.104, etc.; later πλευσοῦμαι “SIG” 402.27 (Chios, 3rd c.BC)}}, found in codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.143, 8.1, (ἐπεσ-) [prev. author] 4.13, (συνεκ-) 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.25, (ἀπο-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 370d, 371b, (συμ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.19, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ πλευσοῦμαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.55}}; but _3rd.pers._ _plural_ πλεύσονται {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“GDI” 5120 B 11,13 (Crete, 3rd c.BC)}}; πλεύσω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 116 (S.V.l.), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.12.3, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.162 (1st c.AD: Nicarchus Epigrammaticus), 245 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus), “OGI” 572.30 (Lycia, 2nd-3rd c.AD)}}, etc.: _aorist 1_ ἔπλευσα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 691 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.: _perfect_ πέπλευκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 404, etc.:}} —_passive_, _future_ πλευσθήσομαι (περι-) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 5.26.2:}}_aorist_ ἐπλεύσθην{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 6.28.6, 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 71.3}}: _perfect_ πέπλευσμαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.16, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.12: 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses only}}_present_, _imperfect_, and _future_ πλεύσομαι (see. above).—Of the _Ionic dialect_ **πλώω**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses _optative_ πλώοιεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.240}}_participle_ ἐπι-πλώων{{Refs|[prev. work] 284, πλώων “Hymni Homerici” 22.7}}: _imperfect_ πλῶον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.302}}; also shortened _aorist_ ἔπλων, ως, ω, _participle_ πλώς, in the compounds ἀπ-έπλω, ἐπ-έπλως, _participle_ ἐπιπλώς, παρέπλ; and {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus}} has ἐπ-έπλω; the _present_, _imperfect_, and _future_ forms occur as variants in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}}, _infinitive_ πλώειν {{Refs|4.156}}, _participle_ πλωούσας {{Refs|8.10, 22, 42:}}_imperfect_ ἔπλωον{{Refs|[prev. work] 41}}; Iterat. πλώεσκον {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 14.656:}}_future_ πλώσομαι (ἀπο-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.5 (πλώσω 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 1044)}}; but the _aorist 1_ forms are read in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, ἔπλωσα 4.148}}; _infinitive_ πλῶσαι {{Refs|1.24}}; _participle_ πλώσας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.156, 8.49 (also once in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus in _compound_ ἐπι-πλώσας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.47)}}: _perfect_ παρα-πέπλωκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.99}}; πέπλωκα occurs {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 532, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 878 (_paratragoedia_).}}—Only εε and εει are contracted in _Attic dialect_ (πλέει is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.28, and πλέετε variant in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.37). [8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses πλέων}} as _monosyllable_, πλέων ἐπὶ οἴνοπα πόντον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.183]}}:—**sail, go by sea,** Λακεδαίμονος ἐξ ἐρατεινῆς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.444; Ἰλιόθεν 14.251; ἐπὶ Κέρκυραν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.53; εὐθὺ Λέσβου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.2.11}}; π. ἐπὶ [σῖτον] to fetch it, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Oec.” 20.27; ἐπί τι “IG” 12.105.9}}; μετὰ [νάκος] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.69; εἰς Ἐρέτριαν ἐπ᾽ ἄνδρας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 240b; more fully, ἐνὶ πόντῳ νηῒ θοῇ πλείοντες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.368; νηῒ.. πλέων ἐπὶ οἴνοπα πόντον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.88; πλέεν.. ποντοπορεύων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.278; ἐν τῇ νηΐ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 341d}}; ἐν τῇ θαλάττῃ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 346b; ἐπλέομεν βορέῃ ἀνέμῳ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.253; αὔρᾳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 691 (Lyric poetry): with _accusative_ _cognate_, πλεῖθ᾽ ὑγρὰ κέλευθα}} **sail** the watery ways, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.71; πλεῖν τὴν θάλατταν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.137, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.19, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.20, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 100:}} —_passive_, τὸ πεπλευσμένον [πέλαγος] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.16, compare 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 71.3; πλεῖται ἡ θάλασσα 1st c.AD: Musonius Philosophus “fragment” 18 B p.104 H.; also π. στόλον τόνδε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1038; τοῦ πλοῦ τοῦ πεπλευσμένου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.12: _metaphorically_, πλεῖν ὑφειμένῃ δοκεῖ,compare ὑφίημι 111: proverbial, ὁ μὴ πεπλευκὼς οὐδὲν ἑόρακεν κακόν 3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 22}}; ἐπὶ γῆς μὴ πλεῖν when on land do not **be at sea,** i.e. avoid the hazards of tax-farming, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus cited in 2nd-3rd c.AD: Clemens Alexandrinus Theologus “Stromateis” 5.5.28.}}
**II** of ships, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.360; νέας ἄμεινον πλεούσας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.10, etc.; ὑπὸ τριήρους.. εὖ πλεούσης ἐπεδιώκοντο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.23; ἡ ναῦς ἄριστά μοι ἔπλει 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 21.6; ἔφευγε ταῖς ναυσὶν εὖ πλεούσαις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.6.16; τριήρης ταχὺ π. [prev. author] “Oec.” 8.8.}}
**II.2** of other things, **swim, float,** τεύχεα καλὰ.. πλῶον καὶ νέκυες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.302; δένδρεα.., τά οἱ πλώοιεν ἐλαφρῶς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.240; [νῆσος] πλέουσα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.156.}}
**II.3** **to be conveyed by sea,** [σκῦλα] πλέοντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.114.}}
**II.4** _metaphorically_, ταύτης ἔπι πλέοντες ὀρθῆς while [the ship of] our country bearing us is on an even keel, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 190}}; οὐδ᾽ ὅπως ὀρθὴ πλεύσεται (i.e. ἡ πόλις) προείδετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.250; πάντα ἡμῖν κατ᾽ ὀρθὸν πλεῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 813d; θεοῦ θέλοντος κἂν ἐπὶ ῥιπὸς πλέοις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 397; also κέρδους ἕκατι κἂν ἐπὶ ῥιπὸς πλέοι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 699. (With πλε (ϝ) -, πλευ-}} cf. Sanskrit _plávate_ 'float', 'swim', Latin _pluit;_ with πλω- cf. Gothic _flōdus_ 'river', 'flood', O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **flćwan** 'flow'.)

4127:
** πληγ-ή**,
_Doric dialect_ **πλᾱγά**, ἡ, (πλήσσω) **blow, stroke,** πεπληγὼν πληγῇσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.264, etc.; πᾶν ἑρπετὸν πληγῇ νέμεται 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 11, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 109b, 3rd c.BC: Erasistratus Medicus cited in Pseudo-Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus “Ther.” 18; ἡ π. τοῦ τραύματος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 877b}}: frequently joined with Verbs of _cognate_ _signification_, πέπληγμαι καιρίαν πληγήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1343; τύπτει τὰς ἴσας πληγὰς ἐμοί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 636}}; τύπτεσθαι τῇ δημοσίᾳ μάστιγι ν πληγάς Legal cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.139; πολλὰς πληγὰς μαστιγούσθω 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 914b (but in such phrases πληγήν or πληγάς}} is frequently omitted, τρίτην ἐπενδίδωμι {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1386; τυπτόμενος πολλάς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 972, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.197; ὀλίγας παῖσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.12; μαστιγωθεὶς ὁπόσας ἂν δόξῃ τοῖς δικασταῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 854d, compare 879e, NT.2Cor.11.24): the person struck is said πληγὰς λαβεῖν, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 673; ὑπὸ τῶν ῥαβδούχων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.50, etc.; πληγῶν δεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 493; πληγὴν ἔχω 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 72; ὑπὸ τὴν π. τοῦ ἀκοντίου ὑπελθεῖν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.4}}; καιρίῃ (i.e. πληγῇ) τετύφθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.64; πληγὰς ὑπομένειν 4th c.BC: Aristopho Comicus 4.6; εἰληφέναι καὶ δεδωκέναι πληγάς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.14}}; π. ἐμβαλεῖν, ἐντείνειν τινί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.11, 2.4.11, etc.; πατάξαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 527d; ἐντρίβειν τινί 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ind.” 25, compare “Somn.” 14; προστρίβεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 5; τὰς ἐξ ἀνθρώπων πληγὰς μαστιγοῦν τινα 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.59; πληγὴν ἐπὶ πληγῇ φέρειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.33.6; π. παρὰ πληγήν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 643}}; πληγαῖς ζημιοῦν, κολάζειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.74, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 762c, etc.; δίκη ὕβρεως ἢ πληγῶν “PHal.” 1.115 (3rd c.BC)}}; πληγῆς ἄρχειν strike the first **blow,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.2.2}}; τὰς π. στέγειν, of the shell of a tortoise, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1295.}}
**2** **stroke by lightning,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 857 (_plural_)}}; πλαγαὶ σιδάρου **strokes** of axe or sword, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.246, “O.” 10(11).37; κλυδωνίου.. πληγαῖς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 796}}; στέρνων πλαγαί **beating** of breasts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 90 (_anapaest meter_)}}; π. τῶν ὀδόντων **strokes** from boars' tusks, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 10.5}}; **spearing** of fish, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 824 (_plural_)}}; of pig-**sticking,** οἱ κάπροι οἱ πρὸς τὴν π... ὠθούμενοι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Euthd.” 294d}}: in _singular_, **fight with clubs,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.63.}}
**3** **stroke** or **impression** on the ears or eyes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 67b, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.490c, etc.; αἱ νοήσεις τύποι ἔσονται· εἰ δὲ τοῦτο, καὶ ἐπακτοὶ καὶ πληγαί 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.5.1.}}
**4** **impact** of bodies, atoms, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 1, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 308, “Placita Philosophorum” 1.4.2, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.6.19.}}
**5** **beat** of the pulse, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 9.464.}}
**6** _metaphorically_, **blow, stroke of calamity,** especially in war, ἐν μιᾷ π. κατέφθαρται.. ὄλβος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 251, compare “Hellenica Oxyrhynchia” 16.2; ἐν πληγαῖς ὄντες [prev. passage]; πληγὴν ὑπήνεγκεν ἡ πόλις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1270a33; πληγῇ περιπεπτωκέναι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 14.9.6; πληγαὶ βιότου 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 933 (_anapaest meter_)}}; π. Διός α heaven-sent **plague,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ag.” 367 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 137 (_anapaest meter_)}}; μὴ 'κ θεοῦ π. τις ἥκει{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 279; δμαθέντες πλαγαῖσι ποντίαισιν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 908 (Lyric poetry)}}; of the ten **plagues** of Egypt, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 5.9.4.}}

4128:
** πλῆθος**, εος, τό,
_Doric dialect_ and {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}} **πλῆθος** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 84.8, +others (Argive, found in Crete, 5th c.BC), “IG” 5(2).6.20 (Tegea, 4th c.BC), etc.}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **πλεῖθος** {{Refs|[prev. work]7.3171.46 (Orchom. _Boeotian dialect_)}}; pseudo-_Doric dialect_ and pseudo-_Aeolic dialect_ **πλᾶθος** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“GDI” 5176.21 (from Crete), “IGRom.” 4.1302.18 (Cyme, 1st c.BC/ 1st c.AD), Hippodamus Pythagoreus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.1.93: (πλήθω}} see at {πίμπλημι}):—**great number, multitude,** especially of people, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.330, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.49, etc.}}; στρατοῦ π., _periphrastic_ for στρατὸς πολύς, {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.73}}; ὡς πλήθει for the **mass** of men, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 389d.}}
**2** τὸ π. the **greater number,** the **mass, main body,** τὸ π. τοῦ στρατοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.82, compare 5.92}}; τὸ π. τῆς ψυχῆς the **largest part** of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 689a}}: as Noun of Multitude with _plural_ Verb, Ἀθηναίων τὸ π. οἴονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.20}}; τὸ π. ἐψηφίσαντο πολεμεῖν **the majority,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 125, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.20}}; τῷ π. by **a majority,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Berl.Sitzb. 1927.8 (_Locrian dialect_, 5th c.BC)}}: hence, **people, population,** σμικρὸν τὸ π. τῆσδε γῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 715.}}
**2.b** **the commons,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.9, etc.; ἡ τοῦ π. ἀρχή, δημοκρατία τοὔνομα κληθεῖσα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 291d; ἐς τὸ π. φέρειν τὸ κράτος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.81}}: frequently of the popular **assembly,** τὸ ὑμέτερον π., τὸ π. τὸ ὑμέτερον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.42, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 31c}}; Ἐρυθραίων τῷ π., Ἀθηναίων τοῦ π., {{Refs|“IG” 12.10.21, 22}}; = Latin _plebs,_ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.15.11, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.25, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.71}}; also, **association, corporation,** or **guild,** τὸ π. τὸ Ἁλιαδᾶν {{Refs|“IG” 12(1).155.6, 156.5 (from Rhodes)}}; τὸ τῶν Πανιαστῶν π. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IGRom.” 4.1680 (Pergam.); τὸ π. τῶν ἱερέων “OGI” 56.24 (Canopus, 3rd c.BC)}}; π. τῶν ἁλιέων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 5.498.2 (3rd c.BC)}}; τὸ π. τῶν μαχαιροφόρων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 737 (Memphis, 2nd c.BC)}}; opposed to αἱ ἀρχαί, οἱ ὀλίγοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.84}}; but also, **populace, mob,** opposed to δῆμος (commons), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 2.18, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.10: also in _plural_, πείθειν τὰ π.}} the **masses,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 452e, compare “Sph.” 268b; ὃ πᾶσι.. σωτήριον, μάλιστα δὲ τοῖς π. πρὸς τοὺς τυράννους 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.24; φιλόσοφον.. π. ἀδύνατον εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 494a.}}
**II** **quantity** or **number,** πόσον π. ἦν νεῶν Ἑλληνίδω; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 334; τῆς σῆς δυνάμεως τί φῂς π. εἶνα; 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.6; ὅμιλος πλήθει φοβερώτατος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.98; ἰσχύϊ καὶ πλήθει προέχων [prev. author] 3.74; τῷ π. αὐτῶν καταπλαγέντες [prev. author] 4.10; πλήθεϊ πολλοί 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.11, compare 6.44; σὺν πλήθει χερῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 123; πλήθει παρόντες}}**in force,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.22: _absolutely_ in _accusative_, κόσοι πλῆθος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.153; πόσοι τὸ π.; 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 17.1; ἐρέται.. π. ἀνάριθμοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 40 (_anapaest meter_); π. ὡς δισχίλιοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.2.2; ἄπειρα τὸ π. [prev. author] “Mem.” 1.1.14; ἄπειρα καὶ π. καὶ σμικρότητα 5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 1; π. τι πάμπολυ φθειρῶν “IG” 42(1).122.45, compare 32 (Epid., 4th c.BC).}}
**III** **magnitude, size,** or **extent,** [ὄρος] πλήθεϊ μέγιστον καὶ μεγάθεϊ ὑψηλότατον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.203}}; πεδίον πλῆθος ἄπειρον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 204; ἡ ἐρῆμος.. ἐοῦσα πλῆθος ἑπτὰ ἡμερέων ὁδοῦ [prev. author] 4.123; π. χώρας καὶ ἀνθρώπων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.9.}}
**III.2** in _Attic dialect_, of **quantity** or **amount,** διὰ π. τῆς ζημίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.70; χρημάτων π. [prev. author] 1.9; διὰ πλῆθος οὐσίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 591e, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1279a19; ταῦτα οὐδέν ἐστι πλήθει οὐδὲ μεγέθει πρὸς ἐκεῖνα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 614a; μετὰ πλήθους ἱδρῶτος}}**multa sudans,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ti.” 84e; τὸ π. τοῦ ῥεύματος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.75.5}}; τὸ παρακείμενον π. the **amount** entered against each, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Ostr.Bodl.” i 252 (2nd c.BC)}}; of money, τὸ ἴσον π. {{Refs|“TAM” 2.526 (from Pinara)}}: in _plural_, **quantities,** ἐμβρύων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 326; θαυμαστὸν ὅσ᾽ ἐστ᾽ ἀγαθῶν π. 4th c.BC: Mnesimachus Comicus 4.51 (_anapaest meter_); οἰκοδομημάτων πλήθεσι ἢ μεγέθεσι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 52.30, compare 10.}}
**III.3** **more than enough,** π. φέρειν {{LXX|LXX.Exo.36.5.}}
**III.4** **plurality,** opposed to ἕν, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 45.}}
**IV** of Time, **length,** χρόνου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 158d, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.180; π. ἐτῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 855; πλήθει πολλῶν μηνῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 722 (Lyric poetry).}}
**V** with Preps., or _adverbs_, ἐς π. **in great numbers,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.14}}; κατὰ πλῆθος a **large number** at a time, {{Refs|“IG” 12.6.112; ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ π.}}**usually, mostly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 275b; ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ π. εἰπεῖν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 786a35; κατὰ π. 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.67.}}

4129:
** πληθ-ύνω**,
_future_ **-ῠνῶ**{{NT|NT.2Cor.9.10}}: _aorist_ ἐπλήθυνα {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 99:}} —causal of πληθύω, **increase, multiply,** {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Gen.17.2, +others, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.496, NT.2Cor.[same place], NT.Heb.6.14}}:—_passive_, ταῖς γυναιξὶ τὸ γάλα πληθύνεται **abounds,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 587b20.}}
**2** **make multiple, 'plurify',** κατὰ ἀναλογίαν [τὸ ἡνωμένον] ἐπληθύναμεν τῶν κατωτέρω πεπληθυσμένων τὸν πρῶτον πληθυσμόν Dam.[same place]; θεοὶ -όμενοι μὲν ἐν τῷ κόσμῳ, περὶ αὐτὸν δὲ ἑνοειδῶς ὄντες {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 4.143b, compare “Or.” 7.222a.}}
**3** _intransitive_, see at {πληθύω} {{Refs|11.}}
**4** **use the plural,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” “Oxy.” 1087.34.}}
**II** _passive_, **to be in the majority, prevail,** δήμου.. χεὶρ ὅπῃ πληθύνεται (codex _middle_ πληθύεται) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 604: with}}_infinitive_, ταύτην ἐπαινεῖν.. πληθύνομαι I **follow the majority,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ag.” 1370}}: _perfect_ πεπλήθυνται {{LXX|LXX.Gen.18.20.}}
**II.2** Grammars, [τὸ ἀπαρέμφατον] οὐ πληθύνεται the infinitive **has** no **plural,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 31.25.}}

4130:
** πλήθ-ω**,
_poetry_ _perfect_ (in _present_ sense) πέπληθα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 29, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 7.84, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22. 38, etc.}}: _pluperfect_ ἐπεπλήθει {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.271:}} — _intransitive_ form of πίμπλημι, mostly in _present_ _participle_, **to be full,** πλήθει.. νεκύων ἐρατεινὰ ῥέεθρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.218; ναῦς.. ἀνδρῶν πληθούσας 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 142.7; θάλασσα.. ναυαγίων πλήθουσα καὶ φόνου 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 420, compare 272}}; χεῖρας κρεῶν πλήθοντες **having** them **full of..,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ag.” 1220; τὸ στεγύλλιον πέπληθε καλῶν ἔργων 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus [same place]: later with _dative_, κρήνην.. ὕδατι πεπληθυῖαν 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus [same place]; δόνακι πλήθοντα λιπὼν ῥόον 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 166, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 6.63 (6th c.AD: Damocharis Epigrammaticus); πεπληθότα λύθρῳ 3rd c.BC: Maiistas Epicus 25}}; but Ἄναυρος ὄμβρῳ χειμερίῳ πλήθων **swollen with** winter's rain, ὄμβρῳ being _dative_ of cause, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 478: _absolutely_, of rivers, ποταμῷ πλήθοντι ἐοικώς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.87; ὡς δ᾽ ὁπότε πλήθων ποταμὸς πεδίονδε κάτεισι 11.492}}; of the **full** moon, σελήνη πλήθουσα [{{Refs 7th c.BC+|18.484, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 3: in _Attic dialect_ Prose only in the phrases ἀγορᾶς πληθούσης, ἐν ἀγορᾷ πληθούσῃ, etc., see at {ἀγορά} IV: πληθούσης ἀγορᾶς}}] rarely = in a **full** assembly, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 257.14 (Delph., 4th c.BC).}}
**II** _transitive_ only in later Poets, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 14.7, 3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 1.126, 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 6.345}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.1392, 4.564, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.232 (1st c.AD(?): Macedonius Epigrammaticus), 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 14.607; πάσαις ἀρεταῖς πληθόμενον κραδίην “BCH” 50.44 (Thespiae, 4th c.AD).}}
** πίμπλημι**,
in _present_ and _imperfect_ formed like{ἵστημι}; _Aeolic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ πίμπλεισι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 25.3}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _subjunctive_ πιμπλῇσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 301}}; _imperative_ πίμπλη conjecture in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Xenarchus Comicus 3, ἐμ-πίμπλη 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1310}}; _participle_ πιμπλάς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 586b; but _nominative_ _plural_ _feminine_ πιμπλεῖσαι 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 880}}: _imperfect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐπίμπλασαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.10: other tenses from πλήθω (which in _present_ and _imperfect_ is _intransitive_)}}: _future_ πλήσω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 692, (ἀνα-) 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.302}}: _aorist_ ἔπλησα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 905, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ πλῆσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.60, +others: _perfect_ πέπληκα (ἐμ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 23e, “Ly.” 204d:—_middle_, _future_ πλήσομαι (ἐμ-) 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 1121, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Συριακή” 7}}:_aorist_ ἐπλησάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.224, etc.}}:—_passive_, _future_ πλησθήσομαι {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Luke.1.15, 2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 5.5, 4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Orationes” 23.14, (ἐμ-) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 664, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.69; also πεπλήσομαι 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 1.16}}: _aorist_ ἐπλήσθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.156, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ πλῆσθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|17.211, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.705}}: _perfect_ πέπλησμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 60, (ἐμ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 518b}}, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ πεπλήαται {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 31 A, πέπληνται 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φυσῶν” 8; also shortened form πλῆνται 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 1.13:}}_aorist 2_ ἐπλήμην, _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ and _plural_ πλῆτο, πλῆντο, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.499, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.57, 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 12.1; ἐν-έπλητο 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 911, 1304; _imperative_ ἔμ-πλησο [prev. work] 603}}; _optative_ ἐμ-πλῄμην, -ῇτο, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ach.” 236, “Lys.” 235}}; _participle_ ἐμ-πλήμενος {{Refs|[prev. author] “V.” 424, 984, etc.}} —In the _compound_ ἐμπίμπλημι (which see; more frequently in Prose) the second μ is sometimes dropped, as ἐμπίπλημ; but returns with the augment, as in ἐνεπίμπλασα; compare πίμπρημι:—**fill**, with _genitive_ _of things_, **fill full of**.., τράπεζαν ἀμβροσίης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.93; πήρην σίτου καὶ κρειῶν 17.411}}; π. τινὰ μένεος, θάρσευς φρένας, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.60, 17.573; καλάμης τὸ πλοῖον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.194; π. κρητῆρα κακῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1398; πίμπλημ᾽ ὄμμα δακρύων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 906}}; δακρύων ἔπλησεν ἐμέ **filled** me **full** of tears, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 368}}: with _dative_ _of things_, **fill with**.., ἰαχῇ τε φόβῳ τε πάσας πλῆσαν ὁδούς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.374; δακρύοις Ἑλλάδ᾽ ἅπασαν ἔπλησεν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1363 (Lyric poetry)}}; simply, **fill**, ἰχθύες.. πιμπλᾶσι μυχούς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.23, compare 14.35, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 411, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 494a; π. μέλος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 57.4: _absolutely_, πίμπλη σὺ μὲν ἐμοί (i.e. τὴν κύλικα) 4th c.BC: Xenarchus Comicus 3.}}
**2** **fill full, satisfy, glut**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 146, etc.}}
**3** **fill, discharge** an office, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 361 (uncertain).}}
**II** _middle_, **fill for oneself**, or **what is one's own**, πλησάμενος οἴνοιο δέπας **having filled himself** a cup of wine, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.224, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.112, etc.}}; π. νῆας **load** ships, {{Refs|[prev. work] 87}}; π... θυμὸν ἐδητύος ἠδὲ ποτῆτος **fill up, satiate** one's desire with..,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|17.603; ματρόθεν δυσώνυμα λέκτρ᾽ ἐπλήσω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 528 (Lyric poetry)}}; πεδία πίμπλασθ᾽ ἁρμάτων **fill** the plain **full of your** chariots, variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 522.}}
**III** _passive_, **to be filled, be full of**, τῶν.. ἐπλήσθη πεδίον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.156; πλῆτο ῥόος.. ἵππων τε καὶ ἀνδρῶν 21.16; ὄσσε δακρυόφι πλῆσθεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.705, etc.; μένεος.. φρένες.. πίμπλαντο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.104; πλῆσθεν.. μέλε᾽ ἐντὸς ἀλκῆς 17.211}}; ἀλκῆς πλῆτο φρένας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|.. [prev. work] 499; ἀϋτῆς.. ἐπλήσθη στέγος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 646: rarely with _dative_, λέκτρα δ᾽ ἀνδρῶν πόθῳ πίμπλαται δακρύμασι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 134 (Lyric poetry); δάκρυσι τὸ στράτευμα πλησθέν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.75.}}
**III.2** **to be filled, satisfied, have enough of** a thing, αἱμάτων γένυσιν πλησθῆναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 121}}; π. τῆς νόσου ξυνουσίᾳ **to be wearied of it by** being with it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 520; ἡδονῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 442a, etc.}}
**III.3** of females, **become pregnant**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 576b29, 578b32}}. (Cf. Sanskrit_piparti_ 'fill', **pūrṇá**-, Latin _plenus_, Gothic _fulls_, etc. 'full'.)

4132:
** πλήμῡρ-α**, ἡ,
={πλημυρίς}, **flood-tide, flood,** {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 29, LXX.Job.40.18(23), “Placita Philosophorum” 3.17.1, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.71, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.291 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus), NT.Luke.6.48, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 3, etc.}}; **inundation** of the Nile, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1409.17 (3rd c.AD)}}: _metaphorically_, λόγου {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.175, compare 690 (_plural_); κακῶν 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 11.157.}}
**2** in Medicine texts, **accumulation, excessive flow,** Lycusap.{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 8.25.39, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 2.9, “SD” 2.2.}}

4133:
** πλήν**,
_Doric dialect_and _Aeolic dialect_ **πλάν** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 56.3(Argos, 5th c.BC), 421.7(Thermum, 3rd c.BC), “IG” 9(1).333.4 (_Locrian dialect_, 5th c.BC), “Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.158 (from Cyrene), “Schwyzer” 633.16, 19 (Eresus, 2nd-1st c.BC)}}: _preposition_ with _genitive_, **except, save,** πάντων Φαιήκων πλήν γ᾽ αὐτοῦ Λαοδάμαντος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.207; τίς ἔτλη.. π. Ἡρακλῆο; 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 74, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 914, “IG” 12.6.108, etc.: with γε, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 909, etc.}}; ὑπεγγύους π. θανάτου liable to any punishment **short of** death, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.71}}; ἐπιτρέψαι περὶ σφῶν αὐτῶν π. θανάτου **save in respect of** death, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.54}}; σκυλεύειν τοὺς τελευτήσαντας π. ὅπλων of anything **save** their arms, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 469c}}; διαρπάσαι.. ἐπέτρεψε πλὴν ἀνδραπόδων to carry off all plunder **save** slaves, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.4.27.}}
**2** later, **besides, in addition to,** {{LXX|LXX.Deu.18.8, 29.1.}}
**B** _conjunction_:
**B.I** with single words and phrases, especially when a _negative_ precedes, οὐκ ἆρ᾽ Ἀχαιοῖς ἄνδρες εἰσὶ π. ὅδ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1238; οὐκ οἶδα π. ἕν [prev. author] “OC” 1161, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 752; οὐ κάτοιδα π. ἐπὶ σμικρὸν φράσαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 414, compare 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 13}}: after a question implying a _negative_, τί σοι πέπρακται π.τεύχειν κακ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 125, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 646: after πᾶς, etc., τὸ δ᾽ ἄρσεν αἰνῶ πάντα π. γάμου τυχεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 737; παντὶ δῆλον π. ἐμοί 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 529a, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 299, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.19, etc.; νικᾶν.. πανταχοῦ.. ἔφυν π. εἰς σέ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1053 (πᾶς}} is sometimes omitted, θνῄσκουσι [πάντες] π. εἷς τις [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 118; ἀλλ᾽ ἔστι [πᾶσι] πλὴν σοί [prev. work] 370): frequently with ἄλλος (much like{ἤ}), τί οὖν μ᾽ ἄνωγας ἄλλο π. ψευδῆ λέγει; [prev. author] “Ph.” 100, compare “Aj.” 125, “Ant.” 236, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 106, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 334b, etc.}}]: after a _comparative_, ταῦτ᾽ ἐστὶ κρείσσω π. ὑπ᾽ Ἀργείοις πεσεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 231, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Minos” 318e, etc.; πάντα μᾶλλον π. αὐτὸς ἅψασθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.179}}: after a _superlative_, τὸ μέγιστον εἴργται π. αἱ τάξεις τοῦ φόρου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 3.5.}}
**B.II** frequently joined with other Particles:
**B.II.1** π. εἰ, π. ἐάν, ὅταν,
**B.II.1.a** followed by a Verb, π. εἴ τις κωμῳδοποιὸς τυγχάνει ὤν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 18d, compare “Tht.” 177d, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.18, 10.39, etc.; π. ὅταν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 260, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 293, etc.; π. εἰ ἄρα μή 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 7.3.8; π. ἐὰν μὴ ἐξέλθῃ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 554b2.}}
**B.II.1.b** with Verb omitted, οὐδεὶς οἶδεν.., π. εἴ τις ἄρ᾽ ὄρνις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 601, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.2.21, etc.}}; π. εἰ μή, after a _negative_, οὐδὲν προσδεησόμεθα, π. εἰ μὴ πάρεργόν τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 286d (variant); οὐδὲν ἂν πάθοι.., π. εἰ μή που κατὰ συμβεβηκός 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 323b26; οὐ δεῖ.. τοῦτο.. κινεῖσθαι, π. εἰ μὴ κατὰ σ. [prev. author] “de An.” 406b8, compare “APr.” 43a39, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 1.10.6, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.74.—The pleonastic phrase π. εἰ μή is censured by 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sol.” 7.}}
**B.II.2** πλὴν ἤ (where ἤ adds nothing to the sense; πλὴν εἰ is a common variant), οὐκ ἄλλῳ π. ἢ Προδίκῳ [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 361, compare 734; οὐδὲν κάκιον.., πλὴν ἄρ᾽ ἢ γυναῖκες [prev. author] “Th.” 532, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.111, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 42a (variant{π. εἰ})}}]; also οὐ τὸν ἄνθρωπον ὑγιάζει π. ἀλλ᾽ ἢ.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 981a18 (variant).}}
**B.II.3** π. οὐ **only** not, πάντες προσδέχονται, π. οὐχ οἱ τύραννοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 1.18 (variant cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.144d), compare “Lac.” 15.6, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.45, 56.23, “Berl.Sitzb.” [prev. cited]}}
**B.II.4** π. ὅτι **except** that.., **save** that.., καίτοι τί διαφέρουσιν ἡμῶν ἐκεῖνοι, π. ὅτι ψηφίσματ᾽ οὐ γράφουσι; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1429; π. ἢ ὅτι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.189}}; πλήν γε ὅτι, πλήν γε δὴ ὅτι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 183a, “Phd.” 57b}}; after ὁμοίως, τὰ αὐτά, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pelopidas” 4, 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 1.56.}}
**B.II.5** π. ὅσον **except** or **save** so far as.., παρήκουσι παρὰ πᾶσαν [τὴν Λιβύην].., π. ὅσον Ἕλληνες.. ἔχουσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.32, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.23; π. ὅσα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 456a; π. καθόσον 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 72.19; π. καθόσον εἰ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88 codices}}
**B.II.5.b** without a Verb, πάντων ἐρήμους, π. ὅσον τὸ σὸν μέρος **save** so far as thou art concerned, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1509}}; τοὺς πολλοὺς ἀπέκτειναν π. ὅσον ἐκ τριῶν νεῶν οὓς ἐζώγρησαν **except** only.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 670a, 856d; ἀληθευτικός, π. ὅσα μὴ δι᾽ εἰρωνείαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1124b30.}}
**B.III** introducing a clause, mostly preceded by οὐδείς, πᾶς, ἄλλος, **save that,** νῦν δ᾽ οὐδεμία πάρεστιν.., π. ἥ γ᾽ ἐμὴ κωμῆτις ἥδ᾽ ἐξέρχεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 5, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 41, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.8.20, etc.; οὐκ ἀντεξῄεσαν π. ἕως ἀκροβολισμοῦ 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.18.2}}: without any such word preceding, **only, albeit,** ἀπέπεμπε κήρυκας ἐς τὴν Ἑλλάδα, π. οὔτε ἐς Ἀθήνας οὔτε ἐς Λακεδαίμονα ἀπέπεμπε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.32, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1643, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.70, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 328e, etc.}}
**B.III.2** to break off and pass to another subject, **only, however,** π. γνώριζε ἄτοπος ὤν {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 454.10 (3rd c.BC),compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.69.14, 2.17.1, NT.1Cor.11.11, 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 8, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 34, etc.}}: in late Prose, π. ἀλλά {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Pyrrh.” 5, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Prom.” 20, “DMort.” 13.3, 20.4, etc.; π. ἀλλά γε [prev. author] “Rh.Pr.” 24.}}
**B.III.3** simply for δέ, **but,** πολλὴν στρατιὰν ἀθροίσας, π. ἄπειρον μάχης {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 3.4.1; δυστυχῶν μέν, π. ἀλλ᾽ Ἑλλήνων 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 6.7. (Cogn. with πλησίος, πελάζω, compare ἔμπλην.)}}

4134:
** πλήρης**, ες,
_genitive_ εος, _contraction_ ους: _comparative_ **-έστερος**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 175d}}: _superlative_ **-έστατος**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1087 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.: (πίμ-πλη-μι):
**I** with _genitive_, **full of,** ἄστυ π. οἰκιέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.180; φορμοὶ ψάμμου π. [prev. author] 8.71; ὁμίχλα.. π. δακρύων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 145 (Lyric poetry); πλῆρες ἄτης στέγος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 307; ποταμὸς π. ἰχθύων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.9; π. μέλιτος τὸ καλὸν στόμα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.146; ταῦτα πάσης ἀλογίας π. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.15.6}}; of persons, κενῶν δοξασμάτων π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 384; αἰδοῦς π. ψυχή 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 310d.}}
**I.2** **infected by,** π. ὑπ᾽ οἰωνῶν τε καὶ κυνῶν βορᾶς **polluted** by birds and dogs with meat (torn from the body of Polynices), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1017; νόσου [prev. work] 1052.}}
**I.3** **satisfied, satiated,** with _genitive_, π. ἔχοντι θυμὸν ὧν χρῄζεις {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 778: with}}_participle_, θηεύμενοι ἔωσι π. they should have gazed their **fill,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.146.}}
**II** less frequently with _dative_, **filled with,** Ἕλλησι βαρβάροις θ᾽ ὁμοῦ π. πόλεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 19.}}
**III** _absolutely_, **full,** of a swollen stream, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.92}}; of the moon, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 53, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.106; π. γαστήρ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 848; ὄγκος γαστρός “Tragica Adespota” 186}}; κρατῆρες, δέπας, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 221, “Hec.” 527, etc.; κεχόρτασμαι.. οὐ κακῶς, ἀλλ᾽ εἰμὶ π. 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 30, compare 53}}; **full of people,** ἐπειδὰν π. ᾖ τὸ θέατρον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.82; π. τὸ βαλανεῖον ποιεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1054; εἰ π. τύχοι ὁ δῆμος ὤν [prev. author] “Ec.” 95, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 2.17; ἡ βουλὴ ἐπειδὴ ἦν π. 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.112; ἐπειδὰν πάντα π. ᾖ τὰ δικαστήρια 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 66.1, compare “IG” 12.41.5; ἐπειδὴ π. αὐτοῖς ἦσαν αἱ νῆες}}**fully manned,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.29, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.1.28, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.32}}; of persons, **satisfied, gorged,** opposed to κενός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 11.18, etc.}}; τὸ π., opposed to τὸ κενόν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Leucippus Philosophus and 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 985b5.}}
**III.2** **full, complete,** ἐπειρώτων.. εἰ λελάβηκε πλήρεα.. τὰ ἀκροθίνια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.122; ὡς ἂν τὴν χάριν πλήρη λάβω 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1411, compare “PGiss.” 40ii6 (3rd c.AD)}}; -εστάτη οἰκειότης **fullest** intimacy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 40; φέρων π. τὸν μισθόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.5.5}}; -εστάτῳ δικαίῳ, = Latin _optimo jure,_ {{Refs 4th c.AD+|PFlor. 66.3 (4th c.AD)}}; of numbers or periods of Time, τέσσερα ἔτεα π. four **full** years, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.20.}}
**III.3** **solid, whole,** of a voting-pebble (ψῆφος), opposed to τετρυπημένος, τρυπητός, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.79, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 68.2, 69.1; π. ὁπλαί 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.191; αὔλημα [prev. author] 4.73; ἄγαλμα.. ἐποίησε πλῆρες 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 9.12.4.}}
**III.4** of sound, **full,** πληρέστερον μέλος {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 14.65.}}
**III.5** of wine, **full-bodied, with a persistent flavour,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.945}}; of the pulse, {{Refs|[prev. author][prev. work]678}}; of wool, {{Refs|[prev. author][prev. work]672.}}
**III.6** ἐκ πλήρους **fully,** ποιεῖν τὰ δίκαια {{Refs|“IG” 22.1343.21}}; **in full,** τὰ ἐκφόρια κομίσασθαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 105.47 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**IV** πλήρης is used _indeclinable_ in later Greek, especially of payments **in full,** Wilcken {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Chr.” 499.9 (2nd-3rd c.AD), etc.}}; frequently variant in {{LXX|LXX, LXX.Gen.27.27, LXX.Num.7.20, LXX.Job.21.24, +others}}
**V** _adverb_ πλήρως **in full,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|Sammelb. 4652.2 (4th c.AD)}}: _superlative_ **-έστατα**{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “Protrepticus” 21.κγ᾽.}}

4135:
** πληροφορ-έω**,
**bring full measure: satisfy fully,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PAmh. 2.66.42 (2nd c.AD), “PMag.Lond.” 121.910}}; especially **assure,** τινὰ ὅρκοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Ctesias Historicus “fragment” 29.39, compare “Cod Just.” 1.1.7.23.}}
**2** **fulfil,** τὴν διακονίαν {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.2Tim.4.5; τὸ πατρῷον συνάλλαγμα “Arch.Papyrus” 5.383 (1st-2nd c.AD)}}:—_passive_, **to be fulfilled,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Luke.1.1, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 43.18.}}
**3** **pay in full,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|POxy. 1473.8 (_passive_, 3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**II** _passive_, of persons, **have full satisfaction, to be fully assured,** ὅτι.. {{NT|NT.Rom.4.21: _absolutely_, [prev. work] 14.5.}}
**II.2** π. τοῦ ποιῆσαι **to be fully bent on** doing, {{LXX|LXX.Eccl.8.11.}}

4136:
** πληροφορ-ία**, ἡ,
**fullness of assurance, certainty,** {{NT|NT.1Thes.1.5, NT.Col.2.2, NT.Heb.6.11.}}
**II** **assurance given,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-6th c.AD: Codex Justinianus 1.1.5.4.}}

4137:
** πληρ-όω**,
_3rd.pers._ _plural_ _imperfect_ ἐπληροῦσαν cited by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 2.64 H. from 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 574}}: _future_ -ώσω: _perfect_ πεπλήρωκα, _Aeolic dialect_ _participle_ πεπληρώκων {{Refs|“IG” 12(2).243.9 (Mytil.):}} —_middle_, _future_ πληρώσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(ἐπι-) 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.14 (see. below)}}: _aorist_ ἐπληρωσάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 493e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.56, etc.}}:—_passive_, _future_ **-ωθήσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 175e, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.37}}; _future_ _middle_ in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 3.6, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 17.28, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.560}}:—**make full**:
**I** with _genitive_ _of things_, **fill full of,** λάρνακας λίθων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.123, etc.}}; κρατῆρα, πίστρα (i.e. οἴνου), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1192, “Cyc.” 29}}:—_passive_, **to be filled full,** τινος **of** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 20, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 550d, etc.; σάλπιγξ βροτείου πνεύματος -ουμένη 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 568; ἀπό τινος 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Sententiae ad intelligibilia ducentes” 32.}}
**I.2** **fill full** of food, **gorge, satiate,** βορᾶς ψυχὴν ἐπλήρουν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1170: _metaphorically_, π. θυμόν}} **glut** one's rage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 324, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1328; τὰς ἐπιθυμίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 494c}}:—_passive_, **to be filled full of, satisfied,** δαιτὸς -ωθείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 213.3; Αἴγυπτος ἁγνοῦ νάματος -ουμένη 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 300.6}}; φόβου, ἐλπίδος, etc., {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 865e, “R.” 494c, etc.; also οὐ πληρωθήσεται οὖς ἀπὸ ἀκροάματος LXX.Eccl.1.8.}}
**I.3** π. τὴν χεῖρά τινος **consecrate,**{{LXX|LXX.Exo.32.29, +others, LXX.Judg.17.5, 12.}}
**II** rarely with _dative_, **fill with,** πεύκαισιν.. χέρας πληροῦντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 373 (Lyric poetry):}} —_passive_, πνεύμασιν -ούμενοι **filled with** breath, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 464; πεπληρωμένους πάσῃ ἀδικίᾳ NT.Rom.1.29, cf. NT.2Cor.7.4.}}
**III** without any modal case, π. νέας **man** ships, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.171, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.29 (_active_ and _passive_) (in full πεντηκόντερον π. ἀνδρῶν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.41); π. ναυτικόν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.52}}; πληροῦτε θωρακεῖα **man** the breastworks, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 32}}:—_middle_, τριήρη πληρωσάμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.48, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.56, etc.; in full, ἐπληρωσάμην τὴν ναῦν ἐρετῶν ἀγαθῶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.15.}}
**III.2** **impregnate,** [τὰ θήλεα] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 574a20, “Metaphorical” 988a6:}} —_passive_ {{Refs|of the female, [prev. passage], “HA” 541a13.}}
**III.3** **make full** or **complete,** τοὺς δέκα μῆνας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.63}}; π. τοὺς χρόνους, τὸν ἐνιαυτόν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 866a, “Ti.” 39d; τὸν τῆς καταδίκης χρόνον “Sammelb.” 4639.5 (3rd c.AD),compare “POxy.” 491.6 (2nd c.AD)}}, etc.:—_middle_, τὰ πάντα ἐν πᾶσι π. {{NT|NT.Eph.1.23}}:— _passive_, of the moon, **to be full,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 871.6; ἵνα.. ᾖ τοι ἀπαρτιλογίη ὑπ᾽ ἐμέο πεπληρωμένη 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.29; πεπλήρωται ὁ καιρός NT.Mark.1.15, etc.: in Mathematics texts, πεπληρώσθω}} **let** the figure **be completed,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mechanica” 854b29.}}
**III.4** π. δικαστήρια **fill** them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.92}}:—_passive_, δικαστήριον πεπληρωμένον ἐκ τούτων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 21.209, compare 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.37; πληρουμένου.. βουλευτηρίου 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 570.}}
**III.5** **render, pay in full,** τροφεῖα πληρώσει χθονί {{Refs|[prev. author] “Th.” 477}}; π. τὴν χρείαν **supply** it, **make** it **good,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.70; πεπλήρωκα τὸν τόκον μέχρι τοῦ Ἐπείφ “POxy.” 114.3 (2nd-3rd c.AD),compare “BGU” 1055.23 (_passive_, 1st c.BC)}}: with _double_ _accusative_, ἵνα πληρώσῃς αὐτοὺς τὴν τιμήν {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PLond.” 2.243.11 (4th c.AD), compare 251.30 (_passive_, 4th c.AD)}}, etc.: _absolutely_, {{Refs|“IG” 14.956.}}
**III.6** **fulfil,** τὸ χρεών (destiny) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 17}}; τὴν ἐπαγγελίαν, τὰς ὑποσχέσεις, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.9.3, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.7.6; π. πᾶσαν ἀρχὴν καὶ λειτουργίαν “IG” 12(5).946.1 (from Tenos), compare 12(2) [prev. cited] (Mytil.), “PFlor.” 382.40 (3rd c.AD), 6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “Mag.” 3.30, +others}}; **execute, perform,** τὰ προσταχθέντα {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 2107.5 (3rd c.AD)}}:—_passive_, λαμπαδηφόρων νόμοι.. διαδοχαῖς πληρούμενοι **fully observed,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 313}}; **to be fulfilled,** of prophecy, {{NT|NT.Matt.1.22, NT.John.13.18.}}
**III.7** ἐς ἄγγος.. βακχίου μέτρημα πληρώσαντες **having poured** wine into the vessel **till it was full,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 954}}:—_passive_, **assemble, muster,** πληρουμένης τῆς ἐκκλησίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 89; ἀρχαί τ᾽ ἐπληροῦντ᾽ εἰς.. βουλευτήρια 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1097 codices; πολλοὶ δ᾽ ἐπληρώθημεν [prev. author] “IT” 306.}}
**III.8** **fill up** a document, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “Mag.” 3.11:}} —_passive_ {{Refs|[prev. work]68.}}
**IV** _intransitive_, ἡ [ὁδὸς] πληροῖ ἐς τὸν ἀριθμὸν τοῦτον the length of road **comes in full** to this number, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.7 (assuming variant).}}

4138:
** πλήρ-ωμα**, ατος, τό,
**that which fills, complement,** κρατήρων πληρώματα, i.e. wine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1051 (Lyric poetry); so κενὸν τόδ᾽ ἄγγος, ἢ στέγει π. τ; [prev. work] 1412}}; χθονὸς π., i.e. men, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Or.” 1642; λίθους τοὺς εἰς τὰ π. τῷ πύργῳ προσάξεται “IG” 22.244.109, compare 90, +others (4th c.BC)}}; τὸ π. τῆς γαστρός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 7, “Mul.” 2.169, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 59}}; of excrement, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 8.35.7.}}
**2** δαιτὸς π. **satiety** of the feast, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 203 (_anapaest meter_)}}; π. τυρῶν **their fill** of cheese, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyc.” 209.}}
**3** of ships, **full number,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.43, 45}}; but, of single ships, **complement, crew,** in _plural_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.4, 12, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.11, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.155, etc.: in _sing._, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.14}}; opposed to ὑπηρεσία (which see), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 21.10; παραλαβὼν τῶν πολιτᾶν ἐπιλέκτους ἐμ π. τρισί “IPE” 12.352.40 (Cherson., 2nd c.BC)}}; π. ἐπίλεκτον {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.47.6: generally, π. παρέχεσθαι πόλεως}} make up **the full number** of citizens, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1267b16, 1284a5, compare 1291a17, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 371e; τῶν φίλων π. ἀθροίσας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 664}}; **gang** of workmen, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 3p.130 (3rd c.BC).}}
**4** of number, **sum,** ὀγδώκοντα ἔτεα ζόης π. μακρότατον προκεῖσθαι {{Refs|80}} years are fixed as life's longest **sum,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.22; τούτων π. τάλαντ᾽ ἐγγὺς δισχίλια γίγνεται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 660}}; **sum, total,** τὸ π. τῶν ἀγαθῶν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 831.14, compare 3rd c.BC: Polystratus Epicureus “Herc.” 346p.81V., 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 2.}}
**5** **piece inserted to fill up, **{{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Matt.9.16, 2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 216.}}
**6** **fullness, full and perfect nature,** {{NT|NT.Rom.11.12}}; τὸ π. τοῦ θεοῦ, τοῦ Χριστοῦ, {{NT|NT.Eph.3.19, 4.13, cf. NT.Col.1.19; τῆς θεότητος [prev. work] 2.9}}; later τὰ π. τῶν θεῶν {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 1.8}}; **the aggregate of properties which constitute the complete nature of a thing, full specification, substance,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 28 bis; φθαρτὰ π. [prev. work] 34; τὰ μέσα π. [prev. work] 35 bis; τὰ ἑκάστου πληρώματα τῆς οὐσίας [prev. work] 14, compare 56, 58}}; πᾶς νοῦς, π. ὢν εἰδῶν {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 177.}}
**7** **reserves** of troops, στρατιῶν {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “Mag.” 3.44 (_plural_).}}
**8** **mass, complex,** ὁ κόσμος π. ἐστι κακίας, ζωῆς, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Corpus Hermeticum” 6.4, 12.15; γενομένη π. ἀρετῶν ἥδε ἡ ψυχή 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.418.}}
**9** **duties of an office,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “Mag.” 3.6, +others}}; **completion of a term of office** (compare 11), {{Refs|[prev. work] 47.}}
**10** **freight, cargo,** νεώς {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.18, compare 465: _metaphorically_, π. εὐτυχίας [prev. work] 547.}}
**II** **filling up, completing,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1213}}; Ζηνὸς ἔχεις κυλίκων π. hast **the task of filling..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 824 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.2** **fulfilment,** νόμου {{NT|NT.Rom.13.10.}}

4139:
** πλήσι-ος**, α, ον,
_Boeotian dialect_ **πλᾱτίος** {{Refs|“Rev.Ét.Gr.” 10.29 (from Thespiae)}}: (πέλας, πελάζω):— **near, close to,** with _genitive_, πλησίοι ἀλλήλων variant in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.249; πλησίαι ἀλλήλων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.71.}}
**2** with _dative_, πλησίοι ἀλλήλοισι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.732, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.149, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 761.}}
**3** _absolutely_, **near, neighbouring,** πλησίαι αἵ γ᾽ ἥσθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.21; uncertain reading in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 195; οἱ π. γύαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 58; τῶν πλησίων ἱερῶν “OGI” 736.5 (from Egypt)}}: _substantive_, **neighbour,** ἰδὼν ἐς π. ἄλλον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.271, etc.; οἱ π. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.152, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 471, etc.}}
**II** _adverb_ πλησίον, _Aeolic dialect_ **πλάσιον** [ᾱ] {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.3, “Supp.” 6.1}}; _Doric dialect_ **πλᾱτίον**: ={πέλας}, **near, hard by,** with _genitive_, τὰ μὲν κατέθεντ᾽ ἐπὶ γαίῃ π. ἀλλήλων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.115, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.14; Σωφροσύνας πλατίον οἰκεῖ 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 101, compare “IG” 42(1).123.15 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; κεῖται στενωποῦ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 366; στῆθι π. πατρός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1076; στρατοπεδεύεσθαι π. τινῶν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.111; ὁρῶ δέ σ᾽ ἤδη τοῦδε π. κακοῦ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1439; π. παρῆσθα κινδύνων ἐμοί [prev. author] “Or.” 1159: with _dative_, σταθεῖσα τῷ τεκόντι π. [prev. author] “IA” 1551; τοῖς πολεμίοις π. προσέρχομαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.234d.}}
**II.2** with the _Article_, ὁ πλησίον (i.e. ὤν) **one's neighbour,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 221, 611, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 996 (_plural_), 5th c.BC: Antipho Sophista 58, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1267a25, etc.; ὁ π. καὶ ὁ γείτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 174b; τοὺς μάλιστα π. ἑαυτῶν [prev. author] “Ap.” 25d}}; _Doric dialect_, ὁ πλατίον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.28, 10.3: with Substantives, ταῖς π. κλίναις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 315d}}; ὁ π. παράδεισος, αἱ π. κῶμαι, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.4.16, 3.4.9, etc.}}
**III** _superlative_ -αίτατος {{Refs|[prev. work] 1.10.5, 7.3.29. _comparative_}} _adverb_ **-αιτέρω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.112}}; **-αίτερον** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.23}}: _superlative_ **-αίτατα**{{Refs|[prev. author] “Vect.” 4.46}}: later _comparative_ **-έστερος**{{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis de Caelo commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 441.14}}. _adverb_ **-έστερον**{{LXX+4th c.AD+|LXX.4Mac.12.3, 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 1.12a.}}—The _adjective_ is _poetry_ and _Ionic dialect_; in _Attic dialect_ Prose only the _adverb_ is found.

4140:
** πλησμον-ή**, ἡ,
**a being filled, satiety,** opposed to ἔνδεια, κένωσις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 571e, “Smp.” 186c}}; especially with food, **repletion, surfeit,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 2.4; οὔτε π. οὔτε μέθη 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.40, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.62K.; ἐς πλησμονάς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1211; ἐν πλησμονῇ τοι Κύπρις, ἐν πεινῶντι δ᾽ οὔ [prev. author] “fragment” 895; ἐσθίειν εἰς π. LXX.Exo.16.3: with _genitive_, τῶν μὲν γὰρ ἄλλων ἐστὶ πάντων π. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 189, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.20; π. ὑγροῦ 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 7.62; τιμῆς τε καὶ νίκης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 586d, etc.; also π. περί τι [prev. author] “Lg.” 837c; π. ἀπό τινος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 33.}}
**II** **abundance,** {{LXX|LXX.Pro.3.10, “Geoponica” 1.10.8 (_plural_).}}

4141:
** πλήσσω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 456, present used by 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and _Attic dialect_ writers only in _compound_ ἐκπλ- (compare πλήγνυμι)}}; _Attic dialect_ **πλήττω** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 224a33}}: _future_ πλήξω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 275, and late Prose, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 5.39, (ἐπι-) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.580, (ἐκ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 436e, (κατα-) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 8.3:}}_aorist_ ἔπληξα, _Epic dialect_ πλῆξα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.266, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 855, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.64, and later Greek, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 4.8.33, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.233f, “BGU” 759.14 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ πλᾶξα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.49}}; never in _Attic dialect_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1579 is spurious) except in compounds ἐκ-, κατα- (which see)}}; in the simple Verb the _future_ and _aorist_ of πατάσσω or παίω are used instead, as also in {{Refs|LXX:}}_perfect_ πέπληγα, _subjunctive_ πεπλήγῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1350}}, _infinitive_ πεπληγέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.5}} (uncertain, but read by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.15e)}}, _participle_ πεπληγώς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.763, +others}} (also in _passive_ sense in late writers, {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.2Ch.29.9, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 31, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Trag.” 115, 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 5.91, etc.)}}; later _perfect_ πέπληχα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 485, “Sam.” 86, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 4.8.33}}: _Epic dialect_ _reduplicate_ _aorist 2_ ἐπέπληγον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.504, πέπληγον 23.363, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.264}}; _infinitive_ πεπληγέμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.728, 23.660}}; but _participle_ πεπλήγοντες in _present_ sense, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Jovem” 53, 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 28.327:}} —_middle_, _future_ πλήξομαι (κατα-) {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.80.2, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.10, etc.:}}_aorist_ ἐπληξάμην, _Epic dialect_ πληξάμην, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 245, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.14}}, and in late Prose, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 16.10.7, (κατα-) 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.52.1, etc.}}; _participle_ πληξάμενος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.125}}: _Epic dialect_ _aorist 2_ πεπλήγετο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|12.162, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.198, πεπλήγοντο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.51}}:—_passive_, _present_ πλήσσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Harmonica - Opera Mathematica” 1.1}}: _future_ πληγήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.10, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.263 (but in _composition_ ἐκ-πλᾰγήσομαι)}}; also πεπλήξομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 894, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 271, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 180a}}: _aorist_ ἐπλήχθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.39, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.93, “Placita Philosophorum” 4.14.3, but mostly ἐπλήγην, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.120, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 605, etc.}} (the former nowhere in Trag., except ἐκ-πληχθείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 183 (Lyric poetry))}}; _participle_ πληγείς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.12, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 608, “Frr.” 139, 180, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.4.3, etc.; _Doric dialect_ πλᾱγείς (see. below 1.1a ad near the end)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ πλάγεις [ᾱ] {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 26.3}}; (ἐπλάγην [ᾰ] only in compounds ἐξ-, κατ-, of persons **struck with terror** or **amazement**): _perfect_ πέπληγμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.41, etc.}}—in _Attic dialect_ and Trag., also LXX, the simple Verb is scarcely found except in _future_ 2 and 3, _aorist_ 2, and _perfect_ _passive_, but _future_ _active_ is used once by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}}, _perfect_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2 πέπληγα by 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus and 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus (see. above); 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus uses the _active_ (_aorist_) only in 3.64, 78.}} —The _present_ πλήσσω, πλήσσομαι are unknown to _Attic dialect_ writers (also to {{LXX|LXX, exc. LXX.4Mac.14.19), who use the}}_present_ _active_ and _passive_ of παίω, τύπτω instead (see. entry his vv.); whereas the _aorist 2_ _passive_ ἐπλήγην is used instead of ἐπαίσθην, ἐπατάχθην, or ἐτύφθην (ἐτύπην): hence παίσαντές τε καὶ πληγέντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 172; πότερον πρότερος ἐπλήγην ἢ ἐπάταξα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 4.15}}; πατάξας καταβάλλω, opposed to πληγεὶς κατέπεσεν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.25,27; ὁ πληγεὶς ἀεὶ τῆς πληγῆς ἔχεται, κἂν ἑτέρωσε πατάξῃ τις, ἐκεῖσ᾽ εἰσὶν αἱ χεῖρες 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.40; ὅταν ὁ μὲν πληγῇ, ὁ δὲ πατάξῃ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1132a8; πατάξαι ἢ πληγῆναι [prev. author] “Rh.” 1377a21; so in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.33,38 the}}_active_ πατάξαι corresponds with the _passive_ πληγῆναι{{Refs|in [prev. work] 36,39}}:—**strike, smite,** frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, especially of a direct blow, opposed to βάλλειν (οὔτε πληγέντα.., οὔτε βληθέντα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.117), πλῆξεν.. κόρυθος φάλον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.362; σκήπτρῳ δὲ μετάφρενον ἠδὲ καὶ ὤμω πλῆξεν 2.266, compare 16.791}}; πλήξας ξίφει αὐχένα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 332; μή τις.. ἐμὲ χειρὶ βαρείῃ πλήξῃ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.57, etc.; ἱστὸς.. πλῆξε κυβερνήτεω κεφαλήν 12.412}}: with _accusative_ _double_ _person_ et _of things_, **strike** one **on..,** τὸν δ᾽ ἄορι πλῆξ᾽ αὐχένα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.240, etc.; τὸν.. ξίφεϊ.. κληῗδα παρ᾽ ὦμον πλῆξ᾽ 5.147; τὸν.. κατ᾽ ἄκνηστιν μέσα νῶτα πλῆξα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.162}}; πὺξ πεπληγέμεν, of boxers, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.660; πλῆξ᾽ αὐτοσχεδίην 12.192}}; πεπληγὼς ἀγορῆθεν ἀεικέσσι πληγῇσιν **having driven** him **with blows,** {{Refs|2.264}}; κῦμα.. μιν.. πλῆξεν **struck** him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.431; ὦσε ποδὶ πλήξας 22.20}}; ἵππω πλήξαντε [ποσὶ τὸν νεκρόν] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.588; πέπληγον χορὸν ποσίν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.264}}; ἵππους ἐς πόλεμον πεπληγέμεν **whip** on the horses to the fray, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.728}}; of Zeus, **strike** with lightning, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 855:}} —_middle_, μηρὼ πληξάμενος **having smitten his** thighs, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.125; καὶ ὣ πεπλήγετο μηρώ 12.162 (but στῆθος πλήξας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.17)}}; πλήξασθαι τὴν κεφαλήν, as a token of grief, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.14}}:—_passive_, **to be struck, smitten,** πληγέντε κεραυνῷ **stricken** by lightning, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.455, etc.}}; of a ship, Διὸς πληγεῖσα κ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.416}}; of a tree, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 422, compare “Th.” 861}}; ἡ κριθὴ ἐπλήγη (by hail?) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.69 (3rd c.BC)}}: frequently in Trag., πληγεὶς θεοῦ μάστιγι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 608; Διὸς πληγέντα.. πυρί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 934}}; πληγείς τινος **stricken** by a man, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Or.” 497 (assuming variant)}}; ἔβραχε θύρετρα πληγέντα κληῗδι **touched** by the key, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.50; ὥσπερ τὰ χαλκία πληγέντα.. ἠχεῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 329a; ὑπὸ δόρατος πλαγεὶς δι᾽ ἀμφοτέρων τῶν ὀφθαλμῶν “IG” 42(1).122.64 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}: with _accusative_ _cognate_, πέπληγμαι καιρίαν πληγήν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1343.}}
**b** **sting,** πληγεῖσα ὑπὸ σκορπίου {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 1267.6 (1st c.AD).}}
**2** with _accusative_ of the thing set in motion, κονίσαλον ἐς οὐρανὸν ἐπέπληγον πόδες ἵππων **struck** the dust up to heaven, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.504}}; Ζεὺς ἐπ᾽ Ἴδᾳ πλᾶξε κεραυνόν (for Ἴδαν πλᾶξε κεραυνῷ) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.71:}} —_passive_, πλήσσονται λινέαις ὄρτυγες ἐν νεφέλαις **are dashed** against the nets, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 3.1.37.}}
**3** **strike** or **stamp** as one does a coin, Κύπριος χαρακτὴρ.. ἐν γυναικείοις τύποις.. πέπληκται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 283.}}
**4** of musical sounds, οὑτωσὶ πληγέντα οὕτως ἐφθέγξατο τὰ φωνήεντα {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.3.5.}}
**II** _metaphorically_ in _passive_, **receive a blow, to be heavily defeated,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.120, 8.130, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.108, 8.38}}; **to be stricken** by misfortune, συμφορῇ πεπληγμένον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.41, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 31 (Lyric poetry)}}; στρατὸν τοσοῦτον πέπληγμαι I **am smitten** in so great a host, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 1015 (Lyric poetry); διανταίαν δόμοισι καὶ σώμασιν πεπλαγμένους [prev. author] “Th.” 896 (Lyric poetry); φθινάσιν πληγεῖσα νόσοις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 819 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II.2** **to be smitten** emotionally, ἱμέρῳ πεπληγμένοι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 544}}; also πληγέντες δώροισι **touched** by bribes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.5; ἐξ ἔρωτος 4th-3rd c.BC: Hermesianax Elegiacus 7.42; τὴν καρδίαν πληγεὶς ὑπὸ λόγων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 218a, etc.}}
**II.3** _active_ of wines, when smelt or drunk, **overpower,** τὴν κεφαλήν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).568, 15.672}}; **shock,** κατασεισμὸς πλήσσει [τινὰ] βιαίως {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.72}}:— _passive_, πληττομένη ἡ μήτρα {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.59. (Cf. πλάζω}} Latin _plango,_ Gothic _faiflōkun_ (_reduplicate_) 'they beat their breasts'.)

4142:
** πλοιᾰρ-ιον**, τό,
_diminutive_ of πλοῖον, **skiff, boat,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 139, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.5.17, NT.John.6.22, “Periplus Maris Rubri [1st c.AD]” 15, “BGU” 812.5, etc.}}
**II** a kind of **woman's shoe,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 7.93.}}

4144:
** πλόος**, ὁ,
_Attic dialect_ _contraction_ **πλοῦς;** _plural_ πλοῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 304, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.7.7; _genitive_ πλῶν “OGI” 132.9 (Egypt, 2nd c.BC)}}; _dative_ πλοῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.83; _accusative_ πλοῦς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 362b24}}; later, a _genitive_ _singular_ πλοός, as if of third _declension_ (compare χοῦς A), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Periplus Maris Rubri [1st c.AD]” 62, “OGI” 572.21 (Myra, 2nd-3rd c.AD), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus Eph.1.14; _dative_ πλοΐ 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 21.2: _plural_ πλόες 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.; _accusative_ πλόας “IGRom.” 4.841 (from Hierapolis)}}: (πλέω):—**sailing, voyage,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.169, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (who always has the _disyllable_ form) 2.29, etc.; ἔσσεται Ἀγεάνακτι καλὸς π. ἐς Μιτυλάναν 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.52; ναῶν π. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.32; πλόον ὁρμαίνειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” [same place]}}; πλοῦν στεῖλαι, ποιεῖσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1045, “Ph.” 552}}; ἔξω πλόου out of **one's course,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.39; ἐπ᾽ ἡμέρας τέσσερας πλόος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.29}}; μῆκός ἐστι πλόος ἡμέραι τέσσερες its length is four days' **sail,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 158}}; ἐκ τῶν πλόων when **the voyage** is done, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.185: _metaphorically_, διὰ τοῦ πλοῦ.. τῆς ζωῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 803b.}}
**2** **time** or **tide for sailing,** ὡραῖος π., εἰαρινὸς π., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 630, 678; καιρὸς καὶ π. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1450 (_anapaest meter_)}}; π. ἡμῖν ἐγίγνετο, i.e. the wind was fair, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.24, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.137; π. ἐστί τινι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 899, “IA” 92; παραπεσόντος π. τισί 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.57.6}}; πλῷ χρήσασθαι to have a **fair wind,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.3; πλόον δοκάζων 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 52: _plural_, καλλίστοις ἐχρήσαντο πλοῖς 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.83.}}
**3** proverbial, δεύτερος π. 'the next best way', of those who try another scheme if the first fails (from those who use oars when the wind fails, ὁ δ. π. ἐστι δήπου λεγόμενος, ἂν ἀποτύχῃ τις οὐρίου (conjecture), κώπαισι πλεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 241), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 99d, “Phlb.” 19c, “Plt.” 300c}}; δεύτερος δὲ π..., πειρᾶσθαι.. the **next best thing** is to try.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1284b19; κατὰ τὸν δ. πλοῦν [prev. author] “EN” 1109a35; δ. ἂν εἴη π. τὸ.. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.36.6: proverbial, οὐ παντὸς ἀνδρὸς ἐπὶ τράπεζάν ἐσθ᾽ ὁ π. 4th c.BC(?): Nicolaus Comicus 1.26}}, compare Κόρινθο; ἡμέτερος ὁ π. 'our innings', {{Refs|“Comica Adespota” 274.}}
**4** later, ={ὁδός}, βαιὸν π. αἰὲν ὀκέλλει, of **the crawling** of a serpent, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 295, cf. Scholia (at [prev. work])}}
**4.b** **highway,** ὑλήεντα διὰ πλόον ἐρχομένοισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Antimachus Colophonius Elegiacus et Epicus 62; ἔχοντα παρὰ πλόον οἰκίον 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hecale” 1.4.13.}}

4145:
** πλούσιος**,
_Laconian dialect_ **πλούτιος** {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 156.20}}: α, ον: (πλοῦτος):— **wealthy, opulent,** opposed to πένης, πενιχρός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 22, “hymnus ad Mercurium” 171, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 621, etc.; πτωχὸς ἀντὶ πλουσίου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 455; ἐμοὶ πένης.. πλουσίου μᾶλλον ξένος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 395; μέγα π. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32}}; πλουσίῳ χαίρειν γένει in his **rich and lordly** race, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1070: proverbial, οὐδ᾽ εἰ Μίδου -ώτεροι εἶεν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 408b.}}
**2** with _genitive_ _of things_, **rich in** a thing, ὁ δαίμων δ᾽ ἐς ἐμὲ πλούσιος κακῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 394; π. οὐ χρυσίου, ἀλλ᾽ οὗ δεῖ τὸν εὐδαίμονα πλουτεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 521a; -ώτερος εἰς τὸ γῆρας.. φρονήσεως [prev. author] “Plt.” 261e.}}
**3** with _dative_, π. τοῖς ἀχρήστοις καὶ περιττοῖς {{NT+3rd c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 18; εἴκοσι μύξαις π... λύχνος 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 56; π. ἐν ἐλέει NT.Eph.2.4.}}
**II** of things, σοὶ δὲ π. τράπεζα κείσθω **richly furnished,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 361}}; **ample, abundant,** κτερίσματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1249; ὕδωρ [prev. author] “fragment” 316.3}}: _superlative_, θησαυρὸς ἀνάκειται ὁμοῖα τοῖσι πλουσιωτάτοισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.57}}. _adverb_ -ίως, ἱρὸν π. κατεσκευασμένον ἀναθήμασι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.44; π. ταφήσεται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 56; κοίτας.. π. σεσαγμένας 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 76, compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.400, etc.; νέον π. ἐπικηρυκευόμενον 5th c.AD: Aristaenetus Rhetor 2.1.}}

4146:
Included with: ** πλούσιος**,
_Laconian dialect_ **πλούτιος** {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 156.20}}: α, ον: (πλοῦτος):— **wealthy, opulent,** opposed to πένης, πενιχρός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 22, “hymnus ad Mercurium” 171, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 621, etc.; πτωχὸς ἀντὶ πλουσίου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 455; ἐμοὶ πένης.. πλουσίου μᾶλλον ξένος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 395; μέγα π. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32}}; πλουσίῳ χαίρειν γένει in his **rich and lordly** race, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1070: proverbial, οὐδ᾽ εἰ Μίδου -ώτεροι εἶεν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 408b.}}
**2** with _genitive_ _of things_, **rich in** a thing, ὁ δαίμων δ᾽ ἐς ἐμὲ πλούσιος κακῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 394; π. οὐ χρυσίου, ἀλλ᾽ οὗ δεῖ τὸν εὐδαίμονα πλουτεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 521a; -ώτερος εἰς τὸ γῆρας.. φρονήσεως [prev. author] “Plt.” 261e.}}
**3** with _dative_, π. τοῖς ἀχρήστοις καὶ περιττοῖς {{NT+3rd c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 18; εἴκοσι μύξαις π... λύχνος 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 56; π. ἐν ἐλέει NT.Eph.2.4.}}
**II** of things, σοὶ δὲ π. τράπεζα κείσθω **richly furnished,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 361}}; **ample, abundant,** κτερίσματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1249; ὕδωρ [prev. author] “fragment” 316.3}}: _superlative_, θησαυρὸς ἀνάκειται ὁμοῖα τοῖσι πλουσιωτάτοισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.57}}. _adverb_ -ίως, ἱρὸν π. κατεσκευασμένον ἀναθήμασι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.44; π. ταφήσεται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 56; κοίτας.. π. σεσαγμένας 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 76, compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.400, etc.; νέον π. ἐπικηρυκευόμενον 5th c.AD: Aristaenetus Rhetor 2.1.}}

4147:
** πλουτ-έω**,
(πλοῦτος) **to be rich, wealthy,** opposed to πένομαι, τάχα σε ζηλώσει ἀεργὸς πλουτεῦντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 313; πενιχρὸς αἶψα μάλ᾽ ἐπλούτησε}}**becomes rich,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 663, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 421d, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Κόλαξ” 42}}; π. μέγα, μάλιστα, μεγάλως, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, 3.57, 6.125; πλούτει κατ᾽ οἶκον μέγα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1168}}; ὄναρ πεπλουτηκέναι 'build castles in the air', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 218c, compare “Tht.” 208b; μὴ σπεύδετε πλουτεῖν μᾶλλον ἢ χρηστοὶ δοκεῖν εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.50; ταχέως πλουτῆσαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 18.18}}; π. ἀπὸ τῶν κοινῶν **to be rich** from the public purse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 569; π. ἀφ᾽ ἑαυτῶν 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Sententiae ad intelligibilia ducentes” 40; π. ἐκ τῶν ἀλλοτρίων 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.25; ὑφ᾽ ὑμῶν πεπλουτηκότας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.189 (ἀφ᾽ Cobet).}}
**2** with _genitive_ _of things_, **to be rich in** a thing, πόνου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 241; φίλων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.42; νομίσματος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1257b13}}; πλουτῖον (i.e. -τεῖον, for the sake of meter for -τέον) τέκνων.. δωμάτιον {{Refs|“IG” 12(8).442.8 (from Thasos).}}
**3** with _dative_ _of things_, π. ἐμπύροισιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 756}}; σιδήρῳ, χαλκῷ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 2.11.}}
**4** with _accusative_ _cognate_, π. πλοῦτον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 48}}; π. φίλους, φιλίαν, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 1.17c, 22.267a.}}

4149:
** πλοῦτος**, ὁ,
**wealth, riches,** ἄφενος καὶ πλοῦτον ἀφύξειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.171; ὄλβῳ τε πλούτῳ τε 16.596; π. ἀρεταῖς δεδαιδαλμένος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.53}}; opposed to πενία, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 421d; ἀνατετροφέναι πλοῦτον 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.131: _plural_, τῶν γὰρ π. ὁδ᾽ ἄριστος}}**treasures,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 137 (_anapaest meter_); πλούτοις καὶ πενίαις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 618b; γένη καὶ πλοῦτοι [prev. author] “Grg.” 523c, compare “Prt.” 354b, etc.:}} with _genitive_ _of things_, π. ἀργύρου, χρυσοῦ, **treasure** of silver or gold, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.ά, “Anacreontea” 34.1; οὔτε ἀργυροῦς π.οὔτε χρυσοῦς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 801b}}; ἀφανὴς π., opposed to γῆ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 602.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, πραπίδων π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 129.2; π. τῆς σοφίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 12a}}; γᾶς π. ἄβυσσος, of the whole earth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 948 (Lyric poetry); πλοῦτον εἵματος κακόν [prev. author] “Ag.” 1383; ὁ ἐν τῇ ἐμῇ ψυχῇ π. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.43, compare 34, etc.}}
**II** _masculine_ _proper name_ **Plutus,** god of riches, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 969}}; represented as blind, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Timocreon Lyricus 8; ὁ δὲ Π. ἡμᾶς.. τυφλοὺς ποιεῖ 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 259:— 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus see at {εὔπλουτον} says that π. originally meant wealth in corn. (Prob. from πλέω in an early sense '*flow', '*abound', as φόρτος from φέρω.)}}]

4151:
** πνεῦμα**, ατος, τό,
(πνέω) **blast, wind,** first in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anaximenes Philosophus 2, ὅλον τὸν κόσμον π. καὶ ἀὴρ περιέχει}}: frequently in Trag., etc., ἀνέμων πνεύματα πάντων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1086 (_anapaest meter_), compare 1047 (_anapaest meter_)}}; θαλάσσας.. πνεύματι λάβρῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 110 (Lyric poetry); πνευμάτων ἐπομβρίᾳ [prev. author] “fragment” 300.3; τέως δὲ κούφοις πνεύμασιν βόσκου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 558; πνεύμασιν θαλασσίοις ἐξωσθέντες 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 278 (but πνοή}} is commoner in Poets; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses πνοιή); πνεύματα ἀνέμων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.16. ; τὸ π. κατῄει 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.84}}; κατὰ πρύμναν ἵσταται τὸ π.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 97; τὸ π. λεῖον καὶ καθεστηκὸς λαβεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1003; τὸ π. ἔλαττον γίγνεται [prev. author] “Eq.” 441; εἰ φορὸν π. εἴη 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.2.27}}; κατὰ πνεῦμα στῆναι τοῦ ἄρρενος to **leeward** of him, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 560b14}}; but κατὰ π. προσιόντες down **wind,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 535a19}}; πνεύματος ἀνείλησις, ἐκπύρωσις, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2pp.44,45 U.}}; as an element, **air,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|Corp.Herm. 1.9, 16; τὸ π. τὸ περὶ τὴν ψυχήν 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.2.2, compare 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Sententiae ad intelligibilia ducentes” 29.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, θαλερωτέρῳ π. with more genial **breeze** or **influence,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 708 (Lyric poetry); λύσσης π. μάργῳ [prev. author] “Pr.” 884 (_anapaest meter_)}}; αἰδοίῳ π. χώρας with **air** or **spirit** of respect on the part of the country, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Supp.” 29 (_anapaest meter_)}}; π. ταὐτὸν οὔποτ᾽.. ἐν ἀνδράσιν φίλοις βέβηκεν **the wind** is constantly changing even among friends, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 612; π. συμφορᾶς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1317; ὅταν θεοῦ σοι π. μεταβαλὸν τύχῃ [prev. author] “HF” 216.}}
**II** **breathed air, breath,** σάλπιγξ βροτείου πνεύματος πληρουμένη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 568}}; αὐλῶν, λωτοῦ π., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 128 (Lyric poetry), “Ph.” 787 (Lyric poetry, _plural_)}}; π. ἀπέρρηξεν βίου **the breath** of life, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 507; π. ἀπώλεσεν [prev. author] “Th.” 984 (Lyric poetry)}}; π. ἄθροισον collect **breath,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 851}}; π. ἀφεῖναι, ἀνεῖναι, μεθεῖναι, to give up **the ghost,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hec.” 571, “Or.” 277, “Tr.” 785 (_anapaest meter_); π. δειμαίνων λιπεῖν [prev. author] “Supp.” 554; π... δυσῶδες ἠφίει 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49}}; πνεύματος διαρροαί the wind-pipe, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 567; τὰς τοῦ π. διεξόδους ἀποφράττον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 91c (see. πνεύμων); πνεύματος ῥώμη 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.804b: proverbial, ἄνθρωπός ἐστι π. καὶ σκιὰ μόνον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 13.}}
**II.2** **breathing, respiration,** frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus, π. πυκνόν, ἀραιόν, ἐκτεῖνον, κατεπεῖγον, “Epid.” 2.3.7; π. πυκνότερον “Acut.” 16}}; π. προσκόπτον checked, difficult **breathing, Aph.**{{Refs|4.68}}; π. ἄσημον indistinct, feeble **breathing, Epid.**{{Refs|6.7.8}}; π. βηχῶδες {{Refs|“Coac.” 622}}; π. μετέωρον shallow **breathing, Epid.**{{Refs|2.3.1}}; τὸ π. ἔχειν ἄνω to be out of **breath,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 23, cf. Sosicrates Comicus 1}}; τὸ π. ἀνήνεγκαν recovered their **breath,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.12 (so without τὸ π. “Aph.” 2.43)}}; but ἀναφέρουσιν.. κλαίοντά τε καὶ ἐς τὰς ῥῖνας ἀνέλκοντα τὸ π. they **sob..,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hebd.” 51.}}
**II.2.b** _plural_, of the air imagined as filling the veins, πνευμάτων ἀπολήψιες ἀνὰ φλέβας {{Refs|[prev. author]}}**Acut.(Sp.)**{{Refs|7, +others}}
**II.3** **flatulence,** in _plural_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 107.9, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 948b25, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.112, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 6.94.}}
**II.4** **breath of life,** π. ζωῆς {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Gen.6.17, 7.15, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 13,etc.}}; π. ἔχειν retain **life,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.10.4}}; **living being,** ἐγὼ Νίνος πάλαι ποτ᾽ ἐγενόμην π. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Phoenix Iambographus 1.16}}; οὐ π. πάντα βρότεια σοὶ (i.e. Πλούτωνι) νέμετα; {{Refs|“IG” 14.769 (from Naples).}}
**II.5** **that which is breathed forth** or **exhaled, odour,** ὦ θεῖον ὀδμῆς π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1391}}; π. βαρὺ ἀφιεῖσα, of a tree, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.647b.}}
**II.6** Grammars, **breathing with which a vowel is pronounced,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 1009e (_plural_), 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 147.18}}; π. δασύ, ψιλόν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pron.” 78.6, “Adv.” 148.9.}}
**III** **divine inspiration,** ἄγρια.. πνεύματα θευφορίης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.220.4 (3rd c.BC: Dioscorides Epigrammaticus); εἰ μή τι θεῖον.. ἐνῆν π. τῇ ψυχῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 370c; τὸ ἱερὸν καὶ δαιμόνιον ἐν μούσαις π. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.605a; καθαρὸν δίκαιον.. π. θεοῦ σωτῆρος “BMus.Inscription” 1062 (Cyrene, 2nd c.AD).}}
**IV** the **spirit** of God, π. θεοῦ {{LXX|LXX.Gen.1.2, etc.}}: frequently in **NT,** τὸ π. τὸ ἅγιον {{NT|NT.Mark.3.29, +others}}
**IV.2** **spirit** of man, εἴτ᾽ ἐστὶ τοῦτο π. θεῖον εἴτε νοῦς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 482.3}}: in **NT,** opposed to ψυχή, {{NT|NT.1Thes.5.23, cf. NT.Rom.8.2}}; τῷ π., opposed to τῷ σώματι, {{NT|NT.1Cor.5.3}}; also, opposed to γράμμα, {{NT|NT.Rom.2.29.}}
**V** **spiritual** or **immaterial being, angel,** {{NT|NT.Heb.1.14, NT.Rev.1.4}}; τὰ ἄχραντα π., τὰ κακὰ π., {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 3.31}}; π. πονηρόν, ψευδές, {{LXX+NT+3rd c.AD+|LXX.Judg.9.23, LXX.1Ki.22.21, cf. NT.Act.19.12, 15, NT.Rev.16.14, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus cited in 4th c.AD: Eusebius Caesariensis Scriptor Ecclesiasticus “Praeparatio Evangelica” 4.23, etc.}}; ἀλάλου καὶ κακοῦ π. οὖσα πλήρης (i.e. ἡ Πυθία) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.438b.}}
**VI** Rhet., **sentence declaimed in one breath,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ εὑρέσεως” 3.10, 4.4, +others}}

4152:
** πνευμᾰτ-ικός**, ή, όν,
**of wind** or **air,** κινήσεις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 916b4; βία π. [prev. author] “HA” 586a17; φύσεις 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.39U.; ἀέρος ψυχρότης 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 4.12.5}}; π. [ὄργανον] a machine **moved by wind,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Vitruvius Scriptor de Architectura Latinus 10.1.1; μηχάνημα 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ ψυχῆς παθῶν κτλ. - Galeni Scripta Minora” 2.3.}}
**2** **of the nature of wind** or **air,** τὰ π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 380a23}}; π. ξηρότης, i. e. a dry **vapour,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 35.}}
**2.b** **of subtle substance,** τὸ π. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.3.5}}; οὐσία, opposed to ὑγρά, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.15, compare 2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 1.8, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.596.}}
**3** **inflated, distended with air,** ὑστέραι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 584b22.}}
**4** _active_ (= πνευματώδης {{Refs|1.3)}}, **causing flatulence,** οἶνος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pr.” 955a35; βρώματα 3rd c.BC: Nicomachus Comicus 1.31, compare 3rd c.BC: Diphilus Siphnius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.73a (_superlative_), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.286e, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.52}}. _adverb_ **-κῶς** **by flatulence,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.537.}}
**5** **breathing, exhaling,** εὐοσμία {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.16.3.}}
**II** **of the breath** or **breathing,** τὸ π. μόριον, ὁ π. τόπος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 781a31, “Pr.” 962a11.}}
**III** **of spirit, spiritual,** interpolated in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.129c}}; opposed to σαρκικός, ψυχικός, {{NT|NT.Rom.15.27, NT.1Cor.2.13, etc.}} _adverb_ -κῶς{{Refs|[prev. work] 14.}}
**IV** οἱ Π. a school of physicians **who referred all questions of health to pneumatic agencies,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.749, 15.111.}}
**V** **conveying** πνεῦμα, κοιλία, of the left ventricle of the heart (opposed to αἱματική), {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Erasistratus Medicus cited in [same work] “UP” 6.12, compare “Placita Philosophorum” 4.5.7.}}
**VI** Rhet., _adverb_ -κῶς **in one breath** (compare πνεῦμα VI), ἀποτείνεσθαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ εὑρέσεως” 4.1.}}

4153:
Included with: ** πνευμᾰτ-ικός**, ή, όν,
**of wind** or **air,** κινήσεις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 916b4; βία π. [prev. author] “HA” 586a17; φύσεις 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.39U.; ἀέρος ψυχρότης 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 4.12.5}}; π. [ὄργανον] a machine **moved by wind,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Vitruvius Scriptor de Architectura Latinus 10.1.1; μηχάνημα 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ ψυχῆς παθῶν κτλ. - Galeni Scripta Minora” 2.3.}}
**2** **of the nature of wind** or **air,** τὰ π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 380a23}}; π. ξηρότης, i. e. a dry **vapour,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 35.}}
**2.b** **of subtle substance,** τὸ π. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.3.5}}; οὐσία, opposed to ὑγρά, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.15, compare 2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 1.8, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.596.}}
**3** **inflated, distended with air,** ὑστέραι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 584b22.}}
**4** _active_ (= πνευματώδης {{Refs|1.3)}}, **causing flatulence,** οἶνος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pr.” 955a35; βρώματα 3rd c.BC: Nicomachus Comicus 1.31, compare 3rd c.BC: Diphilus Siphnius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.73a (_superlative_), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.286e, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.52}}. _adverb_ **-κῶς** **by flatulence,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.537.}}
**5** **breathing, exhaling,** εὐοσμία {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.16.3.}}
**II** **of the breath** or **breathing,** τὸ π. μόριον, ὁ π. τόπος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 781a31, “Pr.” 962a11.}}
**III** **of spirit, spiritual,** interpolated in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.129c}}; opposed to σαρκικός, ψυχικός, {{NT|NT.Rom.15.27, NT.1Cor.2.13, etc.}} _adverb_ -κῶς{{Refs|[prev. work] 14.}}
**IV** οἱ Π. a school of physicians **who referred all questions of health to pneumatic agencies,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.749, 15.111.}}
**V** **conveying** πνεῦμα, κοιλία, of the left ventricle of the heart (opposed to αἱματική), {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Erasistratus Medicus cited in [same work] “UP” 6.12, compare “Placita Philosophorum” 4.5.7.}}
**VI** Rhet., _adverb_ -κῶς **in one breath** (compare πνεῦμα VI), ἀποτείνεσθαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ εὑρέσεως” 4.1.}}

4154:
** πνέω**,
_poetry_ πνείω as always in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus except 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.469:}}_future_ πνεύσομαι (ἐκ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 886 (Lyric poetry), (ἐμ-) [prev. author] “Andr.” 555, (παρα-) 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.133}}; also πνευσοῦμαι {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1221, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 367a13, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 34, 4th c.AD: Palaephatus Paradoxographus 17; πνεύσω 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 32, LXX.Psa.147.7(18), LXX.Sir.43.20, “Geoponica” 1.12.34, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.112 (1st c.BC: Antipater Thessalonicensis Epigrammaticus), (ἀνα-) 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 13.516 (συμ-πνευσόντων}} is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.169)}}: _aorist 1_ ἔπνευσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 506, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.20, etc., (ἐν-) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.456, (ἀν-) 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 274:}}_perfect_ πέπνευκα (ἐπι-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 262d, (ἐκ-) 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 904a1:}} —_passive_, _future_ πνευσθήσομαι (δια-) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.1:}}_aorist_ ἐπνεύσθην (δι-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 5.5.6, etc.— 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and early Prose writers use the simple Verb only in}}_present_ and _imperfect_, to which Trag. add _future_ and _aorist 1_ _active_ —For the form ἄμπνυε, see. {ἀναπνέω}; for ἀμπνύνθη, -πνυτο, see at {ἄμπνυτο}; and for _perfect_ _passive_ πέπνῡμαι, _participle_ πεπνῡμένος, see at {πέπνυμαι}.—Like other _disyllable_ Verbs in -έω, this Verb contracts only εε, εε; but ἐκπνέων is _disyllable_ in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1493, 1517 (both Lyric poetry)}}:—**blow,** of wind and air, οὐδέ ποτ᾽ οὖροι πνείοντες φαίνονθ᾽ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.361; αὔρη δ᾽ ἐκ ποταμοῦ ψυχρὴ πνέει 5.469; ἐτησίαι.. οὐκ ἔπνευσαν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.20, etc.}}; τῷ πνέοντι (i.e. ἀνέμῳ or πνεύματι) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cont.” 3}}; ἡ πνέουσα (i.e. αὔρα) {{NT|NT.Act.27.40}}; also of a flute-player, μέγα πνέων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.72}}; and of the flutes themselves, αὐλοὺς ἡδὺ πνέοντας {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.254 (Myrinus Epigrammaticus)}}; πνεῖται flutes **are sounding,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Mnesimachus Comicus 4.57 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II** **breathe, send forth an odour,** ἀμβροσίη.. ἡδὺ πνείουσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.446}}; π. εὐῶδες, δυσῶδες, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.75, etc.: _absolutely_, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.80.}}
**II.2** with _accusative_, **breathe out, send forth,** Ζεφύρου πνείοντος ἐέρσην {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Apollinem” 82.}}
**II.3** with _genitive_, **breathe** or **smell of** a thing, οὐ μύρου πνέον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 565; τράγου π. “Anthologia Graeca” 11.240 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus); μόγοιο 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 6.164; λύθρου καὶ αἵματος [prev. author] 5.120 (ἐπιπν- codices)}}: rarely with _dative_, μύροισι π. **smell with..,** {{Refs|Anthologia Graeca 5.199}}: frequently _metaphorically_, **breathe, be redolent of,** Χαρίτων πνείοντα μέλη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 184.3; ὄμματα.. πόθου πνείοντα “Anthologia Graeca” 5.258 (Paul. Sil.)}}; φόνου π. conjecture in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|3rd-4th c.AD: Tryphiodorus Epicus 505; αὐθαδείας πολὺς ἔπνει 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.51.}}
**III** of perceptible breathing, [ἵππω] πνείοντε κατ᾽ ὤμων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.385; π. ὕπνῳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 621 (Lyric poetry).}}
**IV** generally, **draw breath, breathe**: hence, **live,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.447}}; οἱ πνέοντες, ={οἱ ζῶντες}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1160; ὄλβος ἀεὶ πνεῖ 3rd c.BC: Simmias 25.12; ἥμισύ μευ ψυχῆς ἔτι τὸ πνέον 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 42.}}
**V** _metaphorically_, with _accusative_ _cognate_, **breathe forth,** μένεα πνείοντες **breathing** spirit, epithet of warriors, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.536, 3.8, 11.508, etc.; so πῦρ π. 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 319, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 146; φόνον δόμοι πνέουσιν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1309; κότον πνέων [prev. author] “Ch.” 34 (Lyric poetry), compare 951 (Lyric poetry)}}; φρενὸς πνέων τροπαίαν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ag.” 219 (Lyric poetry); Ἄρη πνεόντων [prev. work] 376 (Lyric poetry)}}; πνέων χάριν τινί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1206; πῦρ πνειόντων.. ἄστρων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1146 (Lyric poetry); πῦρ π. καὶ φόνον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 288; ὠδῖνας [prev. author] “HF” 862: _paratragoedia_ in Comedy texts, πνέοντας δόρυ καὶ λόγχας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1016}}; τρέχει τις Ἀλφειὸν πνέων, of a swift runner, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Av.” 1121, etc.}}; and in a rhetorical passage, οἱ πῦρ πνέοντες, οἱ νενικηκότες Λακεδαιμονίους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.5.12.}}
**V.2** with _neuter_ _adjectives_ or Prons., πνέοντες μεγάλα giving themselves airs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 189}}; τόσονδ᾽ ἔπνευσας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 327; κενεὰ πνεύσαις 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).93; χαμηλὰ πνέων [prev. author] “P.” 11.30: _absolutely_, ὑπὲρ σακέων πνείοντες}} **breathing** over their shields, i. e. unable to repress their rage for war, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 24; θρασείᾳ πνέων καρδίᾳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.44: with _nominative_, Ἄρης.. μέγας πνέων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 323; πολὺς ἔπνει καὶ λαμπρός 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.57; οὗτος.. καικίας ἢ συκοφαντίας πνεῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 437}}; ᾧ σὺ μὴ πνεύσῃς ἐνδέξιος on whom **thou breathest** not favourably, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 10.3.}}

4155:
** πνίγ-ω** [ῑ],
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 68, +others, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.1.6, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.31, etc.}}: _imperfect_ ἔπνῑγον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1376:}}_future_ πνίξω (ἀπο-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 198, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 171}}; _Doric dialect_ _2nd pers._ _plural_ _future_ πνιξεῖσθε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 155:}}_aorist_ ἔπνιξα, _imperative_ πνῖξον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 27, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.92, Batrachomyomachia 158}}:—_passive_, _future_ πνῐγήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ φυσικῶν δυνάμεων - Scripta Minora” 1.17, (ἀπο-) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1504, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 3.16; also ἀπο-πεπνίξομαι 4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Vitae Sophistarum” p.463 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus:}}_aorist_ ἐπνίχθην (ἀπ-) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 1.7}}; but ἐπνίγην [ῐ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 148, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 1.11, (ἀπ-) 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 159, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 512a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 32.6, etc.:}}_perfect_ πέπνιγμαι, see #11.—The simple verb is less frequently than the _compound_ ἀποπνίγω:— **choke, throttle, strangle,** Sophr.[same place], etc.; of a doctor, πνίγων.. πικρότατα πόματα διδούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 522a}}; ἂν ὕλη πνίγῃ [τὸν σῖτον] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 17.14: proverbial, ὅταν τὸ ὕδωρ πνίγῃ, τί δεῖ ἐπιπίνει; 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1146a35}}:—_passive_, **to be choked, stifled,** ἐπνιγόμην τὰ σπλάγχνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1036; αἱ ὑστερικῶς πνιγόμεναι 2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 10.19.1.}}
**2** _impersonal_ πνίγει, of great heat, **it is stifling,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 941b4, 944a7.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, **vex, torment,** ἕνα χαλκοῦν ἀποβαλὼν αὑτὸν π. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.37 W.}}; ὃ δὲ μάλιστά με πνίγει variant in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Prom.” 17}}; **oppress** by exactions, 'squeeze', {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Misopogon” 368c.}}
**II** **cook in a close-covered vessel, bake, stew,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.92; δικίδιον.. ἐν λοπάδι πεπνιγμένον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 511; πεπνιγμένος 5th c.BC: Metagenes Comicus 6. 9.}}
**III** **drown** {{Refs|compare (πνῖξις 11)}}, in _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.7.25: _metaphorically_, of plants, τὰ φυτὰ.. τοῖς πολλοῖς [ὕδασι] πνίγεται 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.9b.}}

4156:
** πνικ-τός**, ή, όν,
**strangled,** {{NT|NT.Acts.15.20, +others}}
**2** **air-tight,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “(Spiritalia) Pneumatica” 1.3, 16, +others}} _adverb_ -τῶς{{Refs|[prev. work] 2.21.}}
**II** **baked** or **stewed** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (πνίγω 11), 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 175, 5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 29, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 1.4, etc.}}

4157:
** πνοή**, ῆς, ἡ,
_Epic dialect_ **πνοιή**, always in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}; _Doric dialect_ **πνοά** (see. below); Lyric poetry **πνοιά** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.31, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.28}}: (πνέω):— **blowing, blast,** πνοιαὶ παντοίων ἀνέμων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.55, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.839, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 253, 268; πνοιὴ Βορέαο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.697}}:_absolutely_, **blast, breeze,** {{Refs|11.622, 13.590}}, etc.; ὀλίγη π. a light **breeze,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 34.4}}; π. βιαία a stiff **breeze,**{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. work] 35.4; οἷον π. εἰς ἄλλο 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.3.23}}; especially to denote excessive swiftness, ἅμα πνοιῇς ἀνέμοιο along with, i.e.swift as, **blasts** of wind, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.342, etc.; ἅμα πνοιῇ Ζεφύροιο 19.415; ἐπέτοντο μετὰ πνοιῇς ἀνέμοιο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.148; πέτετο πνοιῇς ἀνέμοιο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.207; ἅμα πνοιῇσι πετέσθην 16.149; imitated by 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1396 (Lyric poetry)}}, ἅμ᾽ ἀνέμων πνοαῖσι βαίη; frequently in Trag., ταχύπτεροι πνοαί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 88; πνοαὶ δ᾽ ἀπὸ Στρυμόνος μολοῦσαι [prev. author] “Ag.” 192 (Lyric poetry), compare 654, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 161, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 392b11, etc.}}; **blast** of bellows, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.100.}}
**II** **breathing hard, panting,** of horses, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.380, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 719 (_plural_).}}
**II.2** generally, **breath,** ἔμπνους μέν εἰμι.. καὶ πνοὰς.. πνέω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1092; μητρὸς οἴχονται πνοαί [prev. author] “Or.” 421: _metaphorically_, πνοιὴ Ἡφαίστοιο}} **the breath** of Hephaestus, i.e. **flame,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.355; πυρὸς πνοᾷ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 815 (Lyric poetry); πρὶν καταιγίσαι πνοὰς Ἄρεως 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 63, compare 115 (Lyric poetry); θεοῦ πνοαῖσιν ἐμμανεῖς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1094; πνοαὶ Ἀφροδίτης [prev. author] “IA” 69; θυμοῦ πνοαί [prev. author] “Ph.” 454.}}
**III** **vapour, exhalation,** σποδὸς προπέμπει πλούτου πνοάς, of a burning city, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 820; τηγάνου π. 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 75.8, compare 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 217.7; λιβάνου πνοαί 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 41.37 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**IV** **breath** of a wind-instrument, Αἰολῇσιν ἐν πνοαῖσιν αὐλῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.79; αὐλῶν π. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 313; σύριγγος πνοά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 145 (Lyric poetry).—poetical (5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 419d is no exception), once in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus and frequently in later Prose (see. above) for πνεῦμα.}}

4158:
** ποδ-ήρης**, ες,
**reaching to the feet,** πέπλοι, χιτὼν π., a robe **that falls over the feet,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 833, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.4.2, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.19.6, etc. (later ποδήρης alone (i.e. χιτών)}} of the High Priest's robe, {{LXX|LXX.Exo.25.6, Aristeas 96)}}; π. ἀσπίς large shield **which covered the body down to the feet,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.8.9, “Cyr.” 6.2.10: Comedy texts, πώγων π. καθεῖται 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.52c.}}
**2** ναῦς π. a ship **with feet,** i.e. **oars,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1515.29}}; στῦλος π. a **firmly based** pillar, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 898.}}
**3** τὰ π. **parts about the feet, feet,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|[prev. work] 1594. (-ήρης perhaps from -ᾱρης,compare 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus _proper name_ Ποδάρης.)}}

4159:
** πόθεν**,
_Ionic dialect_ **κόθεν**:
**I** _interrogative_ _adverb_ **whence?**
**I.1** of Place, εἰρώτα.., τίς εἴη καὶ π. ἔλθοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.423; ποδαπὸς ὁ ξένο; π.; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 657; ποῖ δὴ καὶ π.; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 227a: with _genitive_, τίς π. εἰς ἀνδρῶ; 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.150, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.170, +others; κ. τῆς Φρυγίης ἥκω; 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.35; π. γῆς ἦλθε; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 258, etc.}}
**I.2** of origin, π. γένος εὔχεται εἶναι **from what stock** he avows that he is by descent, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.373; τὴν.. τέχνην πῶς καὶ π. ἄν τις δύναιτο πορίσασθα; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 269d; π. ἄλλοθεν..; 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.28: with _genitive_, π. ποτὲ.. θνητῶν ἔφυσα; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 841.}}
**I.3** in speaking, π. ἄρξωμα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 855 (_anapaest meter_); π. ἂν λάβοιμι ῥῆμ; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 521, etc.}}
**I.4** of the cause, **whence? wherefore?** π. χοὰς ἔπεμψε; ἐκ τίνος λόγο; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 515}}; to express surprise or negation, π. γὰρ ἔσται βιοτ; i.e. οὐδαμόθεν, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1159 (Lyric poetry); π. υἱὸς αὐτοῦ ἐστι; NT.Mark.12.37; πόθε;}}**how can it be? impossible! nonsense!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1620, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1145, “Ra.” 1455; σὺ δ᾽ ὁμέστιος θεοῖ; π.; [prev. author] “fragment” 655; ἀλλ᾽ οὐκ ἔστι ταῦτα· πόθε; πολλοῦ γε καὶ δεῖ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.47, compare 24.157, etc.}}; π. γά; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 781.}}
**I.5** with Verbs of finding, taking, purchasing, etc., π. ἂν πριαίμην ῥῖν; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 21; π. ἄν τις τοῦτο τὸ χρῖμα λάβο; 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.4; π. πρᾷον.. ἦθος εὑρήσομε; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 375c, compare “Euthd.” 273e, +others; so κάθησθε κλάοντες περὶ τῆς αὔριον π. φάγητε 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.9.19.}}
**II** **ποθέν**, _enclitic_ _adverb_ **from some place** or **other,** εἴ π. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.380; εἰ καί π. ἄλλοθεν ἔλθοι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.52, compare 5.490; φανεὶς.. π. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 354; ἦλθέ π. σωτήρ [prev. author] “Ch.” 1073 (_anapaest meter_); ἐκ δρυός π. ἢ ἐκ πέτρας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 544d}}; ἐκ βιβλίου π. ἀκούσας from **some** book **or other,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 268c, compare 244d; after ἐνθένδε, ἐντεῦθεν, [prev. work] 229b, 270a, etc.}}

