** ποιέω**,
_Doric dialect_ **ποιϝέω** {{Refs|“IG” 4.800 (from Troezen)}}, etc.: _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ ποίεον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.147}}; _contraction_ ποίει {{Refs|18.482}}; _Ionic dialect_ ποιέεσκον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.36, 4.78:}}_future_ ποιήσω: _aorist_ ἐποίησα, _Epic dialect_ ποίησα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.490:}}_perfect_ πεποίηκα:—_middle_, _Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ ποιεέσκετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.119}}: _future_ ποιήσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.397: in _passive_ sense, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ εὐσχημοσύνης” 11, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1021a23:}}_aorist_ ἐποιησάμην, _Epic dialect_ ποι- {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.251, +others:}}_perfect_ πεποίημαι in _middle_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.22, Decrees cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.29:}} —_passive_, _future_ ποιηθήσομαι (μετα-) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.62}}, see above; πεποιήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.11, 37}}: _aorist_ ἐποιήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.159, etc. (used as _middle_ only in _compound_ προσ-)}}: _perfect_ πεποίημαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.56, etc.}}:—_Attic dialect_ **ποῶ** {{Refs|(“Etymologicum Magnum” 679.24)}}, etc., is guaranteed by metre in Trag. and Comedy texts, as ποῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 918, ποεῖν [prev. author] “Tr.” 385, ποεῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 410, etc.}}, and found in codex Laur. of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, codex Rav. of 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus, also “IG” 12.39.6}} (ποήσω), 82.9 (ποεῖ), 154.7 (ἐποησάτην), etc.; but ποι- is always written before -οι, -ου, -ω in Inscrr.: πο- also in _Aeolic dialect_ πόημι πόης πόει [{{Refs 7th c.BC+|“PBouriant” 8.71, 75, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 1.9, +others, and 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus ποέντω, ={ποιούντων}, “IG” 5(2).6.9 (Tegea, 4th c.BC)}}]; compare ποιητής. Used in two general senses, **make** and **do.**
**A** **make, produce,** first of something material, as manufactures, works of art, etc. (opposed to πράττειν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 163b), in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus frequently of building, π. δῶμα, τύμβον, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.608, 7.435; εἴδωλον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.796}}; π. πύλας ἐν [πύργοις] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.339}}; of smith's work, π. σάκος{{Refs|[prev. work] 222; ἐν [σάκεϊ] ποίει δαίδαλα πολλά 18.482, compare 490, 573: frequently in Inscrr. on works of art, Πολυμήδης ἐποίϝη}}**h'** (= ἐποίησε) Ἀργεῖος {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 5 (6th c.BC,compare “Class.Phil.” 20.139); Θεόπροπος ἐποίει Αἰγινάτας “SIG” 18 (6th-5th c.BC), etc.; ἐποίησε Τερψικλῆς [prev. work]3b(Milet., 6th c.BC), etc.}}; τίς.. τὴν λίθον ταύτην τέκτων ἐποίε; {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.22}}; εἵματα ἀπὸ ξύλων πεποιημένα **made** from trees, i.e. of cotton, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.65; ναὸν ἀπὸ τοῦ ἱεροῦ ἀργυρίου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.3.9; πλοῖα ἐκ τῆς ἀκάνθης ποιεύμενα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.96; καρβάτιναι πεποιημέναι ἐκ βοῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.14: with _genitive_ materiae, πωρίνου λίθου π. τὸν νηόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.62; ἔρυμα λίθων λογάδην πεποιημένον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.31; φοίνικος αἱ θύραι πεποιημέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.22: rarely ποιεῖσθαί τινι}}**to be made with..,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Longus Scriptor Eroticus 1.4}}; also τῶν τὰ κέρεα.. οἱ πήχεες ποιεῦνται the horns of which **are made** into the sides of the lyre, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.192}}; also δέρμα εἰς περικεφαλαίας πεποίηται {{Refs|Scholia Patm.D.in “BCH” 1.144}}:—_middle_, **make for oneself,** as of bees, οἰκία ποιήσωνται **build them** houses, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.168, compare 5.735, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.251, 259, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 503}}; [ῥεῖθρον] π., of a river, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.1.5}}; also, **have** a thing **made, get** it **made,** ὀβελούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.135; στεφάνους οὓς ἐποιησάμην τῷ χορῷ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.3.5}}; τὸν Ἀπόλλω, i.e. a statue of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 361a; αὑτοῦ εἰκόνας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 5, compare “Inscription Prien.” 25.9 (3rd c.BC?).}}
**A.2** **create, bring into existence,** γένος ἀνθρώπων χρύσεον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 110, compare “Th.” 161, 579, etc.; ὁ ποιῶν}}**the creator,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 76c; ἕτερον Φίλιππον ποιήσετε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.11}}:—_middle_, **beget,** υἱόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.124; ἔκ τινος [prev. author] 4.22}}; παῖδας ποιεῖσθαι, ={παιδοποιεῖσθαι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.19, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.43}}; **conceive,** παιδίον π. ἔκ τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 203b}}:—_active_ in this sense only in later Gr., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.312a}}; of the woman, παιδίον ποιῆσαι {{Refs|[prev. work]145d.}}
**A.3** generally, **produce,** ὕδωρ π., of Zeus, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 261: _impersonal_, ἐὰν πλείω ποιῇ ὕδατα, ={ἐὰν ὕη}, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 1.19.3}}]; π. γάλα, of certain kinds of food, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 522b32}}; ἄρρεν π., of an egg, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 1.15}}; μέλι ἄριστον π., of Hymettus, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.1.23}}; π. καρπόν, of trees, {{NT|NT.Matt.3.10}} (_metaphorically_ in religious sense,{{Refs|[prev. work] 8)}}; of men, κριθὰς π. **grow** barley, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1322; π. σίτου μεδίμνους 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.20}}; π. πενίαν, πλοῦτον, of the stars, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.3.1.}}
**A.3.b** in Mathematics texts, **make, produce,** τομήν, σχῆμα, ὀρθὰς γωνίας, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 1.16, 38, “Con.Sph.” 12; ὁ Α τὸν Γ πολλαπλασιάσας τὸν Η πεποίηκεν 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 7.19:}} —_passive_, πεποιήσθω ὡς.. **let it be contrived** that.., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 2.6.}}
**A.3.c** **postulate, imply,** ἡ προσθήκη ἀφαίρεσιν καὶ ἔλλειψιν ποιεῖ {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.9.3.}}
**A.3.d** π. τὸ πρόβλημα **effect a solution of** the problem, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Apollonius Pergaeus Geometra “Conica” 2.49, 51}}; π. τὸ ἐπίταγμα **fulfil, satisfy** the required condition, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 1.2, 3.}}
**A.4** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, of Poets, **compose, write,** π. διθύραμβον, ἔπεα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.23, 4.14; π. θεογονίην Ἕλλησι [prev. author] 2.53}}; π. Φαίδραν, Σατύρους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 153, 157}}; π. κωμῳδίαν, τραγῳδίαν, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 223d; παλινῳδίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.64, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 243b, etc.; ποιήματα [prev. author] “Phd.” 60d}}: _absolutely_, **write poetry, write as a poet,** ὀρθῶς π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.38; ἐν τοῖσι ἔπεσι π. [prev. author] 4.16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 534b}}: followed by a quotation, ἐπόησάς ποτε.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 193; εἴς τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61b; περὶ θεῶν [prev. author] “R.” 383a, etc.}}
**A.4.b** **represent in poetry,** Ὅμηρον Ἀχιλλέα πεποιηκέναι ἀμείνω Ὀδυσσέως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Mi.” 369c, compare 364c, “Smp.” 174b}}; ποιήσας τὸν Ἀχιλλέα λέγοντα **having represented** Achilles saying, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.105b, compare 25d, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 525d, 525e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1453b29.}}
**A.4.c** **describe in verse,** θεὸν ἐν ἔπεσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 379a}}; ἐποίησα μύθους τοὺς Αἰσώπου **put** them **into verse,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phd.” 61b; μῦθον 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 100.}}
**A.4.d** **invent,** καινοὺς θεούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 3b}}; ὑπὸ ποιητέω τινὸς ποιηθὲν [τοὔνομα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.115; πεποιημένα ὀνόματα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1404b29, compare “Po.” 1457b2}}; opposed to αὐτοφυῆ, κύρια, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Isaeo” 7, “Pomp.” 2.}}
**A.II** **bring about, cause,** τελευτήν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.250; γαλήνην 5.452; φόβον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.432; σιωπὴν παρὰ πάντων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.3.10; τέρψιν τοῖς θεωμένοις [prev. author] “Mem.” 3.10.8; αἰσχύνην τῇ πόλει 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.54, etc.}}; also of things, ἄνεμοι αὐτοὶ μὲν οὐχ ὁρῶνται· ἃ δὲ ποιοῦσι φανερά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.14; ταὐτὸν ἐποίει αὐτοῖς νικᾶν τε μαχομένοις καὶ μηδὲ μάχεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.6, compare 2.89.}}
**A.II.b** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **cause** or **bring about** that.., σε θεοὶ ποίησαν ἱκέσθαι [ἐς] οἶκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.258; π. τινὰ κλύειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 926; π. τινὰ βλέψαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 459, compare 746; π. τινὰ τριηραρχεῖν [prev. author] “Eq.” 912, compare “Av.” 59}}; π. τινὰ αἰσχύνεσθαι, κλάειν, ἀπορεῖν, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.48, 2.2.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 149a, etc.: with ὥστε inserted, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.29, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 351, etc.}}: followed by a _relative_ clause, π. ὅκως ἔσται ἡ Κύπρος ἐλευθέρη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.109, compare 1.209; ὡς ἂν.. εἰδείην ἐποίουν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.3.18:}} —also _middle_, ἐποιήσατο ὡς ἐν ἀσφαλεῖ εἶεν{{Refs|[prev. work] 6.1.23.}}
**A.II.2** **procure,** π. ἄδειάν τε καὶ κάθοδόν τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.76; ὁ νόμος π. τὴν κληρονομίαν τισί 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.1}}; λόγος ἀργύριον τῷ λέγοντι π. **gets** him money, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.76}}:—_middle_, **procure for oneself, gain,** κλέος αὐτῇ ποιεῖτ᾽ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.126; ἄδειαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.60; τιμωρίαν ἀπό τινων [prev. author] 1.25; τὸν βίον ἀπὸ γεωργίας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 6.11, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.5.}}
**A.II.3** of sacrifices, festivals, etc., **celebrate,** π. ἱρά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.19, compare 2.49 (_active_ and _passive_)}}; π. τὴν θυσίαν τῷ Ποσειδῶνι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.5.1}}; π. Ἴσθμια{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 4.5.2; τῇ θεῷ ἑορτὴν δημοτελῆ π. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.15; παννυχίδα π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 328a}}; π. σάββατα **observe** the Sabbath, {{LXX|LXX.Exo.31.16}}; π. ταφάς, of a public funeral, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 234b; π. ἐπαρήν “SIG” 38.30 (Teos, 5th c.BC)}}; also of political assemblies, π. ἐκκλησίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 746, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.139; π. μυστήρια [prev. author] 6.28 (_passive_)}}; ξύλλογον σφῶν αὐτῶν {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.67}}:—_middle_, ἀγορὴν ποιήσατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.2; ἢν θυσίην τις ποιῆται 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.57 (variant); δημοσίᾳ ταφὰς ἐποιήσαντο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.34; π. ἀγῶνα [prev. author] 4.91; π. ἐκκλησίαν τοῖς Γρᾳξὶ περὶ μισθοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 169.}}
**A.II.4** of war and peace, πόλεμον π. **cause** or **give rise to** a war, πόλεμον ἡμῖν ἀντ᾽ εἰρήνης πρὸς Αακεδαιμονίους π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.48}}; but π. ποιησόμενοι **about to make** war (on one's own part), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.5.24}}; εἰρήνην π. **bring about** a peace (for others), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1199; σπονδὰς π. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.14; ξυμμαχίαν ποιῆσαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.29}}; but εἰρήνην ποιεῖσθαι **make** peace (for oneself), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.11; σπονδὰς ποιήσασθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.28, etc.}}:—_passive_, ἐπεποίητο συμμαχίη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.77, etc.}}
**A.II.5** frequently in _middle_ with Nouns _periphrastic_ for the Verb derived from the Noun, μύθου ποιήσασθαι ἐπισχεσίην **submit** a plea, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.71}}; ποιέεσθαι ὁδοιπορίην, for ὁδοιπορέειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.29; π. ὁδόν [prev. author] 7.42, 110, 112, etc.}}; π. πλόον, for πλέειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.95, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.21}}; π. κομιδήν, for κομίζεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.95}}; θῶμα π. τὴν ἐργασίην, for θωμάζειν, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.68}}; ὀργὴν π., for ὀργίζεσθαι, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.25}}; λήθην π. τι, for λανθάνεσθαί τινος, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.127}}; βουλὴν π., for βουλεύεσθαι, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.101}}; συμβολὴν π., for συμβάλλεσθαι, {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.45}}; τὰς μάχας π., for μάχεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 302, etc.}}; καταφυγὴν π., for καταφεύγειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.4}}; ἀγῶνα π., for ἀγωνίζεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.89}}; π. λόγον [τινός] **make** account of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.156}}; but τοὺς λόγους π. **hold** a conference, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.128}}; also simply for λέγειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.2, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 527a, etc.}}; also π. δι᾽ ἀγγέλου, π. διὰ χρηστηρίων, **communicate** by a messenger, an oracle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.4, 8.134.}}
**A.III** with _adjective_ as _predicate_, **make, render** so and so, ποιῆσαί τινα ἄφρονα **make** one senseless, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.12}}; [δῶρα] ὄλβια ποιεῖν **make** them blest, i.e. prosper them,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|13.42, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.30; τοὺς Μήδους ἀσθενεῖς π. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.5.2, etc.; χρήσιμον ἐξ ἀχρήστου π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 411b: with a}} _substantive_, ποιῆσαι ἀθύρματα **make** into playthings, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.363; ποιεῖν τινα βασιλῆα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.387; ταμίην ἀνέμων 10.21; γέροντα 16.456; ἄκοιτίν τινι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.537; γαμβρὸν ἑόν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 818; [μύρμηκας] ἄνδρας π. [καὶ] γυναῖκας [prev. author] “fragment” 76.5; πολιήτας π. τινάς 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.156; Ἀθηναῖον π. τινά 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.29, etc.; π. τινὰ παράδειγμα 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.39}}: hence, **appoint, instal,** τὸν Μωϋσῆν καὶ τὸν Ἀαρών {{LXX+NT|LXX.1Ki.12.6; δώδεκα NT.Mark.3.14:—}}_middle_, ποιεῖσθαί τινα ἑταῖρον **make** him **one's** friend, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 707, compare 714}}; π. τινὰ ἄλοχον or ἄκοιτιν **take** her **to oneself** as wife, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.409, 9.397, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.120, etc.}}; π. τινὰ παῖδα **make** him **one's** son, i.e. **adopt** him as son, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.495, etc.}}; θετὸν παῖδα π. **adopt** a son, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.57: without υἱόν}} **adopt,** ἐπειδὴ οὐκ ἦσαν αὐτῷ παῖδες ἄρρενες, π. Λεωκράτη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 41.3, compare 39.6,33, 44.25, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 923c, etc.; π. τινὰ θυγατέρα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.180: generally, ἅπαντας ἢ σῦς ἠὲ λύκους π. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.433; π. τινὰ πολίτην 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.54; μαθητήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 428b; τὰ κρέα π. εὔτυκα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.119}}; τὰ ἔπεα ἀπόρρητα π. **making** them a secret, {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.45, etc.}}; also ἑωυτοῦ ποιέεται τὸ.. ἔργον **makes** it **his own,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.129; μηδ᾽ ἃ μὴ 'θιγες ποιοῦ σεαυτῆς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 547.}}
**A.IV** **put** in a certain place or condition, etc., ἐμοὶ Ζεὺς.. ἐνὶ φρεσὶν ὧδε νόημα ποίησ᾽ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.274; σφῶϊν ὧδε θεῶν τις ἐνὶ φρεσὶ ποιήσειεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.55; αἲ γὰρ τοῦτο θεοὶ ποιήσειαν ἐπὶ νόον νησιώτῃσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.27, compare 71; ἐν αἰσχύνῃ π. τὴν πόλιν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.136; τὰς ναῦς ἐπὶ τοῦ ξηροῦ π. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.109; ἔξω κεφαλὴν π. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.33; ἔξω βελῶν τὴν τάξιν π. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.1.3; ἐμαυτὸν ὡς πορρωτάτω π. τῶν ὑποψιῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.37}}; of troops, **form** them, ὡς ἂν κράτιστα.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.11, compare 3.4.21}}; in politics, ἐς ὀλίγους τὰς ἀρχὰς π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.53}}; and in war, π. Γετταλίαν ὑπὸ Φιλίππῳ **bring** it under his power, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.48; μήτε τοὺς νόμους μήθ᾽ ὑμᾶς αὐτοὺς ἐπὶ τοῖς λέγουσι π. [prev. author] 58.61}}:—_middle_, ποιέεσθαι ὑπ᾽ ἑωυτῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.201, compare 5.103, etc.; ὑπὸ χεῖρα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 1.22}}; π. τινὰς ἐς φυλακήν, τὰ τῶν ξυμμάχων ἐς ἀσφάλειαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.3, 8.1; τινὰς ἐς τὸ συμμαχικόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.106}}; τὰ λεπτὰ πλοῖα ἐντὸς π. **put** the small vessels in the middle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.83, compare 6.67}}; π. τινὰ ἐκποδών (see. ἐκποδών); ὄπισθεν π. τὸν ποταμόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.10.9.}}
**A.IV.2** in Mathematics texts, **multiply,** π. τὰ ιβ ἐπὶ τὰ έ, τὰ ζ ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτὰ π., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Metrica” 1.8, 2.14.}}
**A.V** _middle_, **deem, consider, reckon** a thing as.., συμφορὴν ποιέεσθαί τι **take** it for a misfortune, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.83, 6.61}}; δεινὸν π. τι **esteem** it a grievous thing, **take** it ill, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.127, etc.}} (rarely in _active_, δεινὰ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2.121.έ, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.42)}}; μέγα π. with _infinitive_, **deem** it a great matter that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.3, compare 3.42, etc.; μεγάλα π. ὅτι.. [prev. author] 1.119}}; ἑρμαῖον π. τι **count** it clear gain, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 489c; οὐκέτι ἀνασχετὸν π. τι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.118: frequently with Preps., δι᾽ οὐδενὸς π.}} **deem** of no account, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 584}}; ἐν ἐλαφρῷ, ἐν ὁμοίῳ π., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.118, 7.138; ἐν σμικρῷ μέρει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 498; ἐν ὀλιγωρίᾳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.5; ἐν ὀργῇ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.16}}; ἐν νόμῳ π. **consider** as lawful, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.131}}; ἐν ἀδείῃ π. **consider** as safe, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.42; παρ᾽ ὀλίγον π. τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.6.11}}; περὶ πολλοῦ π., Latin _magni facere,_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.1, etc.}}; περὶ πλείονος, περὶ πλείστου π., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 14.40, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 21e, etc.}}; περὶ ὀλίγου, περὶ ἐλάττονος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.58, 18.63; περὶ παντός [prev. author] 2.15 (rarely πολλοῦ π. τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 328d)}}; πρὸ πολλοῦ π. with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.138.}}
**A.VI** **put the case, assume** that.., ποιήσας ἀν᾽ ὀγδώκοντα ἄνδρας ἐνεῖναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.184, compare 186, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.7.9}}: without _infinitive_, ἐν ἑκάστῃ ψυχῇ ποιήσωμεν περιστερεῶνά τινα (i.e. εἶναι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 197d}}:—_passive_, πεποιήσθω δή **be it assumed** then, {{Refs|[prev. work]e}}; οἱ φιλοσοφώτατοι ποιούμενοι **those who are reputed..,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 498a, compare 538c, 573b:—but for τὸν φιλόσοφον ποιώμεθα νομίζειν [prev. work] 581d}} read τί οἰώμεθα..;
**A.VII** of Time, οὐ π. χρόνον **make** no long time, i. e. not to delay, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.163 codices}}; μακρότερον ποιεῖς you **are taking** too long, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 48.4 (3rd c.BC)}}; μέσας π. νύκτας **let** midnight **come,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 50d, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 11.85 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus)}}; ἔξω μέσων νυκτῶν π. τὴν ὥραν **put off** the time of business to past midnight, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.26}}; τὴν νύκτα ἐφ᾽ ὅπλοις ποιεῖσθαι **spend** it under arms, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.28 (assuming variant); ποιήσουσιν ἐν πλούτῳ ἔτη πολλά LXX.Pro.13.23, cf. LXX.Tob.10.7; δύο ἡμέρας ποιεῖ ἐν τῷ Ἀνουβιείῳ “UPZ” 70.21 (2nd c.BC),compare “PSI” 4.362.15 (3rd c.BC)}}; τὰς ἡμέρας ἐν τοῖς ὕδασι π. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.35}}; **tarry, stay,** μῆνας τρεῖς {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Act.20.3, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 11.330 (1st c.AD: Nicarchus Epigrammaticus).}}
**A.VIII** in later Greek, **sacrifice,** μοσχάριον {{LXX|LXX.Exo.29.36}}; καρπώσεις ὑπέρ τινος{{LXX|LXX.Job.42.8: without _accusative_, π. Ἀστάρτῃ}} **sacrifice** to Ashtoreth,{{LXX|LXX.1Ki.11.33.}}
**A.IX** **make ready, prepare,** as food, μοσχάριον{{LXX|LXX.Gen.18.7f}}; π. τὸν μύστακα **trim** it,{{LXX|LXX.2Ki.19.24(25).}}
**A.X** ποιεῖν βασιλέα **play** the king,{{LXX|LXX.3Ki.20 (21). 7.}}
**B** **do,** much like{πράσσω, οὐδὲν ἂν ὧν νυνὶ πεποίηκεν ἔπραξεν} {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.5; περὶ ὧν πράττει καὶ μέλλει ποιεῖν [prev. author] 8.2, compare 18.62; ἄριστα πεποίηται 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.56; πλείονα χρηστὰ περὶ τὴν πόλιν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 811; τὰ δίκαια τοῖς εὐεργέταις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.12; ἅμα ἔπος τε καὶ ἔργον ἐποίεε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134 near the end}}; ποιέειν Σπαρτιητικά **act** like a Spartan, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.40; οὗτος τί ποιεῖ; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 911, etc.; τὸ προσταχθὲν π. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1010}}; π. τὴν μουσικήν **practise** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 60e, etc.}}; πᾶν or πάντα π., see at {πᾶς} {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 111.2, etc.: in Mathematics texts, ὅπερ ἔδει ποιῆσαι, = “Q.E.F.”, 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 1.1, etc.}}
**B.2** with _double_ _accusative_, **do** something to another, κακά or ἀγαθὰ ποιεῖν τινα, first in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.75, +others}}; ἀγαθόν, κακὸν π. τινά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 16.50, etc.; μεγάλα τὴν πόλιν ἀγαθά 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.17; also εὖ ποιεῖν τὸν εὖ ποιοῦντα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.8}}; τὴν ἐκείνου (i.e. χώραν) κακῶς π. {{LXX+5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.18; in LXX with _preposition_, π. κακὸν μετά τινων LXX.Gen.26.29; ταῦτα τοῦτον ἐποίησα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.115; κοὐκ οἶδ᾽ ὅ τι χρῆμά με ποιεῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 697, compare “Nu.” 259}}; also of things, ἀργύριον τωὐτὸ τοῦτο ἐποίεε he **did** this same thing with silver, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.166: less frequently with _dative_ _person_, τῷ τεθνεῶτι μηδὲν τῶν νομιζομένων π. 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.19; ἵππῳ τἀναντία 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 9.12 codices, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 388, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.37: with _dative_ _of things_, τί ποιήσωμεν κιβωτ; LXX.1Ki.5.8}}:—in _middle_, φίλα ποιέεσθαί τισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.152, 5.37.}}
**B.3** with an _adverb_, ὧδε ποίησον **do** thus, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.112}}; πῶς ποιήσει; how **will you act?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 652; πῶς δεῖ ποιεῖν περὶ θυσίας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.1; ποίει ὅπως βούλει [prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.4.9; μὴ ἄλλως π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 328d; πρὸς τοὺς πολεμίους πῶς ποιήσουσι; [prev. work] 469b; ὀρθῶς π. [prev. work] 403e; εὖ, κακῶς π. τινά, see above 2}}: frequently with _participle_, εὖ ἐποίησας ἀπικόμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.24, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 60c; καλῶς ποιεῖς προνοῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.4.13; οἷον ποιεῖς ἡγούμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 166c}}; καλῶς ποιῶν almost Adverbial, καλῶς γ᾽, ἔφη, ποιῶν σύ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Smp.” 174e; καλῶς ποιοῦντες.. πράττετε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.110, compare 1.28; εὖ ποιοῦν}}**fortunately,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 23.143.}}
**B.4** in Prose (rarely in Poetry, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 935), used in the second clause, to avoid repeating the Verb of the first, ἐρώτησον αὐτούς· μᾶλλον δ᾽ ἐγὼ τοῦθ᾽ ὑπὲρ σοῦ ποιήσω}} I will **do** this for you, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.52, compare 292, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.97, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.35.}}
**B.II** _absolutely_, **to be doing, act,** ποιέειν ἢ παθεῖν πρόκειται ἀγών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.11}}; ποιεῖν, as a category, opposed to πάσχειν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 2a3, compare “GC” 322b11, “Ph.” 225b13.}}
**B.II.b** of medicine, **operate, be efficacious,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 117b; λουτρὰ κάλλιστα ποιοῦντα πρὸς νόσους 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 5.3.6}}; πρὸς στραγγουρίαν, πρὸς τοὺς δαιμονιζομένους, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 7.14.1, Pseudo-Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fluv.” 16.2: frequently in 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus, πρὸς ἐπιλημπτικούς 1.6, +others; εἰς τὰ αὐτά 2.133: with _dative_, στομαχικοῖς 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.183: _absolutely_, ἄκρως π. [prev. work]265}}; also of charms, {{Refs|“PMag.Osl.” 1.361.}}
**B.II.2** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus has a peculiar usage, ἡ εὔνοια παρὰ πολὺ ἐποίει μᾶλλον ἐς τοὺς Λακεδαιμονίους}} good-will **made** greatly for, on the side of, the L., {{Refs|2.8: _impersonal_, ἐπὶ πολὺ ἐποίει τῆς δόξης τοῖς μὲν ἠπειρώταις εἶναι, τοῖς δέ.. it was the general character of the one to be landsmen, of the others.., 4.12}}: the former passage is imitated by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.3, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.82, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 57.6.}}
**C** _middle_, ={προσποιέομαι}, **pretend,** with _infinitive_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Zosimus Epigrammaticus 2.14.1, 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “Historia Arcana” 10. (ποιϝέω perhaps from *ποι-ϝό-ς (in κλινοποιός, νεωποιός, τραπεζοποιός, etc.)}} 'builder', 'maker', cf. Sanskrit _cinóti, cáyati_ 'arrange in order', 'build'.)

4161:
** ποί-ημα**, ατος, τό,
(ποιέω) **anything made** or **done**: hence,
**I** **work,** π. χρύσεα, χάλκεα καὶ σιδήρεα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.5, 7.84, compare 2.135; Γλαύκου τοῦ Χίου π. [prev. author] 1.25}}; of the works of Daedalus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 97e}}; π. ἐραστοῦ a lover's **invention,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 474e}}; **product,** of land formed by silting-up of rivers, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.4 (_plural_).}}
**I.2** **poem,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 186, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 60d, “Ly.” 221d; τὰ μετὰ μέτρου π. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.7, 15.45; π. εἰς τὰς Μούσας “IG” 7.1773.17 (Thespiae, 2nd c.AD)}}: _plural_, of single **verses,** ={ἔπη}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.41, “Comp.” 3.}}
**I.2.b** **poetical,** especially **metrical, form,** περὶ ποιήματος, title of work by Hephaestio.
**I.3** **fiction,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.5 (_plural_).}}
**I.4** **onomatopoeic word,** [{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus see at {μάματα}.}}]
**II** **deed, act,** opposed to πάθημα, {{LXX+6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 437b (_plural_), “Sph.” 248b; π. πονηρά LXX.2Esd.9.13. (Written ποιϝήματα (in signification 1.1) in “Mnemos.” 57.208 (Argos, 6th c.BC).)}}

4162:
** ποί-ησις**, εως, ἡ,
**fabrication, creation, production,** opposed to πρᾶξις (**action,** see {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1140a2, “Pol.” 1254a5), [μύρου] 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.22; νεῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.2, etc.; ἡ τῶν ζῴων π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 197a; ἡ τῶν μελῶν π. [prev. author] “Grg.” 449d; μίμησις π. τίς ἐστιν, εἰδώλων μέντοι [prev. author] “Sph.” 265b, etc.; αἱ ὑπὸ πάσαις ταῖς τέχναις ἐργασίαι ποιήσεις εἰσί [prev. author] “Smp.” 205b.}}
**2** of Poetry, ἡ τῶν διθυράμβων π., τῆς τραγῳδίας, τῶν ἐπῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 145.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 502a, 502b, “R.” 394c}}: _absolutely_, **art of poetry,** οἱ ἐν π. γενόμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.82, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 868, etc.; οὕτως.. ἀταλαιπώρως ἡ π. διέκειτο [prev. author] “fragment” 254}}; οἱ ἄκροι τῆς π. ἑκατέρας, i.e. tragedy and comedy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 152e; ᾠδαὶ καὶ ἡ ἄλλη π. [prev. author] “Phdr.” 245a; π. ψιλὴ ἢ ἐν ᾠδῇ [prev. work] 278c.}}
**2.b** **poetic composition, poem,** ἐς ποίησιν ἐσενείκασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.23, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.10, etc.; περὶ ὧν Ὅμηρος τὴν π. πεποίηκεν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 531d: _plural_, [prev. author] “Lg.” 829e.}}
**II** ={εἰσποίησις}, **adoption,** in _plural_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.1, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 44.7, +others}}; κατὰ ποίησιν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. passage], “Michel” 836.5 (Didyma, 2nd c.BC)}}; ποιήσει υἱοί {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.7; τῇ παρ᾽ ὑμῶν π. πολίτης 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.30.}}
**II.2** in _collective_ sense, **those adopted,** τῆς π. ἣν ἐκεῖνος ἐποιήσατο {{Refs|[prev. author] 44.61.}}
**III** **method of procedure,** in Magic, {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.1248.}}

4163:
** ποι-ητής**, οῦ, ὁ,
**maker,** μηχανημάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author = 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus] “Cyr.” 1.6.38; κλίνης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 597d; τὸν π. καὶ πατέρα τοῦδε τοῦ παντός [prev. author] “Ti.” 28c}}; ζῴων, of the painter, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sph.” 234a}}; **inventor,** θεῶν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Euthphr.” 3b; νόμων}}**lawgiver,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Def.” 415b.}}
**2** **workman**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.388.8, +others (3rd c.BC, _plural_).}}
**II** **composer of a poem, author,** π. κωμῳδίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 935e; π. καινῶν δραμάτων, τραγῳδιῶν κτλ. “SIG” 1079.2, +others (5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Mae., 2nd-1st c.BC)}}: _absolutely_, **poet,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.53, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 96, 1030, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 534b, etc.}}; Homer was called ὁ π., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 485d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1365a11, 1380b28, Teles p.34 H., 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.21.3, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.1.10, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 26.19, etc.}}; so also Hesiod, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 901a; and others, [prev. author] “Thg.” 125e, 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 78.44.}}
**II.b** **composer** of music, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 812d.}}
**II.2** **author** of a speech, opposed to deliverer of it, π. λόγων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Euthd.” 305b, compare “Phdr.” 234e, 278e, 4th c.BC: Alcidamas Rhetor “περὶ σοφιστῶν” 34, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.192. (Written ποητής “IG” 22.2319.65, “SIG” 711 L 35 (Delph., 2nd c.BC), etc.)}}

4164:
** ποικίλος** [ῐ], η, ον,
**many-coloured, spotted, pied, dappled,** παρδαλέη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.30; ὄφις 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 300 codices (αἰόλον Scholia)}}; δράκων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.46}}; ἴυγξ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 4.214; νεβρίδες 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 249; ὄρνιθες 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.4.29}}; also of cattle, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Preis.” 37.9 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; -ώτερον ταὧ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 110.14, compare 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 9.397c}}; opposed to ὁμόχρους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 543a25; κιθῶνες 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.61}}; λίθος Αἰθιοπικὸς π., of the red granite of Syene, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.127, compare “IG” 42(1).106i96, 113(Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; σφαῖρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 110b; in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.4.32}}, **tattooed.**
**II** **wrought in various colours,** of woven or embroidered stuffs, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus as epithet of πέπλος, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.735, +others; ἱμάς 14.215; φᾶρος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 586}}; ἐν ποικίλοις.. κάλλεσιν βαίνειν, of a rich carpet, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 923; ποικίλα, τά, [prev. work] 926, 936, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.78}}; π., τό, **a broidered robe,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 38; ἐπίβλημα π. “IG” 12.387.28}}; of Cyprian, Carthaginian, and Sicilian stuffs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 611, 5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 63.23, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 76.4}}. _adverb_ -λως, ὑφασμένον {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 99 (uncertain); στρωμναὶ π. διηνθισμέναι LXX.Est.1.6.}}
**II.2** of metal work, τεύχεα π. χαλκῷ **cunningly wrought** in bronze, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” [423]; θώρηξ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.134; τεύχεα, ἔντεα, σάκος, δίφρος, κλισμός, etc., 4.432, 10.75, 149, 501, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.132, etc.; but δεσμὸς π.}}**intricate,** {{Refs|8.448.}}
**II.3** ἡ στοὰ ἡ π. the {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Painted” Hall at Athens, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.186; ἡ π. στοά 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.17, 59.94, compare 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.15.1}}; also Ποικίλη alone, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 5.11.6, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMeretr.” 10.2}}; or ἡ Π., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pisc.” 13, 16, etc.}}; also π. στοά, at Olympia, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.21.17}}; λέσχη π., at Sparta, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.15.8; θρᾶνος π. “PCair.Zen.” 445.5 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II.4** of drugs, **complicated,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “χρονίων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.4.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, **changeful, diversified, manifold,** εὐμορφία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 495; π. κακῶν ταμιεῖον 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 149; -ώτερος αὐτοῦ Πρωτέως 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sacr.” 5; ποικίλα ἀντὶ ἁπλοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 146d; -ώτερα ποιεῖν τὰ νοσήματα [prev. author] “R.” 426a}}; παντοδαπὰς ἡδονὰς καὶ π. καὶ παντοίως ἐχούσας{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 559d; οὕτω δὲ π. τί ἐστι τὸ ἀγαθὸν καὶ παντοδαπόν [prev. author] “Prt.” 334b; πηδήσεις ὡς ἔνι -ωτάτας ποιεῖσθαι 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 43.3}}; π. μῆνες the **changing** months, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).18 (nisi to be read ποικίλα [χθών]); π. εὐεργεσία “IG” 5(2).268.22 (Mantinea, 1st c.BC)}}. _adverb_ **-λως** **in various ways,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 33, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.91: _comparative_ -ωτέρως, θρεπτέον}} Herod._middle_ in {{Refs|“Rh.Mus.” 58.85}} but **-ώτερον** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “Vit.Hp.” 4.}}
**III.2** of Art, π. ὕμνος a song **of changeful strain** or **full of diverse art,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.87; ποικίλον κιθαρίζων [prev. author] “N.” 4.14; δεδαιδαλμένοι ψεύδεσι ποικίλοις μῦθοι [prev. author] “O.” 1.29}}; of style, λέξις ποιητικωτέρα καὶ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.47 (_comparative_)}}; σχηματισμοί {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Isaeo” 3.}}
**III.3** **intricate, complex,** εἱλιγμοὶ -ώτατοι, of a labyrinth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.148}}; of an oracle, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.111 (_comparative_)}}; ὁ θεὸς ἔφυ τι π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 711}}; π. νόμος, opposed to νοῆσαι ῥᾴδιος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 182b}}; π. μηχάνημα, λόγοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 762, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 438}}; opposed to ἁπλούστερος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1416b25; οὐδὲν π. οὐδὲ σοφόν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.37.}} _adverb_ -λως, αὐδώμενος speaking **in double sense,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 130; π. ᾐνιγμένος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 196.}}
**III.3.b** of abstruse knowledge, **intricate, subtle,** εἰδέναι τι π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 300}}; οὐδὲν π. nothing **abstruse** or **difficult,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 75e, “Grg.” 491d, etc.}}
**III.3.c** of persons and things, **subtle, artful, wily,** of Prometheus, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 511, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 310}}; of Odysseus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 526; π. γὰρ ἁνήρ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 758; φύσει π. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.18.4; ἀλώπηξ κερδαλέα καὶ π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 365c}}; π. λαλήματα, of the Sirens, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 937; π. τόξον 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 9.43; βουλεύματα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.28}}. _adverb_ **subtly, artfully,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 888 (Lyric poetry); σοφῶς.. καὶ π. 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 110.20; π. χρώμενοι τοῖς πράγμασιν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.30.7.}}
**III.4** **changeable, unstable,** ὁ εὐδαίμων οὐ π. καὶ εὐμετάβολος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1101a8}}; π. ἐλπίδες **doubtful** hopes, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 14.1.5; π. περιστάσεις “OGI” 194.5 (Egypt, 1st c.BC).}} _adverb_ -λως, ἔχειν to be **different,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.6.21; δέος π. περιαμύττον τὸν νοῦν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 365c: _comparative_ -ωτέρως uncertain in 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 5G.}} (Cf. Sanskrit _pimśáti_ 'dress (meat)', 'adorn', **péśas** 'shape', 'colour', 'embroidery', Lithuanian _pi[etilde]šti_ 'draw', 'write', Slavonic _p[icaron]sati_ 'write'.)

4165:
** ποιμαίνω**,
_Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ ποιμαίνεσκεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.188}}: (ποιμήν):—**herd, tend,** μῆλα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” [same place]; ἄρνας 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 23; ποίμνας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 26, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.1004}}; πρόβατα variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 345c}}; ποιμαίνειν ἐπ᾽ ὄεσσι **to be shepherd** over sheep, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.25, 11.106}}: _absolutely_, **act as shepherd, tend flocks,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 174d, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.65; ἐν τοῖς ἄλσεσιν μὴ π. “SIG” 986.3 (Chios, 5th-4th c.BC)}}:—_passive_, **to be herded, roam the pastures,** of flocks, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.245, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 579 (Lyric poetry): _metaphorically_, ἀτρεκέων ποιμαίνεται ἔθνος ὀνείρων 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 2.5 (unless}}_middle_, with ὕπνος (l. {{Refs|3) as subject).}}
**2** πᾶς πεποίμανται τόπος every country **has been traversed** (as by a shepherd or flocks of sheep), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 249.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **tend, cherish,** ζωᾶς ἄωτον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).12; ἱκέτην 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 91; τὸ σῶμα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 209a; θεσμόν “Anthologia Graeca” 12.99.}}
**II.2** **guide, govern,** στρατόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 744; μάλα καὶ κατόπιν ἡμᾶς ἐποίμαινον αὖραι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 6.}}
**II.3** **soothe, beguile,** ἔρωτα π. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.80; ὀνομάτων κομψεύμασι τοὺς ἀμαθεῖς π. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 54}}: hence, generally, **deceive,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 153 (Lyric poetry, codex M and Scholia for πημαίνει).}}
**II.4** εἴδωλα ἃ ποιμαίνουσιν images which they **send flocking,** i.e. represent as flocking, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.420b.}}

4166:
** ποιμήν**, ένος, ὁ,
_vocative_ ποιμήν (not -μέν) Hdn.Gr. 2.717:— **herdsman,** whether of sheep or oxen, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.82 - 5, +others}}; opposed to lord or owner (ἄναξ), {{Refs|4.87.}}
**2** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always **shepherd,** βουκόλοι καὶ π. {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 714, compare 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 281, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 174d, “R.” 343a, “Lg.” 735b, etc.; π. προβάτων LXX.Gen.4.2.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **shepherd** of the people, regularly of Agamemnon, Ἀγαμέμνονα π. λαῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.243, +others}}: generally, **captain, chief,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 85, +others, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 360 codices (Lyric poetry); ναῶν ποιμένες 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 767; λόχων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1140; ὄχων [prev. author] “Supp.” 674}}; ποιμένες δώρων Κυπρίας, of the Loves, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.6}}: _absolutely_, **master, lord,** πλοῦτος ὁ λαχὼν ποιμένα [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “O.” 10(11).88; for 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 657, see at {στρόβος}.}}]
**II.2** in {{LXX+NT|LXX and “NT, pastor, teacher, LXX.Jer.2.8, NT.Eph.4.11, etc.}} (Cf.Lithuanian _piemuõ,_ _genitive_ _singular_ **piemeñs** 'shepherd'.)

4167:
** ποίμν-η**, ἡ,
**flock,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.122}}; properly **of sheep,** βουκολίας ἀγέλας τε καὶ αἰπόλια πλατἔ αἰγῶν ποίμνας τ᾽ είροπόκων ὀΐων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 446; τά τε αἰπόλια καὶ τὰς π. καὶ τὰ βουκόλια 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 653, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 415e, etc.: generally, ποῖμναι κάπρων λεόντων τε 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 238}}; of a single animal, χρυσεόμαλλος π., of the ram with golden fleece, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 725 (Lyric poetry), compare 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 52.4.}}
**2** _metaphorically_ of persons, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 642 (Lyric poetry, of the Furies), “Eu.” 197}}; ποίμνη δίπους, of mankind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 267c.}}

4168:
** ποίμν-ιον**, τό,
={ποίμνη}, especially **of sheep,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.2, 3.65 (variant), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 761, 1028, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 416a, etc.}}; of goats, {{Refs|“IG” 12.45.3 (uncertain)}}: _plural_, **head of cattle,** π. τρισχίλια {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.25.2.}}
**II** _metaphorically_ of disciples, {{NT|NT.Luke.12.32, +others; π. Θεοῦ NT.1Pet.5.2.}}

4169:
** ποῖος**, α, ον,
_Ionic dialect_ **κοῖος**, η, ον, **of what kind?** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus commonly expressing surprise and anger, π. τὸν μῦθον ἔειπες}} **what manner of** speech hast thou spoken! {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.552, +others; ποῖόν σε ἔπος φύγεν ἕρκος ὀδόντων 4.350, +others; simply, ποῖον ἔειπες 13.824, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.85, +others; ποῖον ἔρεξας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.570}}; ποῖοί κ᾽ εἶτ᾽ Ὀδυσῆϊ ἀμυνέμεν **what sort** would ye be to.. ! {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.195}}; in simple questions, ποίῃ.. νηΐ σε ναῦται ἤγαγο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|16.222; κοίῃ χειρ; 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.155, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 304 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; ποῖος οὐ _interrogative_, equivalent to **every** affirmative, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.21, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 420, etc.}}
**2** frequently in Comedy texts and Prose dialogue, used in repeating a word used by the former speaker, to express scornful surprise, Πρωτέως τάδ᾽ ἐστὶ μέλαθρα. Answ. ποίου Πρωτέω; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 874, compare “Ach.” 62, 157, 761, “Nu.” 367, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 180b, “Grg.” 490e, “Chrm.” 174b: twice in Trag., 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 427, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 567: with _Article_, τὰν ποίαν σύριγγ; 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.5: abbreviation ποῖ (which see).}}
**3** with the _Article_, when the question implies a Noun which is defined by the _Article_ or the context, τὸ π. εὑρὼν.. φάρμακο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 251; τὰ π. τρύχ; μῶν ἐν οἷς..; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 418}}; λέγεις δὲ τὴν π. κατάστασιν ὀλιγαρχία; Answ. τὴν ἀπὸ τιμημάτων πολιτείαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 550c}}: frequently with the _demonstrative_, ὁ ποῖος οὗτος Λάμαχο; Answ. ὁ δεινός, ὁ ταλαύρινος.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 963, compare “Nu.” 1270, 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 12.4 (corr. Elmsley)}}: sometimes the answer is given more generally, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 120, 291, “OC” 1415, “Ph.” 1229: in Prose, τὸ π.; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 220e, etc.; τὸ π. δ; [prev. author] “Tht.” 147d, “Phdr.” 279a; τὰ π. ταῦτ; [prev. author] “Cra.” 395d, etc.; τῆς π. μερίδος γενέσθαι τὴν πόλιν ἐβούλετ᾽ ἄ; 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.64}}: so also without the _Article_, κοῖα ταῦτα λέγει; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.48}}; π. Ἐρινὺν τήνδε..; **what sort of** Fury is this that..? {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1119}}; π. ἐρεῖς τόδ᾽ ἔπο; **what sort of** word is this that thou wilt speak? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1204 (Lyric poetry), compare 441, etc.}}
**4** ποῖός τι; making the question less definite, κοῖόν μέ τινα νομίζουσι Πέρσαι εἶνα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.34, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1163, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.1.6, etc.: with _Article_, τὰ ποῖ᾽ ἄττ; [prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.3.8, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 240c.}}
**II** like{ὁποῖος}, in indirect questions, διδάξω.. ποῖα χρὴ λέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 519, compare “Pr.” 196, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 153 (Lyric poetry), etc.; ποῖα ἄττα δεῖ ἡμᾶς λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 398c}}; οὐκ οἶδα ὁποίᾳ τόλμῃ ἢ ποίοις λόγοις χρώμενος ἐρῶ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 414d; εἴρετο.. κοῖός τις δοκέοι ἀνὴρ εἶναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.34; doubled, ποίαν χρὴ [γυναῖκα] ποίῳ ἀνδρὶ συνοῦσαν τίκτειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 149d.}}
**III** ={ποδαπός}; ποῖος οὑτοσὶ [ὁ] Τιμόθεο;—Μιλήσιός τις.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 145.20, compare 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 36.}}
**III.2** **whose?** ἐν π. δυνάμει ἢ ἐν π. ὀνόματ; {{NT|NT.Act.4.7, cf. NT.Matt.21.23.}}
**IV** simply, **what, which?** especially of place or time, ποίης ἐξ εὔχεται εἶναι γαίη; {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.406, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.97; ἐν π. πόλε; 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 23 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, compare 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 267.6; ἐκ ποίας πόλεως σὺ ε; LXX.2Ki.15.2, cf. LXX.1Ki.13.12, +others, NT.Act.23.34}}; ποίᾳ ἄλλῃ (i.e. ὁδῷ); by **what** other way? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1219 (hence κοίῃ}} _metaphorically_, **how?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30)}}; ποίου χρόνο; since **what** time? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 278, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 815 (nisi to be read πόσον); ἀπὸ π. χρόνο; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 920, “UPZ” 65.7 (2nd c.BC)}}; ἀπὸ ποίου ἔτους {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PAmh.” 2.68.7 (1st c.AD)}}; ποίᾳ ἡμέρ; {{NT+4th c.BC+|NT.Matt.24.42, compare 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Epitaphius” 31, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 5a20, 22, “SIG” 826 E ii28 (Delph., 2nd c.BC), “IG” 5(1).1390.113 (Andania, 1st c.BC), “PUniv.Giss.” 20.18 (2nd c.AD)}}; φυλᾶς ἑλομένοις ἑκάστου (={-ῳ}) ποίας κε βέλλειτει (ἧστινος ἂν βούληται, i.e. εἶναι) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 9(2).517.20 (Larissa, 3rd c.BC)}}; ποίας φυλῆς ἐστι {{LXX|LXX.Tob.5.8}}; π., = **quis,** {{Refs|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}}
**V** ={πότερος}, {{Refs|“Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 1.284.}}
**VI** _adverb_ ποίως Hdn.Gr.2.925, {{Refs|Bacchius Musicus “Εἰσαγωγὴ τέχνης μουσικῆς” 93}}. [The first syllable is sometimes short in Trag. and Comedy texts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 911, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1369.]}}

4170:
** πολεμέω**,
_Ionic dialect_ Iterat. πολεμέεσκε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Acusilaus Historicus 22J.}}: _future_ **-ήσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.5}}: _perfect_ πεπολέμηκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1396a11, 4th c.BC: Ephippus Comicus 9:}} —_middle_, _aorist_ ἐπολεμησάμην (κατ-) {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.31.6}}:—_passive_, πολεμηθήσομαι {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.41.14, etc.}}; πολεμήσομαι in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.68, 8.43, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.110}}: _aorist_ ἐπολεμήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.26:}}_perfect_ πεπολέμημαι (κατα-) {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.16}}: (πόλεμος):— **to be at war** or **make war,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.140, etc.; ἀφ᾽ ἡσυχίας πολεμῆσαι [prev. work] 124}}; opposed to εἰρήνην ἄγειν, {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.76}}; τινι **with** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.37, “IG” 22.236.19, etc.; πολεμοῦσαι πρὸς ἀλλήλας πόλεις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 5.8, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 686b, “SIG” 182.12 (Argos, 4th c.BC), etc.}}; μετά τινος or σύν τινι in conjunction with.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.27, “An.” 2.6.5; περὶ τῆς ἀρχῆς π. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.98.}}
**2** **fight, do battle,** ἀπὸ τῶν ἵππων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 350a}}; ἀπὸ [καμήλων] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.49}}; but ἀφ᾽ ὅτου πολεμήσωμεν what **our means of war are,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.16.}}
**3** generally, **quarrel, wrangle with** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.11; π. χρείᾳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 191 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1386; τισὶν ὑπέρ τινος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.31: _metaphorically_ of disease, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.103.}}
**II** later with _accusative_, **make war upon,** τὴν πόλιν {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.36 codices; τὰς Ἀθήνας 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.61, compare 13.84, 14.37, LXX.1Mac.5.30, etc.; τὰς Συρακούσας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.15.10, etc.: _metaphorically_, τὰς σταφυλάς 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.22}}:—_passive_, also in early writers, **have war made upon** one, **to be treated as enemies,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.37, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.4.20; ὑπό τινων 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.49; καὶ αὐτοὶ.. ἐκ πολλοῦ πολεμούμενοι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.43; αὐτὸς μὲν πολεμεῖν ὑμῖν, ὑφ᾽ ὑμῶν δὲ μὴ πολεμεῖσθαι [prev. author] 9.9; -ηθείσης τῆς χώρας “OGI” 748.8 (Cyzicus, 3rd c.BC).}}
**II.2** with _accusative_ _cognate_, πόλεμον π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 551d}}, Arist.[same place], etc.:—_passive_, [πολέμους] τοὺς ἐπὶ Θησέως πολεμηθέντας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.10; κατὰ θάλατταν ὁ π. ἐπολεμεῖτο [prev. author] “HG” 5.1.1, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 600a}}; ὅσα ἐπολεμήθη whatever **hostilities took place,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.1.1; τὰ περὶ Πύλον κατὰ κράτος ἐπολεμεῖτο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.23, compare 3.6.}} —The form used by Poets is πολεμίζω.

4171:
** πόλεμος**,
and _Epic dialect_ **πτόλεμος**, ὁ, **war,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.61, etc. (the usual meaning in post-Homeric Greek)}}; also, **battle, fight,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 226}}, etc.; even of **single combat,**{{Refs|7.174; πόλεμοί τε μάχαι τε 1.177, 5.891; φυλόπιδος.. καὶ πολέμοιο 18.242; ἀϋτήν τε πτόλεμόν τε 1.492, compare 14.37, 96}}; π. καὶ δηϊοτῆτος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5.348, etc.: periphrastic, νεῖκος, φύλοπις, ἔρις πολέμοιο, 13.271, 635, 17.253; π. ἄγριος, αἱματόεις, ἀργαλέος, ἀλίαστος, δακρυόεις, δήϊος, δυσηλεγής, δυσηχής, κακός, λευγαλέος, ὀϊζυρός, ὀκρυόεις, ὀλοός, ὁμοίιος, πευκεδανός, πολυᾶϊξ, πολύδακρυς, στυγερός, φθισήνωρ, [prev. work] 737, 19.313, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.531, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.797, 5.737, 7.119, 20.154, 2.686, 1.284, 13.97, 3.112, 9.64 (to be read κρυόεντος), 3.133, 9.440, 10.8, 1.165, 3.165, 4.240, 9.604; π. Ἀχαιῶν, ἀνδρῶν, i.e. brought by them, 3.165, 24.8 (_plural_), etc.}}; ὁ τῶν βαρβάρων π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.24; Ἑλλήνων π. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.22; ὁ παρὼν π. Κορινθίων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.32}}; ὁ μέλλων καὶ ὅσον οὐ παρὼν π.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 36; ὁ πρὸς Δαρεῖον π. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.2; ἀσχημοσύνῃ καὶ Ἔρωτι πρὸς ἀλλήλους ἀεὶ π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 196a}}; Δωριακὸς π. Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.54; ὁ Ἰωνικὸς π. [prev. author] 8.11; ὁ Φωκικὸς π. 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.148; π. Δεκελεικός 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.37, 14.31; π. ξενικός 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1272b20; δουλικοὶ π. 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 6.272f; ἱερὸς π. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 556, etc.}}; πόλεμον ἄρασθαι levy **war,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 342, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 913, etc.: with _dative_, ἢ τοῖσιν ἢ τοῖς π. αἴρεσθαι μέγαν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 439; π. ἄρασθαι πρός τινας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.45; π. θέσθαι τινί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 13; π. ἀναιρέεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.36, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.7, etc.}}; π. κινεῖν, ἐγεῖραι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 3.5.3; π. τοῖς ἔργοις ἐξενήνοχε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 11.20, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.829e; ἐς π. καθίστασθαί τισι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1168; π. ἐπαγαγεῖν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.140; ἀγαγεῖν ἐπί τινας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.19}}; π. ποιεῖν make **war,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.7}}; π. ποιεῖσθαι carry **it** on, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.5.24}}; π. καταλύεσθαι put an end to **it,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.17, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.36; ὁ π. ἀναπέπαυται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.47: proverbial, οὐ πόλεμον ἐπαγγέλλεις}} i.e. that is good news, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 702d, “Phdr.” 242b: in _plural_, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 250, etc.; διὰ τὴν τῶν χρημάτων κτῆσιν πάντες οἱ π. ἡμῖν γίγνονται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 66c, compare “R.” 460a, +others}}
**II** personified, **War, Battle,** Ἀλαλὰ Πολέμου θύγατερ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 78, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 205}}; Π. πάντων μὲν πατήρ ἐστι, πάντων δὲ βασιλεύς {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 53; ὁ π. τῆς γενέσεως 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 423.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_ of womankind, πολυτελὴς π. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 8.}}

4172:
** πόλις**,
_Epic dialect_ also **πτόλις** (found sometimes in Trag. etc., see at {πτόλις}), ἡ: _Epic dialect_ forms, _accusative_ _singular_ πόληα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 105, Callias Epigrammaticus “Aet.Oxy.” 2080.62}}; _genitive_ πόλιος or πτόλιος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.811, 4.514, +others, πόληος 16.395, +others (also 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 757), πόλεος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.567; _dative_ πόλει 5.686, +others, πτόλεϊ 17.152, πόληϊ 3.50 (also 7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 12.15): _plural_, _nominative_ πόλιες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.412, πόληες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.45; _genitive_ πολίων 1.125, +others; _dative_ πολίεσσι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.252; _accusative_ πόλεις 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.648, +others, πόληας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.486, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 9.70}} P.(scanned ?~X ?~X ?~X ¯ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 12.826), πόλιας (_disyllable_) 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.560, 574, (_trisyllable_) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.308 (assuming variant, πόλεας 3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus)}}: _Ionic dialect_ forms, _genitive_ πόλεως {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 12(8).356 (from Thasos), “GDI” 5653a13 (from Chios), etc., also 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 2.9, 22, variant in 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1043; written πόλειως “GDI” 5532.19 (from Zeleia); πόλεος [prev. work]5339.41 (Orop.), “IG” 12(7).103 (from Amorgos), 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 776, etc., πόλιος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.26, +others, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.8, +others, πόληος 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus (see. above), conjecture in 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 47, compare “Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 1.361}}; _dative_ mostly πόλει, but πόλῑ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.60, +others, πόληϊ (or -ῃ) “SIG” 169.3 (lasos, 4th c.BC): _plural_, usually πόλεις, πόλεων, πόλεσι, but in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus πόλιες 1.142, +others, πολίων [prev. work] 6, +others, πόλισι [prev. work] 151, +others}}; _accusative_ πόλῑς {{Refs|2.177, +others, πόλιας 1.142, 2.102, +others}}: _Doric dialect_ _genitive_ _singular_ πόλιος {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 615.3 (Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}; _dative_ _singular_ πόλι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 4.839 (Calaurea, 4th c.BC)}}; _dative_ _plural_ πολίεσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 7.8}}; πολίεσσι Foed.Lac. cited in [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.77 (variant{πολίεσι}), 79, “IG” 42(1).74.4 (Epid., 3rd c.BC)}}]; Cretan dialect πόλιθι {{Refs|“GDI” 5019.3}}: _Aeolic dialect_ _genitive_ πόλιος {{Refs 7th c.BC+|“IG” 12(2).526a8 (πόληος is an Epicism in 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 17.6); _genitive_ _plural_ πολίων “IG” 11(4).1064b20; _dative_ _plural_ πολίεσσι [prev. work] 12(2).1.6: Trag., _genitive_ πόλεως}} _disyllable_ (as also in Comedy texts, except {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 763), thrice πόλεος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1167 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 162, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 897:—_Attic dialect_ Inscrr. earlier than 4th c.BC sometimes have _dative_ _sing._ πόλῃ, “IG” 12.108.35,22.17.10,42.5, 53.7}}; _Attic dialect_ dual πόλη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.116, πόλη or πόλει 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 5th-4th c.BC: Socratis et Socraticorum Epistulae 8 (where 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus cites both forms, “in Theod.” 1.314, 136 H.); _genitive_ τοῖν πολέοιν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.73: Elean _nominative_ _sing._ πόλερ “Schwyzer” 425.16; _genitive_ πόλιορ [prev. work]20 (3rd-2nd c.BC)}}:—**city,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus [same places], 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 270, etc.}}; π. ἄκρη, ἀκροτάτη, ={ἀκρόπολις}, **the citadel,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.88, 20.52}}; which at Athens also was in early times called simply π., while the rest of the city was called ἄστυ, καλεῖται.. ἡ ἀκρόπολις μέχρι τοῦδε ἔτι ὑπ᾽ Ἀθηναίων π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.15}}; ἐν πόλει in treaties, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.23, 47, compare “IG” 12.372.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 245, 758; ἐς πόλιν “IG” 12.91.4; πρὸς πόλιν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 288 (Lyric poetry); ἐκ πόλεως [prev. author] “Eq.” 1093; but ἐν τῇ πόλει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.1.27, uncertain in 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.39}}; so Ἰνάχου π. **the citadel** of Argos, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 228.6}}; of the Cadmea at Thebes, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pelopidas” 18, compare 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.6.8}}; of Alexandria, {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 239.13}}; π. ἡ ἁγία, of Jerusalem, {{LXX|LXX.Neh.11.1: with the name of the city added in _genitive_, Ἰλίου π., Ἄργους π.}} **the city of..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 29, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 813}}; also in _apposition_, ἡ Μένδη π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.130; ἡ π. οἱ Ταρσοί 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.26.}}
**2** **one's city** or **country,** πόθι τοι π. ἠδὲ τοκῆε; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.170, etc., compare πόλιν· τὴν χώραν, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**3** ὁ ἐπὶ τῆς πόλεως **city** governor, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 113.3,134.2, +others(Cyprus, 2nd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.39.3: without _Article_, ἐπὶ πόλιος “IG” 7.2809.2 (Hyettus, 3rd c.BC), etc.; ἐπὶ πόλεως [prev. work]299.1 (Oropus. 3rd c.BC)}}; στρατηγὸς πόλεως {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“OGI” 743 (1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus); στρατηγὸς τῆς π. “BGU” 729.1 (2nd c.AD)}}; στρατηγὸς κατὰ πόλιν, = Latin _praetor urbanus,_ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.951.2 (Rome, 1st c.BC).}}
**II** **country,** as dependent on and called after its city, ἀνθρώπων οἳ τήνδε π. καὶ γαῖαν ἔχουσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.177, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 380, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1533. etc.}}; especially of **islands** peopled by men, Αῆμνον π. Θόαντος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.230}}; π. Αἴαντος, ={Σαλαμίς}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).48, etc.; περιρρύτας π. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 77, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 294, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 251 (see. Scholia); also διώχληκε π. πολλὰς.., Σικελίαν, Ἰταλίαν, Πελοπόννησον, Θετταλίαν κτλ. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.6, compare 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.3.31.}}
**III** **community** or **body of citizens** (opposed to ἄστυ, their dwellings, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.144, but in δῆμός τε π. τε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.14, π.}} denotes the **town**), ὧν π. ἀνάριθμος ὄλλυται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 179 (Lyric poetry): hence}}
**III.2** **state** or **community,** ξύμπασα π. κακοῦ ἀνδρὸς ἀπηύρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 240, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.88, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 22, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 947, etc.; π. ἄνδρα διδάσκει 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 67}}; especially **free state, republic,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 738 (compare 734), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.28, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1276a23; τὰ τῆς π.}}**state affairs, government,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 319a; π. ἡ γενῶν καὶ κωμῶν κοινωνία ζωῆς τελείας καὶ αὐτάρκους 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1280b40}}; τὴν π. φεύγειν shun one's **public duties,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.66}}; **assembly of citizens,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Berl.Sitzb. 1927.8 (_Locrian dialect_, 5th c.BC).}}
**III.3** **rights of citizenship,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 718, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.106.}}
**IV** πόλιν παίζειν, a game resembling backgammon, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 56}}, perhaps alluded to in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 422e}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _pūr,_ Lithuanian _pilìs_ 'fortrees'.)

4174:
** πολῑτ-εία**,
_Ionic dialect_ **πολῑτ-ηΐη**, ἡ, **condition and rights of a citizen, citizenship,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.34, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.104, etc.; π. δοῦναί τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.2.10}}: _plural_, **grants of citizenship,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 54.3.}}
**2** **the daily life of a citizen,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.10, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.184; ἐν εἰρήνῃ καὶ π. [prev. author] 20.122}}; **life, living,** ἡ ἐν Βοιωτίᾳ π. {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.43.6; so perhaps NT.Eph.2.12.}}
**3** concrete, **body of citizens,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1292a34.}}
**4** = Latin _civitas_ in geographical sense, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“SIG” 888.118 (Scaptopara, 3rd c.AD), Mitteis “Chr.” 78.6 (4th c.AD), etc.}}
**II** **government, administration,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 219, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.9.15, etc.; ἄγειν τὴν π. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.127; πρασύτατα καὶ ἀσελγέστατα τῇ π. κεχρῆσθαι 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 29}}; **course of policy,** τῇ π. καὶ τοῖς ψηφίσμασι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.87, compare 9.3 (_plural_), 18.263; ἡ Κλεοφῶντος π. 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.150; ἡ πρὸς Ῥωμαίους ὁμιλία καὶ π. 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 16.2.46}}: _plural_, **acts of policy,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Vita” 65.}}
**II.2** **tenure of public office,** πᾶσαν π. ἐπιφανῶς ἐκτελέσαι {{Refs|“IG” 4.716.6 (from Hermione)}}; ἐν τοῖς τῆς π. χρόνοις {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IPE” 12.32 B 76 (Olbia, 3rd c.BC).}}
**III** **civil polity, constitution of a state,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.1, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.37, etc.; τὴν ἐλευθερίαν.., μᾶλλον δὲ καὶ τὰς π. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.65}}; **form of gouernment,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 562a, etc.; ὁμολογοῦνται τρεῖς εἶναι π., τυραννὶς καὶ ὀλιγαρχία καὶ δημοκρατία 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.4, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1293a37, etc.; αἱ τέτταρες π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 544b; ἥτις ἂν π. συμφέρῃ 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.8; π. ἐστὶ τάξις ταῖς πόλεσιν ἡ περὶ τὰς ἀρχάς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1289a15, compare 1274b26 (_plural_), 1289b27 (_plural_); ὅπου μὴ νόμοι ἄρχουσιν οὐκ ἔστι π. [prev. work] 1292a32}}; τὴν ἀρίστην πολιτεύεσθαι π.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1288b31, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 1.1, etc.}}
**III.2** especially **republican government, free common-wealth,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1160a34, “Pol.” 1293b22}}; ὅταν δὲ τὸ πλῆθος πρὸς τὸ κοινὸν πολιτεύηται συμφέρον, καλεῖται π.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1279a39; ἄπιστον ταῖς π. ἡ τυραννίς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.5; οὐ γὰρ ἀσφαλεῖς ταῖς π. αἱ πρὸς τοὺς τυράννους.. ὁμιλίαι [prev. author] 6.21; τοὺς τὰς π. μεθιστάντας εἰς ὀλιγαρχίαν [prev. author] 15.20; ταῖς μὲν π. πολεμοῦσι τὰς δὲ μοναρχίας συγκαθιστᾶσι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.125; ἔστι δήμου ἡ π. βίος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.826c.}}

4175:
** πολῑτ-ευμα**, ατος, τό,
**business of government, act of administration,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.108, 136}}: more frequently in _plural_, **measures of government** or **institutions,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 945d, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.78; τῶν τοιούτων π. οὐδὲν πολιτεύομαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.71}}; ἔν τε τοῖς κατὰ τὴν πόλιν π. καὶ ἐν τοῖς Ἑλληνικοῖς both in my home and foreign **policy,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] 18.109; κάλλιστον π. ἐποιήσατο “IG” 42(1).81.9 (Epid., 1st c.AD)}}; π. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Catonis” 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 6.1.13, compare 9.7.3.}}
**II** the concrete of πολιτεία {{Refs|111}}, **the government,** π. ἐστὶν ἡ πολιτεία {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1278b11, compare 1279a26, 1283b31, etc.}}; οἱ ἐν π. the **citizens,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1303b26, compare 1305b34; τὸ τῆς δημοκρατίας π. 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.172; τὸ πάτριον π. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.9.9, compare 4.25.7 (_plural_)}}; π. ἀκέραια, σωφρονικά, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.13.12, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.41; τὰ π.}}**free republics,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.69}}; **form of government,** πολίτεομα (sic) εἶναι ἐν Χίῳ δῆμον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 283.3 (Edict of Alexander, Chios, 4th c.BC)}}, cf. Decrees _Attic dialect_ cited in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.851f.}}
**III** **citizen rights, citizenship,** ἀξίους τοῦ παρ᾽ ὑμῖν π. {{NT|“IG” 9(2).517.6 (Larissa, Epist. Philipp. V), etc.: _metaphorically_, ἡμῶν τὸ π. ἐν οὐρανοῖς ὑπάρχει NT.Phil.3.20.}}
**IV** concrete, **body of citizens,** τὸ π. τὸ Μιλησίων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 633.59 (Milet., 2nd c.BC),compare “OGI” 229.60 (Smyrna, 3rd c.BC), etc.}}; **souereign body,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1302b16, 1332b31; π. ἔστω οἱ μύριοι “Abh.Berl.Akad.” 1925 (5).6 (from Cyrene); πᾶν τὸ π. [prev. work]7.}}
**IV.2** **corporate body of citizens** resident in a foreign city, Καυνίων τὸ π. (at Sidon) {{Refs|“OGI” 592; τὸ π. τῶν ἐν Βερενίκῃ Ἰουδαίων “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 5361.21}}; τὸ π. τῶν Κρητῶν (in Egypt) {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 32.17 (2nd c.BC).}}
**IV.2.b** generally, **corporate body, association,** τὸ π. τῶν γυναικῶν {{Refs|“BCH” 15.182,205 (from Panamara)}}; τὸ π. τινός **founded by** a person, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 5793 (1st c.AD).}}

4176:
** πολῑτεύω**,
_future_ **-σω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.19, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.2}}:—**to be a citizen** or **freeman, live in a free state,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.46, 3.34, 4.114, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.26; οἴκοι π. “SIG” 306.21 (Tegea, 4th c.BC)}}; π. παρά τισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.5.19}}; πεπολιτευκὼρ πὰρ ἁμέ, ={μετοικῶν}, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 425.5 (Elis, 3rd-2nd c.BC)}}; κατὰ νόμους π., opposed to monarchy, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.76.2: more frequently in}}_middle_, see below
**2** **have a certain form of government, administer the state,** κατ᾽ ὀλιγαρχίαν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.19, 3.62; π. ὥσπερ εἰώθεσαν [prev. author] 4.130; κατὰ τὰ ἴδια κέρδη π. [prev. author] 2.65; πρὸς τὸ ἴδιον κέρδος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.4.13; ἐλευθέρως τὰ πρὸς τὸ κοινὸν π. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.37}}:—_passive_, of the state, **to be governed,** τὰς εὖ -ευομένας πόλεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.35, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 427a, etc.; ἄνευ ὁμονοίας οὔτ᾽ ἂν πόλις εὖ -ευθείη 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.16}}; τὰ πεπολιτευμένα αὐτοῖς **the measures of** their **administration,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.28; τὰ κοινῇ πεπ. [prev. author] 18.8, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 16.45, etc.}}
**2.b** _passive_, in Law, **to be customary,** τὸ μέχρι νῦν -ευόμενον {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 73.8.2, compare 52 “Praef.”}}; ἡ -ευομένη τῆς ἀρτάβης (i.e. τιμή) **customary** price, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“PGiss.” 105.7 (5th c.AD).}}
**3** _passive_, **to be made a citizen,** τοὺς ἐπὶ Τέλωνος πολιτευθέντας {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.72.}}
**B** most frequently in _middle_, _future_ πολιτεύσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1365, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 3.9}}: _aorist_ ἐπολιτευσάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.10, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.207}}; also _passive_ ἐπολιτεύθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.92, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 26.5, (ἐν-) 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.5, etc.}}: _perfect_ πεπολίτευμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.10, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 13.35, etc.}}:—like the _active_, **live as a free citizen,** chiefly in Prose (once in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus (see. below), twice in 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus (see. below)); π. μεθ᾽ ὑμῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator [same place]; ἐν δημοκρατίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.1.1, etc.; ἐν ἐλευθερίᾳ καὶ νόμοις ἐξ ἴσου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.4}}; opposed to μετοικέω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.20; ἐν εἰρήνῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.22; ἀδίκως πρὸς τοὺς ἄλλους π. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.42; εἰ πένης.. λαὸς πολιτεύοιτο πλουσίων ἄτερ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 21.}}
**B.II** **take part in the government,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 45 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.15 (as variant), 4th-3rd c.BC: Nausiphanes Philosophus 2, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 27, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.18}}; **meddle with politics,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 561d}}; opposed to ἰδιωτεύειν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.195}}; **hold public office, show public spirit,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 4.858 (Methana, compare “Glotta” 14.78), “SIG” 850.14 (Epist. Antonini Pii), etc.}}
**B.II.2** with _accusative_, **administer, govern,** ἅπαντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 573; τὰ καθ᾽ αὑτοὺς πολιτεύεσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.74; ἃ καὶ πεποίηκα καὶ πεπολίτευμαι [prev. author] 18.4; οὐ τὰ βέλτιστα π. [prev. work]207; π. πόλεμον ἐκ πολέμου}} **make** perpetual war **the principle of government,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.177}}: _absolutely_, **conduct the government,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1365; κατὰ συμμορίας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.29; διὰ τοὺς ἀδίκως -ομένους ἐν τῇ ὀλιγαρχίᾳ δημοκρατία γίγνεται 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.27; τοῖς ὑπὲρ αὐτοῦ πεπολιτευμένοις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.4}}; οἱ πολιτευόμενοι the **ministers,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.30, 24.157.}}
**B.III** **have a certain form of government,** τοὺς ἄριστα τῶν ἄλλων πολιτευομένους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.24; ἡμῶν ἐγγὺς π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 568b}}; κατὰ τὰ πάτρια π. Decrees cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.83}}; οἱ τὴν ἄνισον πολιτείαν πολιτευόμενοι, i. e. those living in an oligarchy or a tyranny, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.5.}}
**B.IV** **serve as curialis,** Mitteis {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“Chr.” 97i18 (4th c.AD), “PLips.” 62i2 (4th c.AD), “POxy.” 2106.19 (4th c.AD), etc.}}
**B.V** in Law, **execute according to custom,** διαθήκας {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Nou.” 66.1 “Intr.”}}
**B.VI** **deal with,** in private affairs, ἀλλήλοις {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.63.11 (3rd c.BC)}}; πρὸς [τοὺς θεοὺς] ὁσίως καὶ δικαίως {{NT+2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 144.14, compare 110.78 (2nd c.BC), LXX.2Mac.11.25, Aristeas 31, NT.Act.23.1; π. πᾶσαν πολιτείαν κατὰ τὸν ἰουδαϊσμόν “BCH” 56.293 (Stobi, 1st-2nd c.AD)}}; **behave,** {{NT|NT.Phil.1.27.}}
**B.VI.b** _metaphorically_, **arrange, bring about,** συνοδίαν, γάμον, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 1.1, 2.2.}}

4177:
** πολῑτ-ης**, ου, ὁ,
_Ionic dialect_ **πολιήτης** (which see), **citizen, freeman,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.558, 22.429, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.131, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 5.16, etc.; π. ἀγαθός 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.42, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 517c; κακός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 271}}; πόλεως, πόλεων π., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.78, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.5; ὦ γᾶς πατρίας πολῖται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 806 (Lyric poetry); π. ὁρίζεται τῷ μετέχειν κρίσεως καὶ ἀρχῆς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1275a22.}}
**2** **fellow-citizen** (compare πολιήτης), {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 1.14, etc.; Κάδμου π. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1; Ἀθηναίων π. 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.139; ὑμῶν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.12; σός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 339e}}: and by a Comedy texts _metaphorically_, οἴνου π. ὢν κρατίστου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 36.}}
**II** _adjective_ **belonging to, connected with one's city** or **country,** θεοὶ πολῖται, ={πολιοῦχοι}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 253}}; π. δῆμος, ={ὁ τῆς πόλεως}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 574.}}

4178:
** πολλάκις** [ᾰ],
_Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry **πολλάκι**, sometimes in Trag. (only Lyric poetry) for the sake of meter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 227, “Supp.” 131, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1456 (_anapaest meter_)}}; never in Prose: (πολύς): _adverb_
**I** of Time, **many times, often,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.396, etc.; π. καὶ οὐκὶ ἅπαξ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.46; π. τοῦ μηνός 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.9; π. ἀγωνοθέτης “Ephes.” 3p.152No. 70.}}
**II** of Degree and Number, π. μυρίοι **many** tens of thousands, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 810d, “Tht.” 175a}}; of Quantity, [τὴν] οὐσίαν π. τοσαύτην ἐποίησε {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 330b}}; of Size, μεῖζον π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.944a.}}
**II.2** τὸ π. **mostly, for the most part,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.32}}; **very much, altogether,** χρὼς ὅμοιος ἐγίνετο πολλάκι θάψῳ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.88; χαίρετε π Μοῖσαι [prev. author] 1.144.}}
**III** in _Attic dialect_, after conditional particles, **perhaps,** σεισμὸς εἰ γένοιτο π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 791}}; ἐάν τι πολλὰ π. πάθω{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1105: with ἄρα, ἐὰν ἄρα π. νυμφόληπτος γένωμαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 238d, compare “Phd.” 60e, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 32.3}}; also μὴ π. lest **perchance,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐν κεφαλη—ͅ τρωμάτων” 14, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 361c, +others}}

4181:
Included with: ** πολυ-μερής**, ές,
(μέρος) **consisting of many parts, manifold,** opposed to εἷς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 98d (_superlative_),compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 411b11, “PA” 683b5 (_comparative_)}}; πρᾶξις {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “Po.” 1459b1; -έστατον τὸ δωδεκάεδρον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.427b}}. _adverb_ **-ρῶς**{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Sententiae ad intelligibilia ducentes” 34.}}
**2** **of divers kinds,** τῆς ὕβρεως οὔσης π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1311a33}}. _adverb_ **-ρῶς** **in many ways,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Heb.1.1, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.537d, 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 127.}}

4182:
** πολῠ-ποίκῐλος**, ον,
**much-variegated,** φάρεα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1149 (Lyric poetry); στέφανος 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 105 (_anapaest meter_); διαχωρήματα 7th c.AD: Stephanus Medicus “in Hp.” 1.157 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**2** **manifold,** σοφία τοῦ θεοῦ {{NT+5th c.AD+|NT.Eph.3.10; φαντασία τῶν οἰνωμένων Anonymus “Incred.” 17; τελετή 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Hymni” 6.11.}}

4183:
** πολύς**,
_Attic dialect_ πολλή, πολ; _genitive_ πολλοῦ, ῆς, ou=; _dative_ πολλῷ, ῇ, ; _accusative_ πολύν, πολλήν, πολύ:—_Ionic dialect_ **πολλός** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 43.3}}, πολλή, πολλόν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 9, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 219, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 1, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 3.19}}; also in Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 86, “Tr.” 1196}}; _accusative_ πολλόν, πολλήν, πολλόν: {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}} uses the _Ionic dialect_ forms, but codices have πολύν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2.121.δ, 3.57, variant in 6.125, πολύ 2.106, 3.38, 6.72, 7.46, 160 (πολύ also in 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 114, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 244)}}:—both sets of forms are found in _Epic dialect_, also _genitive_ _singular_ πολέος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.244, etc.: _nominative_ _plural_ πολέες 2.417, +others}}, once _contraction_ πολεῖς {{Refs|11.708; _genitive_ πολέων (_trisyllable_) 5.691, (_disyllable_) 16.655}}; _dative_ πολέσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|10.262, +others; πολέσσι 13.452, +others; πολέεσσι 9.73, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.54, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 119, etc.}}; _accusative_ πολέας (_trisyllable_) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.126, etc., (_disyllable_) 1.559, 2.4, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 580}} (frequently with variant{πολεῖς} {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.66, etc.)}}; in later _Epic dialect_ πολέες is used as _feminine_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 28, also πολέας [prev. author] “Dian.” 42, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.21; _neuter_ πολέα 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 1.74 (see. below)}}:—_Epic dialect_ also have **πουλύς** (once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus, “Th.” 190, also 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 509, sometimes _feminine_ in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, πουλὺν ἐφ᾽ ὑγρήν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.27, ἠέρα πουλύν 5.776), _neuter_ πουλύ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.387}}; these forms are found in codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus and 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus (who uses πολύ, πουλύ and πολλόν in _neuter_), but not in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus:}} — Lyric poetry and Trag. (Lyric poetry) sometimes use _Epic dialect_ forms, _dative_ _singular_ πολεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 745; _nominative_ _plural_ πολέες 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.17; _neuter_ πολέα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 723; πολέων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1332 (_feminine_, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.100); _dative_ _plural_ πολέσι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1263. [ῠalways.]}}
**I** of Number, **many,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.417, etc.}}; ἐκ πολλῶν, opposed to ἐξὀλίγων, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 447}}; τριηκόντων ἐτέων πόλλ᾽ ἀπολείπων wanting **many** of thirty years, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Op.” 696; παρῆσάν τινες, καὶ πολλοί γε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 58d; οὐ πολλοί τινες 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 510: with Nouns of multitude, πουλὺς ὅμιλος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.109; πλῆθος πολλόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.141; ἔθνος πολλόν [prev. author] 4.22}}; later πουλὺ.. ἐπ᾽ ἔτος **many** a year, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.235 (Thall.); π. ἦν ὁ καταπλέων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.26.10}}; of anything often repeated, περὶ σέο λόγος ἀπῖκται π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30; πολλὸν ἦν τοῦτο τὸ ἔπος [prev. author] 2.2, compare 3.137, etc.; πολὺ.. τὸ σὸν ὄνομα διήκει πάντας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 305; τούτῳ πολλῷ χρήσεται τῷ λόγῳ}}**often,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.29}}; τοῦτο ἐπιεικῶς πολὺ νῦν ἐστι is fairly **frequent,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 15.}}
**I.2** of Size, Degree, Intensity, **much, mighty,** ὄμβρος, νιφετός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.6; π. ὕπνος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.394; πῦρ.. π. 10.359}}; π. ὑμέναιος a **loud** song, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.493}}; π. ὀρυμαγδός, ῥοῖζος, etc.,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.810, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.315, etc.}}; π. ἀνάγκη **strong** necessity, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1674}}; π. γέλως, βοή, **much** or **great,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 303, 1149; μωρία [prev. work] 745}}; ὄλβος, αἰδώς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 251, “Ag.” 948; ἀσφάλεια 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.11}}; ἀλογία, εὐήθεια, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 67e, “Phdr.” 275c, etc.}}
**I.2.b** rarely of a single person, **great, mighty,** μέγας καὶ πολλὸς ἐγένεο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.14, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1}}; ὁ π. σοφιστής, στρατηγός,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Chor. p.23 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus, [prev. author]in “Rev.Phil.” 1.68; ὁ πάντα π. [prev. author] p.27 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}; ὁ πολύς alone, of Hippocrates, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.530}}; of Trajan, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “Mag.” 2.28; ῥώμην σώματος πολύς 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.42.}}
**I.2.c** joined with a Verb, Κύπρις γὰρ οὐ φορητός, ἢν πολλὴ ῥυῇ if she flow **with full stream,** _metaphorically_ from a river, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 443; θρασυνομένῳ καὶ πολλῷ ῥέοντι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.136}}; from the wind, ὡς π. ἔπνει καὶ λαμπρός was blowing **strong** and fresh, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 25.57, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 760, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.49 (5th c.BC: Evenus Elegiacus)}}: generally, **with might** or **force,** ὅταν ὁ θεὸς.. ἔλθῃ πολύς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 300; ἢν π. παρῇ [prev. author] “Or.” 1200; π. καὶ τολμηρὸς ἅνθρωπος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 40.53: with}}_participle_ and εἰμί, πολλὸς ἦν λισσόμενος was **all** entreaties, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.91; ἦν πολλὸς ὑπὸ παντὸς ἀνδρὸς αἰνεόμενος [prev. author] 1.98; Ἐτεοκλέης ἂν εἷς π... ὑμνοῖθ᾽ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 6; π. ἐνέκειτο λέγων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.158; π. τοῖς συμβεβηκόσιν ἔγκειται 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.199; also π. ἦν ἐν τοῖσι λόγοισι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.59; πρὸς ταῖς παρασκευαῖς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.49.7; ἐπὶ τῇ τιμωρίᾳ 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.107}}: without a _preposition_, π. ἦν τοῖς ἐπαίνοις καὶ ἐπαχθής {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.41}}; π. μὲν γὰρ ὁ Φίλιππος ἔσται will be **often mentioned,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.166.}}
**I.3** of Value or Worth, πολέος δέ οἱ ἄξιος ἔσται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.562, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.405; πολλοῦ ἄξιος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.1.28, etc.; πολλῶν ἄξιος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 918}}; περὶ πολλοῦ ποιεῖσθαί τι, Latin _magni facere,_{{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare περί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus IV}}; ἐπὶ πολλῷ at a **high price,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.53; ἐπὶ π. ἐρραθυμηκότες [prev. author] 1.15}}; πολύ ἐστί τι it is **worth much, of great conscquence,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 18.7.}}
**I.4** of Space, **large, wide,** π. χώρη, πεδίον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.520, 4.244, etc.}}; πόντος, πέλαγος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 635, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 635; χῶρος πλατὺς καὶ π. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.39}}; λίμνη μεγάλη τε καὶ π.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 109; π. ἡ Σικελία 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.13; π. ἡ Ἑλλάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 78a, etc.}}; πολλὸς ἔκειτο he lay **outstretched wide,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.156, compare 11.307}}; π. κέλευθος a **far** way, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 748 (troch.): without ὁδός, πολλὴ μὲν εἰς Ἡράκλειαν.., πολλὴ δὲ εἰς Χρυσόπολιν.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.3.16: διὰ πολλοῦ, ἐκ πολλοῦ, see infr. IV.}}
**I.5** of Time, **long,** χρόνος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1402 (_anapaest meter_), etc.; πολὺν χρόνον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.343, etc.; οὐ π. χρ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 348, etc.; so πολλοῦ χρόνου 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 98; χρόνῳ πολλῷ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 228}}; διὰ πολλοῦ (i.e. χρόνου) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nec.” 15; ἐκ πολλοῦ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.58, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.41}}; πρὸ πολλοῦ **long** before, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.43; οὐ μετὰ πολύ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 54}}; ἔτι πολλῆς νυκτός while still **quite** night, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.101}}; πολλῆς ὥρας **late** in the day, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.8.3; ἤδη ὥρα πολλή NT.Mark.6.35; ἔτι ἔστιν ἡμέρα πολλή LXX.Gen.29.7.}}
**II** Special usages:
**II.1** with _partitive_ _genitive_, e.g. πολλοὶ Τρώων, for πολλοὶ Τρῶες, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.271, etc.}}; _neuter_, πολλὸν σαρκός, for πολλὴ σάρξ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.450}}: in Prose, the _adjective_ generally takes the gender of the _genitive_, τὸν πολλὸν τοῦ χρόνου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.24}}; τῆς γῆς οὐ πολλήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.7; τῆς ἀθάρης πολλήν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 694; πολλὴν τῆς χώρας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.2; ὁ π. τοῦ λόγου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 44.6}}; see below 3.
**II.2** joined with another _adjective_, πολλὰ δυστερπῆ κακά {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 277, compare 585 (Lyric poetry), etc.: more frequently joined to another}} _adjective_ by καί, πολέες τε καὶ ἐσθλοί **many** men and good, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.452, etc.; πολέες τε καὶ ἄλκιμοι 21.586; πολλὰ καὶ ἐσθλά 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.312}}; παλαιά τε πολλά τε{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 188; ἄκοσμά τε π. τε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.213; πολλαί γε.. καὶ ἄλλαι 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 363; π. τε καὶ κακά 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.167, etc.; π. κἀγαθά 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 351 (but π. ἀγαθά “IG” 12.76.45); π. καὶ ἀνόσια 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 416e; π. καὶ μακάρια [prev. author] “Plt.” 269d; π. καὶ πονηρά 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.9.6; πολλά τε καὶ δεινά [prev. author] “An.” 5.5.8; μεγάλα καὶ π. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.22}}; π. καὶ καλοὺς (assuming variant) κινδύνους, π. καὶ καλὰ παραδείγματα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.109.}}
**II.3** with the _Article_ (in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} without the _Article_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.483, 5.334, 22.28), of persons or things well known, Ἑλένα μία τὰς πολλάς, τὰς πάνυ π. ψυχὰς ὀλέσασ᾽}} those **many** lives, {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1456 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 845, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.87, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 88a, “Ti.” 54a, NT.Act.26.24}}: with abstract Nouns, τᾶς πολλᾶς ὑγιείας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1001 (Lyric poetry, uncertain); τὸ πολλόν}}**numbers,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.136.}}
**II.3.b** οἱ π. the **many,** i.e. the **greater number,** Ἀθηναῖοι.. ἀπῆλθον οἱ πολλοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.126, compare 3.32, etc. (so in _sing._, ὁ πολλὸς λόγος}} the **prevailing** report, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.75); τοῖς π. κριταῖς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1243: with _genitive_, τοῖς π. βροτῶν [prev. work] 682; οἱ π. τῶν ἀνθρώπων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.24; οἱ πολλοὶ ἅπαντες}}**far the most,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 20 (variant{μάλιστα} for ἅπαντες); for τὰ πολλὰ πάντα, see below 111.1a: hence οἱ πολλοί}}] the **people,** the **commonalty,** opposed to οἱ μείζω κεκτημένοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.6}}; opposed to οἱ κομψότεροι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 505b}}; οἱ π., = Latin _plebs,_ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.36}}; τῶν πολλῶν εἷς one **of** the **multitude,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.96; also ὁ π. λεώς 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 53, compare “Rh.Pr.” 17; ὁ π. ὅμιλος [prev. author] “Luct.” 2. 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.1.1, etc.; ὁ π. δῆμος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Apol.” 15; ὁ π. ὄχλος 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.4}}; ὁ π. alone, = **vulgus,** variant in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.29}}; the **ordinary man,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 478, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.154S.; νίμμα ὁ π. λέγει, ἡμεῖς ἀπόνιπτρον λέγομεν 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 170, compare369}}; ὁ ἐμπαθὴς καὶ π. ἄνθρωπος 'l'homme **moyen** sensuel', {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Hermias Alexandrinus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedrum scholia” p.146A.}}; ὁ π. ἄνθρωπος (with _plural_ Verb) the **average** man, opposed to τὸ ἐξαίρετον, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Fragmenta Historica - HGM” p.216 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**II.3.c** τὸ πολύ, with _genitive_, τῆς στρατιῆς τὸ πολλόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.100; τὸ π. τοῦ χρόνου 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 20; τῶν λογάδων τὸ π. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.73; τῶν ὅπλων τὸ π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 288b; also ὁ στρατὸς ὁ πολλός 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.102; ἡ δύναμις ἡ π. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.24}}; ὁ π. βίοτος **the best part** of life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 185 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.3.d** τὰ πολλά **the most,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.273, and perhaps 2.58, 17.537 (elsewhere in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus πολλά}} as _substantive_, means **much riches, great possessions,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.684, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.195); τὰ π. τοῦ πολέμου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13}}; πρὸς τὸ τῶν π. μέγεθος in regard to the size of **the average,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1363b11.}}
**II.4** _plural_ πολλά **very much, too much,** πολλὰ πράσσειν, ={πολυπραγμονεῖν}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 576, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 228; π. ἔπαθεν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.63, etc.}}; π. ἔρξαι τινά to do one **much harm,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 923 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.5** πολλάς with Verbs of beating (πληγάς being omitted), see at {πληγή} {{Refs|1.}}
**II.6** πολύς repeated, ἦ πολλὰ πολλοῖς εἰμι διάφορος βροτῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 579, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 451; τὰ μὲν οὖν πολλὰ πολλοῦ χρόνου διηγήσασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 615a, etc.; πολλοῦ πολύς, see below 111.1b: with _adverbs_ πολλάκις, πολλαχῇ, etc. (which see, compare 111.1 e).}}
**III** Adverbial usages:
**III.a** _neuter_ πολύ (_Ionic dialect_ πολλόν), πολλά, **much,** πόλλ᾽ ἀεκαζομένη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.458, etc.; strengthened, μάλα πολλά 8.22, +others; πάνυ πολύ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.119c; πολύ τι [prev. author] “R.” 484d}}; especially of repetition, **often,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.798, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.29, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 322}}; so of **earnest** commands and entreaties, πολλὰ κελεύων, πόλλ᾽ ἐπέτελλον, πολλὰ λισσομένη, πολλὰ μάλ᾽ εὐχομένω, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.528, 11.782, 5.358, 9.183}}: with the _Article_, τὸ πολύ **for the most part,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 315a, etc.}} (but with numerals, **at most,** Vett.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Val. 9.5); ὡς τὸ π. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.10, etc.; τὰ πολλά 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.13, 2.11, 87, etc.; ὡς τὰ π. [prev. author] 5.65, etc.; τὰ π. πάντα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.203, 2.35, 5.67.}}
**III.b** of Degree, **far, very much,** ἀπέφυγε πολλὸν τοὺς διώκοντας {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.82: also _absolutely_ _genitive_ πολλοῦ}} **very,** θρασὺς εἶ πολλοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 915, compare 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 74; πολλοῦ δύνασθαι 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.9 (assuming variant)}}; πολλοῦ πολύς, πολλὴ πολλοῦ, **much too much,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 822, “Ra.” 1046.}}
**III.c** of Space, **a great way, far,** οὐ πολλόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.104; πολὺ οὐκ ἐξῄεσαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.15, etc.}}
**III.d** of Time, **long,** ὡς πολλὸν τοῦτο ἐγίνετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.126, compare 6.129.}}
**III.e** of Probability, ἐὰν πολλὰ πολλῶν τέκῃς, perhaps ={ἐὰν πολλάκις τέκῃς},{{Refs 1st c.BC+|“POxy.” 744.9 1st c.BC-1st c.AD}}; ἐάν τι πολλὰ πολλάκις πάθω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1105.}}
**III.2** πολύ is frequently joined with _adjectives_ and _adverbs_,
**III.2.a** with a _comparative_ to increase its _comparative_ force, πολὺ μεῖζον, πολλὸν παυρότεροι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.167, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.17}}; πολὺ μᾶλλον **much** more, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.700}}; πολύ τι μᾶλλον _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 4 (p.22 U.-R.): with words, esp. Preps., between πολύ and its}} _adjective_, π. ἐν πλέονι, π. ἐπὶ δεινοτέρῳ,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.35, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 589e; πολὺ ἔτι ἐκ λαμπροτέρων [prev. author] “Phd.” 110c; π. σὺν φρονήματι μείζονι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.22, compare 3.2.30, “Smp.” 1.4 (but the _preposition_ frequently comes first, ἐκ π. ἐλάττονος 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.109, etc.)}}; so πολλῷ is frequently used with the _comparative_, **by far,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 337, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.134, etc.; π. μᾶλλον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1159, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 80e}}; οὐ πολλῷ τεῳ ἀσθενέστερον not **a great deal** weaker, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.181, compare 2.48, 67, etc.: πολύ with all words implying comparison, πολὺ πρίν}} **much** sooner, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.250; π. πρό 4.373}}: with the comp. Verb φθάνω, ἦ κε πολὺ φθαίη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|13.815; so πολὺ προβέβηκας ἁπάντων, πολὺ προμάχεσθαι ἁπάντων, 6.125, 11.217; προὔλαβε πολλῷ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.80: with βούλομαι}} = **prefer,** ἡμῖν πολὺ βούλεται ἢ Δαναοῖσι νίκην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.331, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.404}}; πολύ γε in answers, after a _comparative_ or _superlative_, ἀργὸς.. γενήσεται μᾶλλο; Answ. πολύ γε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 421d, compare 387e, etc.}}
**III.2.b** with a _superlative_, πολὺ πρώτιστος, πολλὸν ἄριστος, **far** the first, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.702, 1.91, etc.; προθυμία π. τολμηροτάτη 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.74, etc.; πολλόν τι μάλιστα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.56; π. δή, π. δὴ γυναῖκ᾽ ἀρίσταν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 442 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 539, 4th c.BC: Archestratus Epicus “fragment” 34.9; also πολλῷ πλεῖστοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92.έ, 8.42; π. μεγίστους [prev. author] 4.82.}}
**III.2.c** with a Positive, to add force to the _adjective_, ὦ πολλὰ μὲν τάλαινα, πολλὰ δ᾽ αὖ σοφή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1295; also ἐς πόλλ᾽ ἀθλία πέφυκ᾽ ἐγώ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 619 (troch.); πολὺ ἀφόρητος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMeretr.” 9.3}}; compare πλεῖστος.
**IV** with Preps.,
**IV.1** διὰ πολλοῦ at **a great interval** of Space or Time, see at {διά} {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.5, 11.2.}}
**IV.2** εἰς πολύ for **a long time,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.1.3.}}
**IV.3** ἐκ πολλοῦ from **a great distance,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.32, etc.}}; for **a long time,** see at {ἐκ} {{Refs|II.1.}}
**IV.4** ἐπὶ πολύ,
**IV.4.a** over **a great space, far,** οὐκ ἐπὶ πολλόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.32}}; ἐπὶ π. τῆς θαλάσσης, τῆς χώρας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.50, 4.3, etc.}}; to **a great extent,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.6, 18, 3.83; compare ποιέω 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11.2.}}
**IV.4.b** for **a long time, long,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.16; τῆς ἡμέρας ἐπὶ π. [prev. author] 7.38, compare 39.}}
**IV.4.c** ὡς ἐπὶ π. **very generally,**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.12 (variant), 4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.1.195}} ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ π. **for the most part,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 294e, etc.; μὴ καθ᾽ ἓν ἕκαστον, ἀλλ᾽ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ π. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.154; τό γ᾽ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ π. [prev. author] 8.35.}}
**IV.5** παρὰ πολύ by **far,** see at {παρά} C.111.5.
**IV.6** περὶ πολλοῦ, see above {{Refs|1.3.}}
**IV.7** πρὸ πολλοῦ **far** before, τῆς πόλεως {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 9.35}}; also of Time, οὐ πρὸ π. not **long** before, {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.62.}}
**IV.8** σὺν πολλῷ **in no small degree, only too much** or **too well,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 2.8, 9.20, 10.9 (compare “CR” 41.53).}}
**V** for _comparative_ πλείων, πλέων, _superlative_ πλεῖστος, (see entry). (Cf. Sanskrit _purú-,_ Gothic _filu_ 'much'.)

4185:
** πολυ-τελής**, ές,
(τέλος) **very expensive, costly,** opposed to εὐτελής, οἰκίη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.79; τράπεζα 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 210; παρακομιδή 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.28; ζῶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Mi.” 368c; παρασκευαί 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 1.20 (_comparative_)}}; π. νεκρός **honoured with a costly funeral,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Per.fragment” 2}}; λίθοι, λιθεία, **precious** stones, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 90.34 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC), 132 (Egypt, 2nd c.BC)}}: generally, **valuable,** -εστάτην τὴν τοῦ ὁρᾶν.. δύναμιν ἐδημιούργησεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 507c}}; -έστατον ζῷον, variant for{πολυφρονέστατον}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|Euryphamus Pythagoreus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.39.27.}}
**II** of persons, **lavish, extravagant,** coupled with ἄσωτος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 615; γυνὴ π. ἐστ᾽ ὀχληρόν [prev. author] 325.7; ἑταίρα π. [prev. author] 824; π. τῷ βίῳ 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 80.5}}. _adverb_ **-λῶς**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 335, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.31, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.11.4}}: _superlative_, τὰ -λέστατα **in the costliest manner,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.87.}}

4186:
** πολῠτῑμ-ος**, ον,
(τιμή) **much-revered,** θεοί {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 109.1.}}
**II** **highly priced,** μουσοεργός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος παιδίου” 13}}; **very costly,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 5.35.5 (2nd-3rd c.AD: Rufinus Epigrammaticus), 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 57.9}}. _adverb_ **-μως**{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 14.2.3 (nisi to be read -τελῶς).}}

4187:
Included with: ** πολύ-τροπος**, ον,
(τρέπω) **much-turned,** i.e. **much-travelled, much-wandering,** epithet of Odysseus, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.1, 10.330.}}
**II** **turning many ways**: _metaphorically_, **shifty, versatile, wily,** of Hermes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 13, 439; τοῖς ἀσθενέσι καὶ π. θηρίοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 291b}}; and in this sense Plato took the word as applied to Odysseus, {{Refs|“Hp.Mi.” 364e (_superlative_), +others}}; τὸ π. τῆς γνώμης their **versatility** of mind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.83}}; τὸ π., of Alcibiades, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 24.}}
**II.2** **fickle,** ὅμιλος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 95.}}
**II.3** of diseases, **changeful, complicated,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Numa” 22; also πόλεμος τοῖς πάθεσι ποικίλος καὶ ταῖς τύχαις πολυτροπώτατος [prev. author] “Mar.” 33; στρατεία 4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Fragmenta Historica - HGM” p.223D.}}
**III** **various, manifold,** ξυμφοραί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.44}}; ἐπιθυμίαι, ἐθισμοὶ τῶν λέξεων, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 471, “Nat.” 28.1 (p.7V.); κακά 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.567; ἔθνη 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marcellus” 12; τύχαι [prev. author] Alcaeus2; ὄργια “Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 36.3; τὸ π. 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 26}}. _adverb_ **-πως** **in many manners,** {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Meno Medicus “Iatrika” 20.31, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.512, NT.Heb.1.1, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de communi mathematica scientia” 12}}: _comparative_, -ωτέρως καὶ ποικιλωτέρως {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 5 G.}}

4188:
** πόμα**, τό,
see at {πῶμα}.
**II** ={φοῖνιξ}, conjecture in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.109.}}

4189:
** πονηρ-ία**, ἡ,
**bad state** or **condition,** ὀφθαλμῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Mi.” 374d; ἡ τοῦ σώματος π. [prev. author] “R.” 609c; π. ψυχῆς [prev. passage]}}
**II** in moral sense, **wickedness, vice, knavery,** ἡ μωρία.. ἀδελφὴ τῆς π. ἔφυ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 925, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 868, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.16}}; εἰς τὴν π. πάλιν τρέπεται turns again to **vice,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.75}}: in _plural_, **knavish tricks, rogueries,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.19, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1389a18.}}
**II.2** **baseness, cowardice,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 645, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.9.}}
**II.3** with a political connotation, **mob-rule,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.47.}}

4190:
** πονηρός**, ά, όν,
in physical sense, **oppressed by toils,** πονηρότατος καὶ ἄριστος, of Heracles, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Frr.” 138,139.}}
**2** of things, **toilsome, painful, grievous,** ἔργα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus “Epigrammata” 14.20; νούσων πονηρότερον 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 274; φορτίον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 352.}}
**II** **in bad case, in sorry plight, useless, good-for-nothing,** σύμμαχοι[{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 220, compare “Nu.” 102; στράτευμα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.34; ἰατρός 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.2.4 (variant for{μοχθηρός})}}]; κύων, ἱππάριον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 298d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.19}}; δίαιτα, τροφή, σιτία, **injurious,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 425e, “Lg.” 735b, “Grg.” 464d, etc.; π. ἕξις σώματος [prev. author] “Ti.” 86e}}; π. σῶμα, opposed to χρηστόν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prt.” 313a, compare “R.” 341e}}; π. σκώμματα **sorry** jests, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 542; π. βούλευμα [prev. author] “Lys.” 517 (_comparative_)}}; π. πράγματα a **bad** state of things, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.97, compare 24}}; π. ἀρχὴ τῆς παιδείας a **bad** beginning, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.11; π. τὴν ναυτιλίαν ναυτίλλεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 551c; π. πολιτεία 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1294b38.}} _adverb_, -ρῶς ἔχειν to be **in bad case,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.83, etc.; ἂ πονηρῶς ἔχει τῶν πραγμάτων 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.35}}; π. διακεῖσθαι, διατεθῆναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.12, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.55.}}
**III** in moral sense, **worthless, knavish,** φήμη, βίος, ζόη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 1045, “Frr.” 90,401, etc.; οὐδεὶς ἑκὼν π. 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 78; π. ἦθος 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 192}}; πονηρὸς.. κἀκ πονηρῶν **rogue** and son **of rogues,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 336 - 7}}; ὦ πόνῳ πονηρέ in a comic jingle, {{Refs|[prev. author] “V.” 466, compare “Lys.” 350}}; π. πόρρω τέχνης past master in **knavery,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “V.” 192; π. τοῖς φίλοις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.4.33; πρὸς ἀλλήλους [prev. author] “An.” 7.1.39; π. λόγων ἀκρίβεια 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.3; πονηρότεροι σύμβουλοι [prev. author] 5.71}}; π. [ῥῆμα] **malicious,** {{NT|NT.Matt.5.11; τὰ π.}}**wickednesses,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.25; πονηρὰ δρᾶσαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1190; τὸ π. LXX.Deu.17.2}}; δόλῳ πονηρῷ, Latin _dolo malo_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 693.6 (Methymna, 2nd c.BC)}}; ὁ π. the **evil one,** {{NT+4th c.AD+|NT.Matt.13.19; π. δαίμων “PLips.” 34.8 (4th c.AD), etc.}}
**III.2** **base, cowardly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 437, etc.}}; π. χρώματα, i.e. the coward's hue, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.34 (interpolated).}}
**III.3** with a political connotation, **of the baser sort,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 424; οἱ λεγόμενοι π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 519a}}; opposed to καλοὶ κἀγαθοί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.100,316, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 186.}} —On the variation of accent, πονηρός and πόνηρος, see at {μοχθηρός} near the end

