**III** in moral sense, **worthless, knavish,** φήμη, βίος, ζόη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 1045, “Frr.” 90,401, etc.; οὐδεὶς ἑκὼν π. 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 78; π. ἦθος 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 192}}; πονηρὸς.. κἀκ πονηρῶν **rogue** and son **of rogues,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 336 - 7}}; ὦ πόνῳ πονηρέ in a comic jingle, {{Refs|[prev. author] “V.” 466, compare “Lys.” 350}}; π. πόρρω τέχνης past master in **knavery,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “V.” 192; π. τοῖς φίλοις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.4.33; πρὸς ἀλλήλους [prev. author] “An.” 7.1.39; π. λόγων ἀκρίβεια 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.3; πονηρότεροι σύμβουλοι [prev. author] 5.71}}; π. [ῥῆμα] **malicious,** {{NT|NT.Matt.5.11; τὰ π.}}**wickednesses,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.25; πονηρὰ δρᾶσαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1190; τὸ π. LXX.Deu.17.2}}; δόλῳ πονηρῷ, Latin _dolo malo_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 693.6 (Methymna, 2nd c.BC)}}; ὁ π. the **evil one,** {{NT+4th c.AD+|NT.Matt.13.19; π. δαίμων “PLips.” 34.8 (4th c.AD), etc.}}
**III.2** **base, cowardly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 437, etc.}}; π. χρώματα, i.e. the coward's hue, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.34 (interpolated).}}
**III.3** with a political connotation, **of the baser sort,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 424; οἱ λεγόμενοι π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 519a}}; opposed to καλοὶ κἀγαθοί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.100,316, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 186.}} —On the variation of accent, πονηρός and πόνηρος, see at {μοχθηρός} near the end

4192:
** πόνος**, ὁ,
(πένομαι) **work,** especially **hard work, toil,** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} mostly of **the toil of war,** μάχης π. the **toil** of battle, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.568}}; πόνος alone, ={μάχη},{{Refs 8th c.BC+|6.77, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.117, +others}}; πόνον ἔχειν, ={μάχεσθαι}, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.525, compare 13.2, +others; ἀνδράσι δυσμενέεσσι π. καὶ δῆριν ἔθεντο 17.158; π. ἀνδρῶν 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 987; πόνοι Ἐνυαλίου 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).54}}; ἐν τούτῳ τῷ π. ὁ πολέμαρχος διαφθείρεται in this **struggle** (at Marathon), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.114; ἐν τοῖσι Τρωϊκοῖσι π. [prev. author] 9.27.}}
**2** generally, **toil, labour,** ἐπεὶ παύσαντο πόνου {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.467, +others}}; π. ὀρνίθεσσι τιθείη cause **toil** to them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 470}}; π. λαβόντας incurring **toil,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.24; π. παρέχειν μανθάνοντι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 526c}}; μάταιος π. **labour** in vain, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ti.” 40d; οἱ κατὰ τὰ σώματα π. [prev. author] “Plt.” 294e; π. συνεχής 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 241; πολλῷ π. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 509; μετὰ πολλοῦ π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 230a; σὺν π. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 9.6; οὐ μακρῷ π. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 75; ἄνευ π. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.6.22}}; ἔχει πόνον πολύν involves much **trouble,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1216 (also εἰνάλιον π. ἐχοίσας σκευᾶς}} when the tackle **labours** in the sea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.79): _plural_, π. ἑκούσιοι 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 240.}}
**3** of special kinds of labour, **bodily exertion, exercise,** στρατιωτικοὶ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.9}}; of **exertions** in the games, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 305, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.1, l. 4(3).47}}, etc.; γυμνάσια.., νεανιᾶν (probably) πόνον **the scene of** youthful **labours,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 211 (Lyric poetry); εἰναλίοισι πόνοισι 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.39.}}
**4** **work, task, business,** ἐπεὶ π. ἄλλος ἔπειγεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.54}}; **enterprise, undertaking,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 864 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}
**5** **implements for labour, stock-in-trade,** οὗτος τοῖς ἁλιεῦσιν ὁ πᾶς πόνος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.14}}; καὶ πόνος ἐντὶ θάλασσα the sea is their **workshop,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus “fragment” 1.10.}}
**II** **stress, trouble, distress, suffering,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.227; Τρώεσσι πόνον καὶ κήδε᾽ ἔθηκεν 21.525; ἦ μὴν καὶ π. ἐστὶν.. 2.291}}; ἐν τούτῳ τῷ π., of a storm, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.190}}; ὁ Μηδικὸς [π.] the **trouble** from the Medes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.1; παῦροι ἐν πόνῳ πιστοί 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.78: frequently in Trag., πόνος πόνῳ πόνον φέρει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 866 (Lyric poetry); πόνον ἔχειν [prev. author] “OC” 232 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.: in _plural_, **sufferings,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 66, 328, etc.}}; πόνους πονεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (πονέω 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.2); διά τινα πόνους ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 975 (Lyric poetry)}}; also of **disease,** κατέβαινεν ἐς τὰ στήθη ὁ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49; πλευρᾶς πόνοι καὶ θώρακος καὶ ἥπατος 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.2; ἰσχίων π. καὶ πλευρᾶς [prev. work] 73.}}
**II.2** **pain,** especially physical, δύο π. ἅμα γενομένων, μὴ κατὰ τὸν αὐτὸν τόπον, ὁ σφοδρότερος ἀμαυροῖ τὸν ἕτερον [{{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 2.46, compare 1st c.AD: Erotianus Grammaticus see entry {πόνοι}, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(2).699; π. ἐν κεφαλῇ 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Acut.(Sp.”) 40; ἐς τὰ ἄρθρα πόνοι [prev. author] “Aph.” 4.44, 45, compare 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.27, +others; π. ἐς ἀμφοτέρας κνήμας 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.26.γ, compare δ, +others, LXX.Gen.34.25}}]; distinguished from λύπη (pain in general), {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “Quaestiones - pars” 125.33}}; but sometimes ={λύπη}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.65U., “Sent.Vat.” 4, “fragment” 442, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.72K.}}
**III** **anything produced by work, a work,** μελισσᾶν τρητὸς π., of honey, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.54; ὑψηλὸς τεκτόνων π. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 357, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1570}}; ὁ ἐμὸς ὠδίνων π., of a child, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 30}}; so, πόνον ὀρταλίχων ὀλέσαντες, i.e. the nestlings, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 54 (_anapaest meter_)}}; τοὺς ἡμετέρους π. the **fruits of** our **labour,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.9.}}
**IV** Πόνος personified as son of Eris, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 226.}}

4194:
** πόντ-ιος**, α, ον,
also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 595 (Lyric poetry)}}: (πόντος):— **of the sea,** epithet of Poseidon, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Hymni Homerici” 22.3, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1072 (Lyric poetry), etc.; πόντιε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1010 (Lyric poetry)}}; π. Θέτις, Νηρηΐδες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.35, p. 11.2}}; π. δάκη **sea** monsters, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 582 (Lyric poetry); π. βόσκημα [prev. author] “fragment” 275}}; π. ὕδωρ, πέλαγος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.64, 7.56}}; κύματα, θύελλα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 89, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1659, etc.}}; ᾅδην π. πεφευγότες, i.e. death by drowning, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 667.}}
**b** π. ῥάκη, of sponges or fragments of such, {{Refs|“Tragica Adespota” 258.}}
**2** **by the sea,** of places, Ἰσθμός, ἀκτή, Χρύση, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.48, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 449, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 269.}}
**3** **in the sea,** of islands, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.18}}; especially of those **far out to sea,** opposed to πρόσγειοι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 368b33, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.6.2, 8.6.6}}; of ships, βάριδες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 553 (Lyric poetry); ὅπλισμα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 253 (Lyric poetry).}}
**4** of persons, ἱκέτας δέχεσθαι ποντίους **from the sea,**{{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyc.” 300}}; ἀφιέναι πόντιον **into the sea,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hec.” 797.}}
**5** **brought by sea** or **from beyond sea,** of iron, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 940 (Lyric poetry).}}

4195:
** πόντος**, ὁ:
_Epic dialect_ _genitive_ ἐκ ποντόφιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.83}}:—**sea,** especially **open sea,** common from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards, except in Prose, where it is chiefly used of special seas (see. below 11)}}; in the general sense, ὁπότε πνεῦμα ἐκ πόντου εἴη {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.26, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 611e, “Ti.” 25a, LXX.Exo.15.5}}; π. ἀπείριτος, ἀπείρων, εὐρύς, μεγακήτης, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.195, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.350, 6.291, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.158; π. ἠεροειδής, ἰοειδής, μέλας, οἶνοψ, 2.263, 11.107, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.79, 23.316; π. ἀτρύγετος, ἰχθυόεις, 15.27, 19.378; opposed to γαῖα, 8.479, etc.}}; κέλευθοι, πλάξ, πεδίον πόντου, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.195, 1.24, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 150 (_anapaest meter_)}}; π. ἁλὸς πολιῆς the **wide waters** of the grey brine, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.59, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 10, 106}}; πόντου γέφυρα, πύλαι, of the Isthmus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.39, 10.27.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, π. ἀγαθῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 159; π. χρυσίου 3rd c.BC: Phoenix Iambographus 1.2; ἐκπεσεῖν εἰς τὸν ἀνομοιότητος π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 273d (cited in 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 5).}}
**II** of special seas, π. Ἰκάριος, Γρηΐκιος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.145, 23.230; ὁ Αἰγαῖος π. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.97, etc.; ὁ π. οὗτος [prev. author] 4.177 (variant)}}; Ἰόνιος, Σαρωνικός, Σικελός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 225 (Lyric poetry), “Hipp.” 1200, “Cyc.” 703: especially π. Εὔξεινος, [prev. author] “IT” 125 (Lyric poetry, nisi to be read Ἄξεινος); ὁ Εὔξεινος π. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.6, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.96, 97 (called Ἄξεινος, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 218 (Lyric poetry))}}; generally called simply ὁ Πόντος or Πόντος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 878 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.147, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 700, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 354a14, +others; but 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus has also ὁ πόντος}} for **the sea,** {{Refs|4.99, 177.}}
**II.2** the country {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Pontus” on the 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}} shore of the Black Sea, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Μιθριδάτειος” 8, etc.:}} _adjective_ Ποντικός (which see).
**III** personified as son of Gaia, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 132, 233f (Cogn. with πάτος, which see)}}

4197:
** πορ-εία**, ἡ,
(πορεύω **mode of walking** or **running, gait,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 126, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 190b, “Ti.” 45a; τὰ ὀργανικὰ μέρη τῆς π. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 432b26}}; περὶ πορείας ζῴων, title of work by Aristotle.
**II** **journey,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 823, +others; ἡ ἐκεῖσε π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 107d; ἡ κατὰ τὰ ἄγκη π. [prev. author] “Cra.” 420e; αἱ κατὰ γῆν π. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.19}}; ἡ εἰς Ἅιδου, εἰς Πέρσας π., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 115a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.5.1: _metaphorically_, π. ἕως εἰς ἄπειρον}}**processus ad infinilum,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Morte - Papiri Ercolanesi” 19.}}
**II.2** in military sense, **march,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.18; κατὰ θάλατταν τὴν π. ποιεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.11; π. ἀνύτειν [prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.6.18; ἰέναι [prev. work] 5.2.31 (nisi to be read εἶναι); ἐκ π. μάχεσθαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.198b}}; **order of march,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 11 at Title Page, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 28.1, +others}}
**II.3** generally, **course** taken by a person, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.4; ἡ[τοῦ κόσμου] π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 274a}}; of the sun, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Hymnus ad Isim” 32 (_plural_), 4th c.BC: Eudoxus Astronomus “Ars astronomica” 2.15; χρόνου π. 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 50.}}
**II.4** **travelling expenses,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 22.1.34, “PRev.Laws” 50.11 (3rd c.BC, _plural_), “PGrenf.” 1.43.8 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II.5** **visitation, inspection,** οἰκοπέδων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 83.1 (2nd-3rd c.AD), etc.}}

4198:
** πορ-εύω**,
_future_ **-σω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 447 (Lyric poetry), etc.:}}_aorist_ ἐπόρευσα, _poetry_ πόρευσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.21}}:—_passive_ and _middle_, _future_ πορεύσομαι {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 676, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 190d; πορευθήσομαι “IG” 22.141.2, LXX.1Ki.14.2:}}_aorist_ ἐπορευσάμην (only compounds ἐν-, προ-, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 313d, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.27.2); ἐπορεύθην 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 75.8, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.107, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.26, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1099 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.: _perfect_ πεπόρευμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 266d, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 53.6}}:(πόρος):
**I** _active_, **make to go, carry, convey,** by land or water, τινα {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Arion Lyricus 1.13, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.77, “P.” 11.21, etc.; ἐπ᾽ εὐστόλου νεὼς πορεύσαιμ᾽ ἂν ἐς δόμους 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 517 (Lyric poetry); ὡς τάχιστά μοι μολὼν ἄνακτα.. τις πορευσάτω [prev. author] “OC” 1476; ἐμὲ πόντιον σκάφος πορεύσει Ἄργος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1086 (Lyric poetry); ποντιὰς αὔρα,.. ποῖ με πορεύσει; [prev. author] “Hec.” 447 (Lyric poetry); βᾶσά νιν δεῦρο πόρευσον [prev. author] “Med.” 181 (Lyric poetry); στρατιὰν πεζῇ π. ὡς Βρασίδαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.132, etc.}}: with _doubleaccusative_, **carry** or **ferry over,** [Νέσσος] ποταμὸν.. Βροτοὺς μισθοῦ 'πόρευε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 560; γυναῖκ᾽ ἀρίσταν λίμναν.. πορεύσας ἐλάτᾳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 443 (Lyric poetry).}}
**I.2** of things, **bring, carry,** ἐπιστολὰς πατρί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1602}}; **furnish, bestow,** χρυσόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 985}}; **set in motion,** κίνησις.. βραδυτῆτάς τε καὶ τάχη.. π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 893d.}}
**I.3** _absolutely_, **conduct a search,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 324 (Lyric poetry, assuming variant).}}
**II** _passive_ and _middle_, **to be driven** or **carried,** μέγας βοῦς ὑπὸ σμικρᾶς μάστιγος εἰς ὁδὸν π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 1254; πρὸς βίαν π. [prev. author] “OC” 845.}}
**II.2** **go, walk, march,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.22, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 2.1, etc.; ἐφ᾽ ἑνὸς σκέλους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 190d; σύνδρομά τινι [prev. author] “Plt.” 266d; ταχέως 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.12; τοῖν ποδοῖν [prev. author] “Cyr.” 4.3.13}}; **go by land,** opposed to going by sea, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 5.3.1}}; also **cross, pass over,** διαφυλάσσειν τὰς σχεδίας, πορευθῆναι βασιλέϊ for the king's **crossing,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.107; π. δι᾽ Εὐρίπου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.29: frequently with Preps., π. ἐκ δόμων, ἔξω δωμάτων, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 392, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1156; εἰς ἀγρόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 563d; εἰς ἐκκλησίαν 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 4.1; ἐξ.. ἐς.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.35; ἐπὶ τὸν Ἀχέροντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 113d}}: c._accusative_ loci, **enter,** π.στέγας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 329, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 51}}; π. διὰ Θεσσαλίης **march** through T., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.196}}; π. παρὰ βασιλέος **come** from his presence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.95; παρὰ βασιλέως πρὸς τὸν σατράπην 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.10}}; π. πρὸς τὸν ἴδιον ἄνδρα **go in** to her husband, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Theano cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 8.43: frequently c._accusative_ _cognate_, μακροτέραν (i.e. ὁδόν) π. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.11, etc.; π.φυγάν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1239 (Lyric poetry); τὴν εἱμαρμένην πορείαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 236d: with _accusative_ loci, γῆν πολλὴν π.}}**go over, trauerse,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 6.23.1; π. τὰ δύσβατα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.27; τοσαῦτα ὄ ρη [prev. author] “An.” 2.5.18: Geometry texts, π. διὰ τοῦ κέντρου}} **pass** through the centre, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ κωνοειδέων καὶ σφαιροειδέων” 16}}; π. γραμμάν **traverse, move along** a line, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Spir.” 14.}} —Special phrases: ἐς ἄρκυν π. **fall** into.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 965}}; ἐπ᾽ ἔργον π., ἐπὶ τὰ δευτερεῖα π., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Or.” 1068, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 23b}}; π. εἰς τὰ κτήματα **enter into possession of**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 44.32}}; ἢν αἱ καθάρσιες πορεύωνται if the menses **come,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 5.60.}}
**II.3** **walk,** i.e. **live,** εἴ τις ὑπέροπτα.. π. {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 884 (Lyric poetry); frequently in LXX, as π. τοῖς νομίμοις LXX.Lev.18.3.}}
**II.4** _metaphorically_, ἡ πονηρία διὰ τῶν ἡδονῶν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.24}}; of discourse, ἐκτὸς τῶν λόγων π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 812a; διὰ τῶν ὁμολογουμένων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.6.15}}; καθ᾽ ὁμοιότητα π. **proceed** by analogy, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 31.}}
**II.5** **proceed** at law, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEleph.” 3.5, 4.6 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II.6** **go on one's way,** i.e. **die,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Epistulae” 14.}}

4199:
** πορθ-έω**,
collateral form of πέρθω (more used in Prose), **destroy, ravage, plunder,** πόλεας καὶ τείχεα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.308; ἀνδρῶν ἀγρούς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.264; τοὺς χώρους 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.58; πόλιν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 583, etc.; Φοινίκην 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.62; τὴν Σελλασίαν ἔκαον καὶ ἐπόρθουν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.27; τὴν ἤπειρον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.57; τὴν χώραν λεηλατῆσαι καὶ τὴν πόλιν πορθῆσαι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.34; π. ἐκ τῶν ἱερῶν τὰ ἀγάλματα 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 12.523b}}:—_passive_, πᾶν τὸ ἄστυ ἐπορθέετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.84; ὅλης τῆς Ἑλλάδος πεπορθημένης 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.49; τἀργύρια πορθεῖται}}**is carried off,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 155.}}
**2** in _present_ and _imperfect_, sometimes **endeavour to destroy, besiege** a town, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.162, Decrees cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.164, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.34, 15.4.}}
**3** of persons, **destroy, ruin,** θεοὺς τοὺς ἐγγενεῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 583; φίλους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 605}}: _absolutely_, **do havoc,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Andr.” 633}}: especially in _passive_, αὐτοὶ ὑφ᾽ αὑτῶν.. πορθούμεθα. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 194; κατ᾽ ἄκρας.. ὡς πορθούμεθα [prev. author] “Ch.” 691}}; of women, κόραι βία πρὸς ἀνδρῶν πορθούμεναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 565}}; σκόροδα πορθούμενος **robbed** of them, Comedy texts phrase in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 164.}}
